Use cRefactorFuncLib.pkg
Use cEncodingHelpers.pkg         // rfHtmlEscape and the encoding helpers
//Use SupplementalRefactoringClasses.pkg
// *** Refactor Function Library ***
//     The object below contains all public refactoring functions.

/*  RULES FOR USING META-TAGS:

    There are four parameters accepted by the procdure RegisterInterface that is used to
    collect info about a function when the program starts. The first one
    is a handle that cannot be used for our purposes. The second one is the function name.
    The third contains the types and names of the parameters and return type for the function.
    That leaves only one more RegisterInterface param we can use, and that is the "sComment".
    The { Description } meta-tag content is what ends up in the sComment parameter.

    As we need to handle more than four parameters, the RegisterInterface has been made in
    such a way that other meta-tags embedded within the { Description } meta-tag, are
    parsed and moved to various struct members.

    Important Note: For the { Description... meta-tag Aligned Multiline Strings are used
                    That is strings that starts and ended with a """ (triple-quote character).
                    Embedded in { Description } meta-tags, *other* meta-tags are being used
                    to describe other characterestica for a function. Those are all parts
                    that are parsed by the RegisterInterface class procedure.

    Studio Note:    The Studio (DF 25) gets very confused when tripple quotes are used with the
                    { Description } meta-tag. Both the Code Explorer and Code Completions
                    stops working. Also the Problem Resolution panel shows issues for
                    text inside the meta-tag.
                    It is therefor advised to edit and test new refactor functions in
                    another package, and then publish it here.

    The {Published} meta-tag must be set to True for a function to show up at all in function
    lists of the running program.
    Sample:
    { Published = True }

    The { Description } meta-tag is used as a compound tag, as it will contain other meta-tags.
    As described above it starts with a tripple quote, and ends with a tripple quote symbol.
    Sample:
    { Description = """
        Some text that can span
        several lines, yada-yada-yada.
        """ }

        The { MethodType } tag describes the type of function, as this is vital for the
        refactoring engine to know how a function should be called. It must be one of the members
        from this list;
            eStandardFunction    - One source line at a time will be passed for these functions.
            eRemoveFunction      - One source line at a time will be passed for these functions.
            eEditorFunction      - A source file as a string array will be passed
            eReportFunction      - A source file as a string array. Makes no source changes.
            eReportFunctionAll   - Makes no source changes.
            eOtherFunction       - A source file as a string array will be passed.
            eOtherFunctionAll    - All selected files as a string array will be passed.
        Sample:
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }

          { SummaryText } A short description text that is used by the cRefactorEngine post run report summary.
          Sample:
          { SummaryText = Split line If/Else line }

          When a refactoring function uses parameters for the "sParameter" param, possible
          values are entered in an { EnumList } meta-tag.
          If an { EnumList } meta-tag is used, it needs to be followed by two other meta-tags;
          { InitialValue } This is the default value from the EnumList, and
          { ParamHelp } This will be the help text presented to the user when hovering the mouse
                        over the grid column item. It may span several lines; ALWAYS end it with
                        its own closing brace (an unclosed tag swallows the next one).
                        Write '\n' to insert a carrige return characters to line up ParamHelp
                        text.
        Sample:
        { EnumList = "eSplitBySpaceAndSemicolon, eSplitBySemicolon, eSplitToBeginEndBlock" }
        { InitialValue = eSplitToBeginEndBlock }
        { ParamHelp = Valid Values;
        \neSplitBySpaceAndSemicolon - Split line with space and semicolon,
        \neSplitBySemicolon - Split line with semicolon,
        \neSplitToBeginEndBlock - Use a Begin/End block }
        """ }

    The { FnGroup } tag decides which group the function is listed under in the Select
    Functions picker. One of: Modernization (brings legacy code up to current DataFlex),
    Cleanup (tidies or removes - shown to the user as "Tidy Up"), Unicode64 (shown as
    "x64 + Unicode"), or omit the tag for "Other".
    Sample:
    { FnGroup = Modernization }

    The { ModRecipe } tag wires the function into the Modernization Analyzer, so a compiler
    message can be routed to it. Format: <msg#> | <match-text> | [category], where the
    match-text disambiguates functions sharing a message number (omit it when the message has
    only one function) and the category is AutoFix (default), Suggest or Explain. Repeat the
    tag for a function that fixes more than one message. After adding or changing one, bump
    CI_ModAnalyzerRecipeVersion at the top of cModAnalyzer.pkg or the recipe table will not
    reseed and the function stays dormant in the analyzer.
    Sample:
    { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: Sysdate }

    The { NoDoc } tag keeps a function OUT of the generated function help. It changes nothing
    else: the function stays published, stays in the Select Functions picker and runs exactly as
    before - only the manual skips it. Use it for a function whose behaviour is fully obvious from
    its name and { SummaryText }, or one still being proven, where a manual entry would be noise
    rather than help. A function carrying { Private = True } is skipped as well, since it is not
    part of the shipped set. Leave the tag off and the function is documented.
    Sample:
    { NoDoc = True }

    The { FnFamily } tag groups functions that do the SAME job on different keywords - the
    fourteen legacy command-to-function converters, for instance. The generated help renders a
    family as ONE table with a shared explanation, instead of a run of near-identical sections
    each repeating the same sentence. The value is free text and is matched exactly, so a typo
    shows up as a family of one in the generator's log.
    Two rules for membership: every member must sit on the SAME { MethodType } (the tables are
    rendered per page), and a function with a substantial Description of its own does NOT belong
    in a family - a table row would throw that prose away. Judge it on the Description, never on
    the name: ChangeCmdlineCommandToFunction is deliberately excluded from 'Command to function'.
    The shared paragraph lives in _FunctionFamilyIntro (cRefactorFuncLib), not in a tag - it
    describes a group, so no single function owns it.
    Sample:
    { FnFamily = Command to function }

    ==========================================================================================
    FORMATTING THE { Description = """ ... """ } BLOCK - TWO HARD COMPILER LIMITS

    Measured against df-cli 1.0.0 (DF 26 GA), 2026-08-23. Break either rule and the compiler
    dies with a SILENT access violation: no error text, no "Failed" line, exit code 0, and a
    ZERO-BYTE .err file. A build that prints "Rebuilding X ..." and then nothing at all IS
    this crash - it is not a no-op and not a cache hit.
    ==========================================================================================

    RULE 1 - INDENT EVERY LINE AT LEAST AS FAR AS THE CLOSING """

        This one has NOTHING to do with length, which is what makes it a trap. A single line
        indented less than the closing triple-quote kills the compile at any size: a five-line
        tag dies exactly like a fifty-line one. It is a dedent underflow inside the compiler.

        In this file the closing """ carries 8 spaces, so EVERY body line needs 8 or more.
            body 6 or 7, closing 8   -> CRASH        body 8, 9 or 16, closing 8 -> OK
            body 3, closing 4        -> CRASH        body 4, closing 4          -> OK
        First, middle or last line - all crash alike. A completely BLANK line is safe.

        The usual way to hit it is pasting a paragraph in: the pasted text lands flush left.
        After any paste into a { Description }, check the left edge before you build.

    RULE 2 - KEEP THE WHOLE BLOCK UNDER 2000 CHARACTERS

        Measured cliff: 1984 characters of value text compiles, 1985 crashes - about a
        2200-character { Description = ... } block as written here with 8-space indents.
        For scale: the largest block in this file (ChangeIndexNumber) is 2136 characters and
        the median is ~680, so most functions are nowhere near it.

        Every embedded sub-tag - { MethodType }, { FnGroup }, { ModRecipe }, { EnumList },
        { InitialValue }, { ParamHelp }, { SummaryText } - sits INSIDE the value and counts
        toward the total. That is how the v54 error-route tags pushed
        RefactorDbGridTocDbCJGrid over the edge.

        WHEN YOU RUN OUT OF ROOM: move examples and limitation prose up into the block comment
        comment above { Published = True }, where there is no limit. NEVER drop a sub-tag to
        make space - the tags drive the engine, the picker and the analyzer; the prose does
        not. ChangeLegacyIndicators and RefactorDbGridTocDbCJGrid are both written that way.

    RULE 3 - LEAVE ONE BLANK LINE BETWEEN FUNCTIONS

        cExportImportFunctions scans for it when walking this file (see the note further down).
*/

// Warning: Do not change the following object line as it is searched for,
//          by the cExportImportFunctions logic.
Object oRefactorFuncLib is a cRefactorFuncLib

    /* *** User defined functions are included here ***
           Use the file to add your own special refactoring function(s) that are particular to
           a special code base/project, and has no general interest.
    */
    #Include UserDefinedRefactorFunctions.pkg

    /*    *** General Purpose Refactoring Functions ***

              These are the pre-made refactoring functions
              delivered with the workspace. If there is a need
              to add private functions that is of no public
              interest, add them to thís package:
              UserDefinedRefactorFunctions.pkg

              If however, new functions are developed that could
              be of public interest, please use the TestBench's
              'JSON Export/Import facility to export new functions
              and email the JSON file to: support@rdctools.com

              There must be at least *one blank line* between each
              function. The blank lines are used by the
              cExportImportFunctions logic when scanning the source code.
    */

    /* SearchArray comparator for DefineSelectMacro' fast reject (below): returns EQ when a source line
       contains sSearchText as a bare, whole-word keyword. The cheap case-insensitive 'contains' rules out the
       many lines with no such text in one step; only those few get the boundary-aware _FindKeywordPos, so
       Selected / Selection / Select_State (substring, not whole word) do NOT match. Declared before its use.
    */
    Function _HasBareSelectKeyword String sSourceLine String sSearchText Returns Integer
        Integer iPos
        If (Lowercase(sSourceLine) contains Lowercase(sSearchText)) Begin
            Get _FindKeywordPos sSourceLine sSearchText True 1 to iPos
            If (iPos > 0) ;
                Function_Return (EQ)
        End
        Function_Return (GT)
    End_Function

    { Published = False }
    { Description = """
        Inserts a missing space between a closing parenthesis or bracket and a keyword.
        Example: ))Begin -> )) Begin  or  ]Set -> ] Set
        Only code is changed: a ')' or ']' in a string or a comment is not changed.
        The DataFlex compiler accepts the missing space, but refactoring functions fail on it, because the code cannot be split into separate words correctly.
        You do not select this function: the engine runs it on every source line before the selected functions run. That is why it is not listed in Select Functions; it is part of the engine.
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { SummaryText = Adds a missing space (blank) after a ')' or a ']', and before a keyword }
        """ }
    Function A_InsertSpaceAfterParenthesis String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        String sChar sText sCode sAt
        Integer iPos iAsc iIndent iLead iAt
        Boolean bChanged

        tTokenizer TokenizerData

        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData
        If (TokenizerData.bLineIsReady = True) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End

        /* CODE ONLY (Nils 2026-10-24). The scans below run over the raw sLine, so a bracket inside a string
           literal or the end comment counted too: a live run turned the DD help text "Whether to (C)redit or
           (D)ebit" into "(C) redit or (D) ebit" - text the user reads, changed on every run. A bracket now
           counts only where the tokenizer's masked line has it as well. sOverstrikeLine is the line without
           its end comment, trimmed the same way sIndentation was cut off, with every string's content
           overstruck - so raw position iPos is masked position iPos minus the indentation's length, and
           inside a string or past the code it is never ')' or ']'. Each space goes into the masked copy
           too, so the brackets after it stay lined up.
           Two guards keep it from doing less than before in code. When the tokenizer's scan found no string
           content to mask it stores the RAW line, untrimmed (_OverstrikeStrings), so there is no indentation to
           take off. And where the masked line has some other code character at that spot the two lines do not
           line up (an end comment the tokenizer removed from an earlier spot, "http://" + a bare "//"), so the
           bracket counts as before. Only a string's overstrike character skips it - CS_U ('^') in a "..." string,
           CS_E ('~') in a '...' one, '_' / '+' on a ';'-continued string's line - and "" (past the code). */
        Move TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine to sCode
        Move (Length(TokenizerData.sIndentation)) to iLead
        If (sCode = TokenizerData.sLine) ;
            Move 0 to iLead

        // Work on the original sLine directly, scanning for ')' followed by a letter or underscore.
        // Use a simple forward search with Pos, starting after the last match each time.
        Move False to bChanged
        Move (Pos(")", sLine)) to iPos
        While (iPos <> 0 and iPos < Length(sLine))
            Move (Mid(sLine, 1, (iPos + 1))) to sChar
            Move (Ascii(sChar)) to iAsc
            Move (iPos - iLead) to iAt
            Move "" to sAt
            If (iAt >= 1) ;
                Move (Mid(sCode, 1, iAt)) to sAt
            // A-Z = 65-90, a-z = 97-122, _ = 95 - after a ')' that is code
            If (((iAsc >= 65 and iAsc <= 90) or (iAsc >= 97 and iAsc <= 122) or (iAsc = 95)) and sAt <> "" and sAt <> CS_U and sAt <> CS_E and sAt <> "_" and sAt <> "+") Begin
                Move (Insert(CS_S, sLine, (iPos + 1))) to sLine
                Move (Insert(CS_S, sCode, (iAt + 1))) to sCode
                Move True to bChanged
            End
            // Search for next ')' after current position
            Move (Length(sLine)) to iIndent
            If ((iPos + 1) < iIndent) Begin
                Move (Mid(sLine, (iIndent - iPos), (iPos + 1))) to sText
                Move (Pos(")", sText)) to iIndent
                If (iIndent <> 0) Begin
                    Move (iPos + iIndent) to iPos
                End
                Else Begin
                    Move 0 to iPos
                End
            End
            Else Begin
                Move 0 to iPos
            End
        Loop

        // Also handle ']' (closing square bracket) followed immediately by a letter or underscore.
        // Example: [Found  ]Set -> [Found  ] Set
        Move (Pos("]", sLine)) to iPos
        While (iPos <> 0 and iPos < Length(sLine))
            Move (Mid(sLine, 1, (iPos + 1))) to sChar
            Move (Ascii(sChar)) to iAsc
            Move (iPos - iLead) to iAt
            Move "" to sAt
            If (iAt >= 1) ;
                Move (Mid(sCode, 1, iAt)) to sAt
            // A-Z = 65-90, a-z = 97-122, _ = 95 - after a ']' that is code
            If (((iAsc >= 65 and iAsc <= 90) or (iAsc >= 97 and iAsc <= 122) or (iAsc = 95)) and sAt <> "" and sAt <> CS_U and sAt <> CS_E and sAt <> "_" and sAt <> "+") Begin
                Move (Insert(CS_S, sLine, (iPos + 1))) to sLine
                Move (Insert(CS_S, sCode, (iAt + 1))) to sCode
                Move True to bChanged
            End
            // Search for next ']' after current position
            Move (Length(sLine)) to iIndent
            If ((iPos + 1) < iIndent) Begin
                Move (Mid(sLine, (iIndent - iPos), (iPos + 1))) to sText
                Move (Pos("]", sText)) to iIndent
                If (iIndent <> 0) Begin
                    Move (iPos + iIndent) to iPos
                End
                Else Begin
                    Move 0 to iPos
                End
            End
            Else Begin
                Move 0 to iPos
            End
        Loop

        Function_Return bChanged
    End_Function

    /* AddFunctionReturnsKeyword - missing 'Returns <type>' on legacy Function declarations (hard
       error 4392 on a modern compiler). The user-facing half of this comment (the bare-declaration
       example, how the type is inferred, the Returns Integer + TODO fallback and the ';'-continuation
       rule) moved into the { Description } on 2026-08-23.

       Origin: the wizard-era shells declare 'Function Verify_Exit_Application' bare - the LegacyApp3 error
       wall, Nils 2026-07-10. Error-shaped recipe: 4392's error line IS the declaration line (fix-site =
       error-site), no match-text needed - 4392 has this one route. Suggest until a real-workspace run
       verifies; then promote to AutoFix alongside the WinPrint v52 routes. */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Adds the required "Returns <type>" to a legacy Function declaration that does not have it (compile error 4392).
        Older compilers accepted a declaration without it, such as "Function Verify_Exit_Application"; a modern compiler does not, which is why an application created with an old wizard can get many 4392 errors.
        The return type is determined from the function's code: a Function_Return of a string, a number or True/False gives the type directly, and a variable name is looked up among the function's local variables. When there are several Function_Return lines, the types are combined: Integer and a decimal number give Number, and anything combined with a String gives String.
        When the type cannot be determined, the declaration gets "Returns Integer" and a TODO (DFRefactor) comment for you to review. That always compiles, because any return type compiles where a declaration without one fails, and the runtime converts the value for the caller.
        A declaration that continues on the next line with ';' is read as a whole. If it already has Returns on the next line, it is not changed; otherwise the Returns is added to the last line.
        { MethodType = eOtherFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4392 | AutoFix }
        { SummaryText = Added: Returns <type> to legacy Function declarations }
        """ }
    Function AddFunctionReturnsKeyword String[] ByRef asCode String sParameter Returns Integer
        Integer iRetval
        Get _TransformFunctionReturns (&asCode) to iRetval
        Function_Return iRetval
    End_Function

    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Adds the default '.pkg' file extension to a Use statement that does not have one.
        Example: Use cMyClass -> Use cMyClass.pkg
        Use statements that already have an extension (.pkg, .vw ...) or a path in quotes are not changed, and neither is 'Use UI', the base package of character-mode DataFlex.
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4535 | file extension }
        { SummaryText = Added '.pkg' extension to a Use statement }
        """ }
    Function AddUseFileExtension String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        Boolean bIsUse
        String sLC sFileName
        Integer iUsePos iStart iEnd
        tTokenizer TokenizerData

        // Not in a DF 23 multi-line string, whose "Use x" line is text (2026-10-24).
        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData
        If (TokenizerData.bIsInDF23MultiLineString = True) ;
            Function_Return False
        Get _IsUseLine sLine to bIsUse
        If (bIsUse = False) ;
            Function_Return False
        // Locate the filename token - the word right after "Use ".
        Move (Lowercase(sLine)) to sLC
        Move (Pos("use ", sLC)) to iUsePos
        Move (iUsePos + 4) to iStart
        While (Mid(sLine, 1, iStart) = " ")
            Increment iStart
        Loop
        Move iStart to iEnd
        While (iEnd <= Length(sLine) and Mid(sLine, 1, iEnd) <> " ")
            Increment iEnd
        Loop
        Move (Mid(sLine, (iEnd - iStart), iStart)) to sFileName
        // Skip if there is no filename, it already carries an extension, or it is a QUOTED path.
        // A quoted Use target (e.g. Use "Acme Electrical\DG\X.dg") is a literal path the developer
        // wrote deliberately - often with spaces and its own extension - so the bare-name ".pkg" auto-add
        // does not apply. Without this guard the space-split grabbed only the first token (`"Acme`),
        // saw no ".", and produced Use "Acme.pkg Electrical\DG\X.dg".
        If ((sFileName = "") or (sFileName contains ".") or (Left(sFileName, 1) = '"') or (Left(sFileName, 1) = "'")) ;
            Function_Return False
        // Nor 'Use UI', the DataFlex character-mode base (Nils 2026-09-23: "I don't think there ever was an
        // UI.pkg" - a Check had turned 'use ui' into 'use ui.pkg'). DataFlex 26 has that base under no name,
        // so the extension gains nothing, and the line stays what the character-mode compiler was given.
        // The Deep Scan rule (eDSUseNoExt) mirrors this, or the board would offer a fix this refuses.
        If (Lowercase(sFileName) = "ui") ;
            Function_Return False
        // Insert ".pkg" immediately after the filename; indent + trailing comment are preserved.
        Move (Left(sLine, (iEnd - 1)) + ".pkg" + Mid(sLine, CI_EOL, iEnd)) to sLine
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    /* AlignCollidingDeclarationType - makes every declaration of one name agree on ONE type. The second
       of the two DF 25/26 error-4544 fixes. The user-facing half of this comment (which of the two fixes
       to pick, the never-run-unasked rule and the missing-parameter skip) moved into the { Description }
       on 2026-08-23.

       The evidence: the pbSilent case (Nils, 2026-07-22) - Boolean in WordTemplateCreator and
       KeyFieldValidation, String in ChilKat, 151 errors, and one word between them and a clean build.

       Rewrites DECLARATION LINES ONLY - "Property <type> <name>" and "Function <name> ... Returns <type>".
       No call site is touched, which is what makes this the small change it looks like. */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Changes a name that is declared with more than one type so that all its declarations have the same type. This fixes error 4544 in DataFlex 25 and 26 for a name that is declared only in the workspace, where renaming would not help.
        The function's parameter is 'Name=Type', for example 'pbSilent=Boolean'.
        Only declaration lines are changed (Property <type> <name>, Function <name> ... Returns <type>); no calls are changed. Changing a declared type can change what the code does, so read the report first.
        Which 4544 fix to choose: rename the name when DataFlex itself declares it with a different type, because that declaration cannot be changed. Use this function when the declarations that differ are all in the workspace; renaming all of them would only move the same problem to the new name. A typical case: pbSilent is declared as Boolean in two packages and as String in a third, and changing one word makes them the same.
        It never runs unless you choose it. Only you know whether some code assigns a value that the new type cannot hold, so the guided flow shows the file, the line and both types before it runs, and the run summary lists every line it changed.
        When it is selected without a parameter, or with a parameter that is not Name=Type, it changes nothing and says so in the run notes.
        A declaration marked Overloaded is not changed, and the run notes say how many there were. Overloaded versions are meant to return different types, because DataFlex chooses between them by the number of parameters, so they cause no 4544 error.
        { MethodType = eOtherFunctionAll }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Aligned a colliding declaration to one type (DF25 error 4544) }
        """ }
    Function AlignCollidingDeclarationType String[] ByRef asFiles String sParameter Returns Integer
        String[] asCode
        String sName sType sParam sFile
        Integer iPos iFile iFileLast iChanged iTotal iOvlSkips
        Boolean bPanel bCancel bIsUtf8 bOK

        Move (Trim(sParameter)) to sParam
        Move (Pos("=", sParam)) to iPos
        If (iPos < 2) Begin
            /* A MISSING PARAMETER IS A CONFIGURATION STATE, NOT A PROGRAM FAULT (Nils
               2026-08-03). This used to `Error DFErr_Program`, which raises a RUNTIME error:
               a modal mid-run in the UI, and in a headless run an entry in Error_Count that
               reads as an engine failure. It fires whenever a parameterised function is
               selected WITHOUT its parameter - which a Select All does by definition, and
               which is how it surfaced in the first /bm run over Order Entry.

               Logged and skipped instead: the user still learns why nothing happened (the
               note lands in the Refactor Engine Error log that the run summary points at),
               the run is not interrupted, and a genuine engine error still means a genuine
               engine error. Real failures - a write that did not land - are still raised.
               Guarded on ghoStatusLog: the DFUnit runner builds no engine, so no log. */
            If (ghoStatusLog <> 0) ;
                Send AddRunNote "AlignCollidingDeclarationType" "SKIPPED - needs a parameter of the form Name=Type, for example pbSilent=Boolean."
            Function_Return 0
        End
        Move (Trim(Left(sParam, (iPos - 1))))               to sName
        Move (Trim(Right(sParam, (Length(sParam) - iPos)))) to sType
        If (sName = "" or sType = "") Begin
            // Configuration state, not a fault - logged and skipped. See the note above.
            If (ghoStatusLog <> 0) ;
                Send AddRunNote "AlignCollidingDeclarationType" "SKIPPED - both a name and a type are required, as in pbSilent=Boolean."
            Function_Return 0
        End

        Move (SizeOfArray(asFiles) - 1) to iFileLast
        Move (ghoStatusPanel <> 0 and Active_State(ghoStatusPanel) = True) to bPanel
        If (bPanel = True) Begin
            // Overall bar = the file list; per-file bar = the LINE walk inside the current file
            // (scaled + reset per file below, stepped by _AlignDeclTypeInLines). It used to be
            // initialised here and never advanced (Nils 2026-07-25, the same dead initialisation
            // as ChangeCollidingMethodName - a still bar reads as a stopped clock).
            Set piMaximumTot of ghoStatusPanel to (iFileLast + 1)
            Set piPosition   of ghoProgressBar to 0
            Set piAdvanceBy  of ghoProgressBar to 1
        End
        Move 0 to iTotal
        Move False to bCancel
        For iFile from 0 to iFileLast
            If (bCancel = False) Begin
                Move asFiles[iFile] to sFile
                If (bPanel = True) Begin
                    Set Caption_Text   of ghoStatusPanel to ("File No:" * String(iFile + 1) * "of:" * String(iFileLast + 1))
                    Set Title_Text     of ghoStatusPanel to "Aligning declarations:"
                    Set TableName_Text of ghoStatusPanel to sFile
                    Send DoAdvanceTot of ghoStatusPanel
                End
                Move (rfFileHasBOM(sFile)) to bIsUtf8
                Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray(sFile)) to asCode
                If (bPanel = True) Begin
                    Set piMaximum  of ghoStatusPanel to (SizeOfArray(asCode))
                    Set piPosition of ghoProgressBar to 0
                End
                Get _AlignDeclTypeInLines of Self (&asCode) sName sType to iChanged
                If (iChanged > 0) Begin
                    // Through the write-time macro guard: macro code is never refactored (see _GuardedWriteEncodedFile).
                    Get _GuardedWriteEncodedFile sFile (&asCode) bIsUtf8 to bOK
                    If (bOK = False) ;
                        Error DFErr_Program ("Write failed:" * sFile * "(AlignCollidingDeclarationType)")
                    Move (iTotal + iChanged) to iTotal
                End
                // Mid-run cancel: poll the safe accessor (NEVER Cancel_Check - it consumes the signal).
                Get pbUserCancelRequested of ghoEngine to bCancel
            End
        Loop
        If (bPanel = True) ;
            Set Title_Text of ghoStatusPanel to ""
        Set piAlignedDecls to iTotal
        // Name what was left alone: a count of aligned declarations on its own reads as "that is
        // all of them" when some were deliberately skipped.
        Get piOverloadSkips to iOvlSkips
        If (iOvlSkips > 0) ;
            Send AddRunNote "AlignCollidingDeclarationType" ("Left " + String(iOvlSkips) + " Overloaded declaration(s) of " + sName + " unchanged. DataFlex resolves an overloaded name by parameter count, so its variants are MEANT to return different types - retyping them would change what they return, and the name cannot raise error 4544 anyway.")
        Function_Return iTotal
    End_Function

    /* ChangeAmbiguousCallToGet - clears error 4544 ("Function GET_<name> with ambiguous return type used in
       expression") by HOISTING the expression-style call into a Get statement, without touching a single
       declaration. What 4544 is, the before/after, the side-effect caution and what is left untouched are
       now in the { Description } below.

       Both halves are verified against DF25 on a minimal two-class fixture: the expression form errors 4544,
       the hoisted Get compiles clean. Local declarations mid-method and inside a Begin/End block are both
       legal (also verified), so the temp is declared right at the hoist site - no declaration-block tracking.

       This is the CALL-SITE route. The other route is to change the few conflicting DECLARATIONS (rename or
       type-align), which is a far smaller diff but a design decision only the code's owners can make - and
       impossible in code you merely have read access to. Neither is "the" answer, so this is Suggest and
       the report names the declarations for whoever can decide. The caution also rides in the emitted marker.

       Conservative by construction: comment lines, continuation lines (the one above ends with ";"),
       unbalanced parentheses and object-less calls are all left untouched rather than half-converted, and
       nested calls hoist innermost-first. The pure core is cRefactorFuncLib._HoistAmbiguousCallsInLines.
    */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Fixes error 4544 by changing calls in expressions of a method with an ambiguous return type into Get statements, without changing any declarations. Enter the method name or names as the function's parameter.
        Error 4544 is reported when one message name is declared with different return types in the workspace, for example a container class and a DEO that both declare Function Value. The compiler cannot determine the type of a call in an expression, and reports every one of them. A few declarations can cause hundreds of errors, in files that have nothing to do with those classes.
        Example: If (Value(oFoo,"k") = 1) becomes three lines, with a Variant variable declared where the call was:
        Variant vDfrGet1
        Get Value of (oFoo) ("k") to vDfrGet1
        If (vDfrGet1 = 1)
        Caution: the call is now made before the rest of the expression is evaluated, so for a call with side effects, this changes what the code does. The other way to fix the error is to rename the few conflicting declarations, or give them the same type. That is a much smaller change, but a design decision that only the code's owners can make, which is why this function is a suggestion.
        Comment lines, lines that continue a statement, calls without an object, and lines with unbalanced parentheses are not changed. Nested calls are changed from the innermost one outwards.
        { MethodType = eOtherFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ParamHelp = Method name(s) to convert, separated by ; or , - e.g. Value;pbSilent. Read them off the compiler's 4544 messages (GET_VALUE means Value). Object methods only. Blank does nothing. }
        { SummaryText = Converted ambiguous function calls to Get statements }
        """ }
    Function ChangeAmbiguousCallToGet String[] ByRef asCode String sParameter Returns Integer
        String[] asNames
        String sParam sOne sThisFile
        Integer iCount iN iP

        Move (Trim(sParameter)) to sParam
        // No names = nothing to do. Deliberately NOT auto-derived yet: deriving the ambiguous set needs a
        // workspace-wide declaration harvest, and caching that across a per-file run is exactly the kind of
        // lingering state that caused trouble before. The compiler's 4544 messages name them outright.
        If (sParam = "") Begin
            // Configuration state, not a fault - logged and skipped, same policy as the collision
            // converters (Nils 2026-08-03). Was a SILENT return: the guided Check always passes the
            // names it read off the 4544s, so a manual tick was the one path that got nothing back
            // and no reason why.
            If (ghoStatusLog <> 0) ;
                Send AddRunNote "ChangeAmbiguousCallToGet" "SKIPPED - needs the ambiguous function name(s) as a parameter, separated by ; or , - for example Value;Split. A guided Check fills these in from the compiler's 4544 messages."
            Function_Return 0
        End
        Move (Replaces(",", sParam, ";")) to sParam
        Move 0 to iN
        While (sParam <> "")
            Move (Pos(";", sParam)) to iP
            If (iP = 0) Begin
                Move (Trim(sParam)) to sOne
                Move "" to sParam
            End
            Else Begin
                Move (Trim(Left(sParam, (iP - 1)))) to sOne
                Move (Trim(Mid(sParam, (Length(sParam) - iP), (iP + 1)))) to sParam
            End
            If (sOne <> "") Begin
                Move sOne to asNames[iN]
                Increment iN
            End
        Loop
        If (iN = 0) ;
            Function_Return 0
        // The core records WHERE it declines; give it the file to record against. Read from the
        // engine because a whole-file function is handed only the lines, not the path.
        Get psCurrentSourceFileName of ghoEngine to sThisFile
        Set psHoistCurrentFile to sThisFile
        Get _HoistAmbiguousCallsInLines (&asCode) asNames to iCount
        Function_Return iCount
    End_Function

    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Converts legacy Begin_Constraints / End_Constraints blocks into a modern OnConstrain procedure.
        Everything in the block (declarations, Get, If and Else lines with the Constrain lines they control, and any Report_* lines) is moved, unchanged and in the same order, into 'Procedure OnConstrain', which starts with 'Forward Send OnConstrain'. Nothing is reordered, because, for example, a Report_Index parameter can depend on code earlier in the block.
        Comments are kept, and blank lines are removed. A block without a matching End_Constraints is not changed. An End_Constraints without a Begin_Constraints, where the start has already been changed to Procedure OnConstrain, is changed to End_Procedure.
        { MethodType = eOtherFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: Begin_Constraint }
        { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: End_Constraint }
        { SummaryText = Refactored: Begin_Constraints -> OnConstrain }
        """ }
    Function ChangeBeginEndConstraint String[] ByRef asCode String sParameter Returns Integer
        String[] asOut asBody asBlockRaw asEmpty
        Integer iCount iSize iBlocks i iMax iPos
        String sLine sTrim sFirst sLow sIndent
        Boolean bInBlock

        Move 0 to iBlocks
        Move False to bInBlock
        Move (SizeOfArray(asCode)) to iSize
        Decrement iSize
        For iCount from 0 to iSize
            Move asCode[iCount] to sLine
            Move (Trim(sLine)) to sTrim
            // First whitespace-delimited token of the trimmed line.
            Move (Pos(" ", sTrim)) to iPos
            If (iPos > 0) Begin
                Move (Trim(Left(sTrim, (iPos - 1)))) to sFirst
            End
            Else Begin
                Move sTrim to sFirst
            End
            Move (Lowercase(sFirst)) to sLow

            If (bInBlock = False) Begin
                If ((sLow = "begin_constraints") or (sLow = "begin_constraint")) Begin
                    // Open a block: remember the Begin_Constraints indentation, reset the buffers.
                    Move True to bInBlock
                    Move (Pos(sTrim, sLine)) to iPos
                    If (iPos > 1) Begin
                        Move (Left(sLine, (iPos - 1))) to sIndent
                    End
                    Else Begin
                        Move "" to sIndent
                    End
                    Move asEmpty to asBody
                    Move asEmpty to asBlockRaw
                    Move sLine to asBlockRaw[0]
                    Increment iBlocks
                End
                Else If ((sLow = "end_constraints") or (sLow = "end_constraint")) Begin
                    // A LONE End_Constraints with no matching Begin_Constraints: the opening was already
                    // hand-migrated to 'Procedure OnConstrain(s)' but the closing is still the obsolete
                    // macro - the 4533 the compiler flags ("Obsolete command: End_Constraint. Use
                    // OnConstrain"). End_Constraints IS the End_Procedure macro, so expand it in place,
                    // preserving indentation, to clear the warning. Context-free and always valid; the
                    // (compiler-accepted) 'Procedure OnConstrain(s)' opening is deliberately left as-is.
                    Move (Pos(sTrim, sLine)) to iPos
                    If (iPos > 1) Begin
                        Move (Left(sLine, (iPos - 1))) to sIndent
                    End
                    Else Begin
                        Move "" to sIndent
                    End
                    Move (sIndent + "End_Procedure") to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                    Increment iBlocks
                End
                Else Begin
                    // Normalize a NON-standard plural 'Procedure OnConstraints' declaration to DF26's actual
                    // DD hook name 'OnConstrain' (singular): the plural overrides NOTHING (confirmed against
                    // Data_set.pkg - no 'OnConstraints' exists in the framework), so its Constrains would never
                    // fire. Completes the half-migrated 'Procedure OnConstraints ... End_Constraints' block
                    // whose End the branch above already fixed. Whole-token, indentation preserved. (Nils
                    // confirmed 2026-07-07.)
                    If (Left(Lowercase(sTrim), 23) = "procedure onconstraints" and (Length(sTrim) = 23 or Mid(sTrim, 1, 24) = " ")) Begin
                        Move (Pos("onconstraints", Lowercase(sLine))) to iPos
                        Move (Left(sLine, (iPos - 1)) + "OnConstrain" + Mid(sLine, Length(sLine), (iPos + 13))) to sLine
                    End
                    Move sLine to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                End
            End
            Else Begin
                Move sLine to asBlockRaw[SizeOfArray(asBlockRaw)]
                If ((sLow = "end_constraints") or (sLow = "end_constraint")) Begin
                    // The ENTIRE block body moves verbatim and IN ORDER into OnConstrain - conditional
                    // constraints (If/Else around Constrain), declarations, AND any Report_* lines stay
                    // exactly where they were. A Report_Index parameter can be set by logic earlier in
                    // the block, so it must NOT be moved out.
                    Move (sIndent + "Procedure OnConstrain") to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                    Move (sIndent + "    Forward Send OnConstrain") to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                    Move (SizeOfArray(asBody) - 1) to iMax
                    For i from 0 to iMax
                        Move asBody[i] to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                    Loop
                    Move (sIndent + "End_Procedure") to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                    Move False to bInBlock
                End
                Else If (sTrim <> "") Begin
                    Move sLine to asBody[SizeOfArray(asBody)]
                End
            End
        Loop

        // Unterminated block (no End_Constraints): restore its raw lines unchanged so nothing breaks.
        If (bInBlock = True) Begin
            Move (SizeOfArray(asBlockRaw) - 1) to iMax
            For i from 0 to iMax
                Move asBlockRaw[i] to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
            Loop
            Move (iBlocks - 1) to iBlocks
        End

        Move asOut to asCode
        Function_Return iBlocks
    End_Function

    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces the legacy Calc, Calculate, MoveInt, MoveNum, MoveReal, MoveStr and MoveDate commands with the Move command.
        The legacy commands still compile, but they do not work with the Studio's statement completion.
        Example: Calc (iA + iB) to iC -> Move (iA + iB) to iC, and MoveInt iVal to iCount -> Move iVal to iCount
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: Calc }
        { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: MoveInt }
        { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: MoveNum }
        { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: MoveReal }
        { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: MoveStr }
        { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: MoveDate }
        { SummaryText = Changed: Calc/MoveInt/MoveNum/MoveReal/MoveStr/MoveDate -> Move }
        """ }
    Function ChangeCalcToMoveStatement String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        Boolean bChanged bFound
        tTokenizer TokenizerData

        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData
        If (LineIsNotCode(&TokenizerData)) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End

        // Check if line contains one of the keywords and that none of the keywords are within quotes.
        Get IsKeywordInLine "calc|calculate|moveint|movenum|movedate|movereal|movestr" TokenizerData.asLineTokens to bFound
        If (bFound = False) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End

        Move False to bChanged
        // 'calculate' is the long spelling of 'calc' (both -> Move; docs list "Calc, Calculate
        // Command" as one obsolete entry). Whole-word/boundary-aware substitution, so it is tried
        // independently of 'calc' - neither can match the other (Nils 2026-07-11: a customer report's
        // 'calculate (...) to ...' lines flagged by Deep Scan but never converted). Length 9.
        If (bChanged = False) Get _SubstituteCalcKeyword (&sLine) "calculate" 9 to bChanged
        If (bChanged = False) Get _SubstituteCalcKeyword (&sLine) "calc"     4 to bChanged
        If (bChanged = False) Get _SubstituteCalcKeyword (&sLine) "moveint"  7 to bChanged
        If (bChanged = False) Get _SubstituteCalcKeyword (&sLine) "movenum"  7 to bChanged
        If (bChanged = False) Get _SubstituteCalcKeyword (&sLine) "movedate" 8 to bChanged
        If (bChanged = False) Get _SubstituteCalcKeyword (&sLine) "movereal" 8 to bChanged
        If (bChanged = False and TokenizerData.bIsMoveStatement = False) Begin
            Get _SubstituteCalcKeyword (&sLine) "movestr" 7 to bChanged
        End

        Function_Return bChanged
    End_Function

    /* Call <procedure> [value ...] invoked a user-defined procedure (a procedure...end_procedure block),
       exactly what Send <procedure> [value ...] does today - the docs say "Call is replaced by the Send
       command". A leading-command keyword swap, like System -> Abort; the procedure name + any value
       arguments ride through intact. (Call targets a real PROCEDURE, not a Gosub label, so this is a clean
       1:1 - distinct from the Gosub->Procedure work.) */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces the obsolete 'Call' command, which calls a procedure, with the modern 'Send' command.
        Example: Call MyProc value1 value2  -->  Send MyProc value1 value2
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: Call }
        { SummaryText = Call command -> Send }
        """ }
    Function ChangeCallToSend String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        Boolean bChanged
        tTokenizer TokenizerData
        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData
        Get _SwapLeadingCommandKeyword (&sLine) TokenizerData "Call" "Send" to bChanged
        Function_Return bChanged
    End_Function

    /* Chain <program> ran another program and TERMINATED the current one; the modern form is
       Runprogram Wait <program> (run it and wait for it). A leading-command keyword swap, exactly like
       System -> Abort - the rest of the line (the program name + any arguments) rides through intact.
       Chain's own syntax was 'Chain [Wait] {command-line} [Export_Files | Export_Only] [Seq_Export]':
       when the source already says 'Chain Wait ...', only the command word is swapped, or the line
       came out 'Runprogram Wait Wait ...' (a 2005-era workspace, Nils 2026-09-19). */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces the obsolete 'Chain' command with 'Runprogram Wait'.
        Example: Chain "report.exe"  -->  Runprogram Wait "report.exe"
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: Chain }
        { SummaryText = Chain command -> Runprogram Wait }
        """ }
    Function ChangeChainToRunprogram String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        Boolean bChanged bHasWait
        tTokenizer TokenizerData
        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData
        // 'Chain Wait <prog>' already carries the Wait - swap the command word alone.
        Move (TokenizerData.iTokenCount > 1 and Lowercase(TokenizerData.asLineTokens[1]) = "wait") to bHasWait
        If (bHasWait = True) ;
            Get _SwapLeadingCommandKeyword (&sLine) TokenizerData "Chain" "Runprogram" to bChanged
        Else ;
            Get _SwapLeadingCommandKeyword (&sLine) TokenizerData "Chain" "Runprogram Wait" to bChanged
        Function_Return bChanged
    End_Function

    /* ChangeCollidingMethodName - the DF 25/26 error 4544 fix applied AT THE DECLARATION rather than at
       the call sites. The user-facing half of this comment moved into the { Description } on 2026-08-23,
       where the generated help can reach it; what is left is the rationale.

       Built because the alternative was to tell the user to do it by hand (Nils, 2026-07-22: "Aren't we
       building a tool to help the user to fix anomalies in their code prior moving to a later DF
       version?"). A mechanical, scoped rename is exactly what a refactoring tool is for.

       Scope is worked out in three passes over the selected files, because none of it can be known from
       one file alone:
         1. which CLASSES declare the name, and the whole class family beneath them - a subclass that
            overrides it (cSummaryTable over cHashTable) must be renamed too, or the workspace is left
            half-renamed and still ambiguous;
         2. which identifiers hold an object of that family - Object declarations are workspace-wide
            (Register_Object) so they carry across files, while a local handle from Get Create stays
            local to its own file, since an "hTbl" here has nothing to do with an "hTbl" there;
         3. the rewrite itself, renaming only what step 2 proved.

       Anything whose receiver cannot be typed from the source is LEFT ALONE and listed on the Manual
       Changes tab. Guessing would break somebody's build - and this runs over library code. */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Renames a method that is declared with more than one return type. This fixes error 4544 in DataFlex 25 and 26 at the declaration, which changing the calls alone cannot fully do.
        The function's parameter is 'OldName=NewName', for example 'Value=ValueByKey'.
        The declaration is renamed in the class and all its subclasses, and so is every call where the object can be shown to belong to one of them. Calls where this cannot be shown are listed on the Manual Changes tab instead of being guessed.
        Renaming the declaration fixes every call of the name, including calls that continue on the next line, calls inside a macro (reported where the macro is used), and calls in DataFlex's own packages, which are never changed. In one test workspace, 3 declarations were behind about 400 calls.
        A subclass that overrides the method is renamed too; otherwise the workspace would be only partly renamed and still ambiguous. Object declarations are recognised in the whole workspace (Register_Object), while a local handle from Get Create is only followed within its own file.
        A declaration outside the workspace's home folder is renamed and also reported, because a shared library can be used by other workspaces as well, which still compile with the old name.
        Nothing is guessed: this also runs on library code, so a call whose object type cannot be determined from the source code is reported, never changed.
        A name declared as Overloaded is not changed: DataFlex chooses the version by the number of parameters, so several return types are valid and no 4544 error occurs.
        { MethodType = eOtherFunctionAll }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Renamed a colliding method declaration and its provable call sites }
        """ }
    Function ChangeCollidingMethodName String[] ByRef asFiles String sParameter Returns Integer
        String[] asCode asClassPairs asFilePairs asSeeds asFileSeeds asFamily asObjRecv asFileObj
        String[] asRecv asLocalRecv asShared asEmptyStr asHandRows asSiteRows
        String sOld sNew sParam sFile sHome sLc sWhatDecl sWhatUse sWhyHand
        Integer i iLast iFile iFileLast iPos iChanged iTotal iDecls iCalls
        Boolean bPanel bCancel bIsUtf8 bOK bInPlay bOvlFound

        Move (Trim(sParameter)) to sParam
        Move (Pos("=", sParam)) to iPos
        // NO GUESSING what to rename to. A blank or malformed parameter is a question for the user, not
        // an invitation to invent an identifier and rewrite a library with it.
        If (iPos < 2) Begin
            // Configuration state, not a fault - logged and skipped. See the note above.
            If (ghoStatusLog <> 0) ;
                Send AddRunNote "ChangeCollidingMethodName" "SKIPPED - needs a parameter of the form OldName=NewName, for example Value=ValueByKey."
            Function_Return 0
        End
        Move (Trim(Left(sParam, (iPos - 1))))                    to sOld
        Move (Trim(Right(sParam, (Length(sParam) - iPos))))      to sNew
        If (sOld = "" or sNew = "" or Lowercase(sOld) = Lowercase(sNew)) Begin
            // Configuration state, not a fault - logged and skipped. See the note above.
            If (ghoStatusLog <> 0) ;
                Send AddRunNote "ChangeCollidingMethodName" "SKIPPED - the old and new names must both be given, and must differ."
            Function_Return 0
        End

        Get psHomeTarget of ghoApplication to sHome
        Move (Lowercase(vFolderFormat(sHome))) to sHome
        Move (SizeOfArray(asFiles) - 1) to iFileLast
        Move (ghoStatusPanel <> 0 and Active_State(ghoStatusPanel) = True) to bPanel
        If (bPanel = True) Begin
            // OVERALL bar = the whole job (3 passes x the file list). The PER-FILE bar shows the
            // LINE walk inside the current file: its maximum is set per file below and the
            // line-walking helpers step it (_NudgeLineProgress, every ~20 lines). It used to be
            // initialised here and then never advanced at all, so it sat empty for the whole run -
            // and a still bar reads as a stopped CLOCK, since the Elapsed Time only repaints when
            // a panel setter fires and this function's finest update was one FILE (Nils 2026-07-25).
            Set piMaximumTot of ghoStatusPanel to (3 * (iFileLast + 1))
            Set piPosition   of ghoProgressBar to 0
            Set piAdvanceBy  of ghoProgressBar to 1
        End
        Move False to bCancel

        // ---- Pass 1: the class family that declares the name ----
        For iFile from 0 to iFileLast
            Move asFiles[iFile] to sFile
            If (bPanel = True) Begin
                Set Caption_Text   of ghoStatusPanel to ("Classes - File No:" * String(iFile + 1) * "of:" * String(iFileLast + 1))
                Set Title_Text     of ghoStatusPanel to "Finding the classes that declare it:"
                Set TableName_Text of ghoStatusPanel to sFile
                Send DoAdvanceTot of ghoStatusPanel
            End
            Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray(sFile)) to asCode
            // Scale the per-file bar to THIS file's lines; the helpers below step it as they walk.
            If (bPanel = True) Begin
                Set piMaximum  of ghoStatusPanel to (SizeOfArray(asCode))
                Set piPosition of ghoProgressBar to 0
            End
            Get _ClassPairsFromLines of Self asCode to asFilePairs
            For i from 0 to (SizeOfArray(asFilePairs) - 1)
                Move asFilePairs[i] to asClassPairs[SizeOfArray(asClassPairs)]
            Loop
            // An OVERLOADED declaration of the name stops the whole run, before anything is written.
            // A rename has to be all-or-nothing here: rename some declarations of an overload set and
            // not the rest and you leave an overloaded and a non-overloaded method sharing a name -
            // DataFlex error 4390. And there is nothing to fix anyway: an overloaded name resolves by
            // parameter count, so it cannot raise the 4544 this function exists to clear.
            If (bOvlFound = False) ;
                Get _NameIsOverloadedInLines of Self asCode sOld to bOvlFound
            Get _ClassesDeclaringName of Self asCode sOld to asFileSeeds
            For i from 0 to (SizeOfArray(asFileSeeds) - 1)
                If (SearchArray(asFileSeeds[i], asSeeds, Desktop, RefFunc(DFSTRICMP)) = -1) Begin
                    Move asFileSeeds[i] to asSeeds[SizeOfArray(asSeeds)]
                    // A declaration outside the workspace home lives in a shared library tree - renamed,
                    // but the user has to know that other applications compile against the old name.
                    Move (Lowercase(sFile)) to sLc
                    If (sHome <> "" and Pos(sHome, sLc) <> 1) Begin
                        If (SearchArray(sFile, asShared, Desktop, RefFunc(DFSTRICMP)) = -1) ;
                            Move sFile to asShared[SizeOfArray(asShared)]
                    End
                End
            Loop
            Get pbUserCancelRequested of ghoEngine to bCancel
            If (bCancel = True) ;
                Move iFileLast to iFile
        Loop
        If (bOvlFound = True) Begin
            Send AddRunNote "ChangeCollidingMethodName" ("SKIPPED - " + sOld + " is declared Overloaded. DataFlex resolves an overloaded name by parameter count, so it holds several return types legally and cannot raise error 4544 - there is nothing here to rename. Renaming part of an overload set would raise error 4390 instead. Nothing was changed.")
            Function_Return 0
        End
        If (SizeOfArray(asSeeds) = 0 or bCancel = True) Begin
            // Distinguish "no class declares this" from "nothing to do" - see pbRenameNoClassSeeds.
            Set pbRenameNoClassSeeds to (SizeOfArray(asSeeds) = 0)
            Set pasRenameSharedFiles to asShared
            // CANNOT rename it - so hand back the whole job instead of a shrug. The declarations AND every
            // use of the name, because a rename is both and the uses are the half nobody can find by eye.
            If (SizeOfArray(asSeeds) = 0 and bCancel = False) Begin
                Move ("Rename this " + sOld + " declaration - the name belongs to DataFlex") to sWhatDecl
                Move ("Rename this use of " + sOld + " to match its renamed declaration")     to sWhatUse
                Move ("DataFlex owns the name " + sOld + ", so the workspace's declaration has to change - and every call that means THAT declaration has to change with it. Which declaration a call means cannot be told from the source, so none of this was guessed at.") to sWhyHand
                Get pasRenameUnproven to asHandRows
                For iFile from 0 to iFileLast
                    Move asFiles[iFile] to sFile
                    If (bPanel = True) Begin
                        Set Caption_Text   of ghoStatusPanel to ("Listing sites - File No:" * String(iFile + 1) * "of:" * String(iFileLast + 1))
                        Set Title_Text     of ghoStatusPanel to "Finding every place it is used:"
                        Set TableName_Text of ghoStatusPanel to sFile
                        Send DoAdvanceTot of ghoStatusPanel
                    End
                    Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray(sFile)) to asCode
                    If (bPanel = True) Begin
                        Set piMaximum  of ghoStatusPanel to (SizeOfArray(asCode))
                        Set piPosition of ghoProgressBar to 0
                    End
                    Get _NameSitesInLines of Self asCode sOld sFile sWhatDecl sWhatUse sWhyHand to asSiteRows
                    For i from 0 to (SizeOfArray(asSiteRows) - 1)
                        Move asSiteRows[i] to asHandRows[SizeOfArray(asHandRows)]
                    Loop
                    Get pbUserCancelRequested of ghoEngine to bCancel
                    If (bCancel = True) ;
                        Move iFileLast to iFile
                Loop
                Set pasRenameUnproven to asHandRows
                If (bPanel = True) ;
                    Set Title_Text of ghoStatusPanel to ""
            End
            Function_Return 0
        End
        Get _ExpandClassFamily of Self asClassPairs asSeeds to asFamily

        // ---- Pass 2: the OBJECT names of that family (workspace-wide, so gathered across all files) ----
        For iFile from 0 to iFileLast
            Move asFiles[iFile] to sFile
            If (bPanel = True) Begin
                Set Caption_Text   of ghoStatusPanel to ("Receivers - File No:" * String(iFile + 1) * "of:" * String(iFileLast + 1))
                Set Title_Text     of ghoStatusPanel to "Finding the objects it is called on:"
                Set TableName_Text of ghoStatusPanel to sFile
                Send DoAdvanceTot of ghoStatusPanel
            End
            Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray(sFile)) to asCode
            If (bPanel = True) Begin
                Set piMaximum  of ghoStatusPanel to (SizeOfArray(asCode))
                Set piPosition of ghoProgressBar to 0
            End
            Get _ReceiverNamesFromLines of Self asCode asFamily True to asFileObj
            For i from 0 to (SizeOfArray(asFileObj) - 1)
                If (SearchArray(asFileObj[i], asObjRecv, Desktop, RefFunc(DFSTRICMP)) = -1) ;
                    Move asFileObj[i] to asObjRecv[SizeOfArray(asObjRecv)]
            Loop
            Get pbUserCancelRequested of ghoEngine to bCancel
            If (bCancel = True) ;
                Move iFileLast to iFile
        Loop

        // ---- Pass 3: rewrite ----
        Move 0 to iTotal
        Move 0 to iDecls
        Move 0 to iCalls
        For iFile from 0 to iFileLast
            If (bCancel = False) Begin
                Move asFiles[iFile] to sFile
                If (bPanel = True) Begin
                    Set Caption_Text   of ghoStatusPanel to ("Renaming - File No:" * String(iFile + 1) * "of:" * String(iFileLast + 1))
                    Set Title_Text     of ghoStatusPanel to "Renaming:"
                    Set TableName_Text of ghoStatusPanel to sFile
                    Send DoAdvanceTot of ghoStatusPanel
                End
                Move (rfFileHasBOM(sFile)) to bIsUtf8
                Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray(sFile)) to asCode
                If (bPanel = True) Begin
                    Set piMaximum  of ghoStatusPanel to (SizeOfArray(asCode))
                    Set piPosition of ghoProgressBar to 0
                End
                // Local handles come from THIS file only; object names are workspace-wide.
                Get _ReceiverNamesFromLines of Self asCode asFamily False to asLocalRecv
                Move asObjRecv to asRecv
                For i from 0 to (SizeOfArray(asLocalRecv) - 1)
                    If (SearchArray(asLocalRecv[i], asRecv, Desktop, RefFunc(DFSTRICMP)) = -1) ;
                        Move asLocalRecv[i] to asRecv[SizeOfArray(asRecv)]
                Loop
                // Is the family in play in this file at all? If not, a same-named call belongs to somebody
                // else and must not be reported as unfinished work.
                Get _ClassesDeclaringName of Self asCode sOld to asFileSeeds
                Move ((SizeOfArray(asLocalRecv) > 0) or (SizeOfArray(asFileSeeds) > 0)) to bInPlay
                Get _RenameMethodInLines of Self (&asCode) sOld sNew asRecv asFamily sFile bInPlay to iChanged
                If (iChanged > 0) Begin
                    // Through the write-time macro guard: macro code is never refactored (see _GuardedWriteEncodedFile).
                    Get _GuardedWriteEncodedFile sFile (&asCode) bIsUtf8 to bOK
                    If (bOK = False) ;
                        Error DFErr_Program ("Write failed:" * sFile * "(ChangeCollidingMethodName)")
                    Move (iTotal + iChanged) to iTotal
                End
                Get pbUserCancelRequested of ghoEngine to bCancel
            End
        Loop
        If (bPanel = True) ;
            Set Title_Text of ghoStatusPanel to ""
        Set piRenamedDecls       to (SizeOfArray(asFamily))
        Set piRenamedCalls       to iTotal
        Set pasRenameSharedFiles to asShared
        Move asEmptyStr to asFileObj
        Function_Return iTotal
    End_Function

    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces the legacy keyword 'Current_Object' with 'Self'.
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Changed: Legacy Current_Object -> Self }
        """ }
    Function ChangeCurrent_ObjectToSelf String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        String sChar
        Boolean bFound
        Integer iPos iItem
        tTokenizer TokenizerData

        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData
        If (LineIsNotCode(&TokenizerData)) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End

        Move (SearchArray(CS_Current_Object, TokenizerData.asLineTokens, Desktop, RefFunc(DFSTRICMP))) to iItem
        If (iItem = -1) Begin
            Move (Pos(Lowercase(CS_Current_Object), Lowercase(sLine))) to iPos
            If (iPos <> 0) Begin
                Move (Mid(sLine, 1, (iPos -1))) to sChar
                If (sChar <> "(") Begin
                    Function_Return False
                End
            End
            Else Begin
                Function_Return False
            End
        End

        Get ReplaceLineToken TokenizerData sLine CS_Current_Object CS_Self to sLine
        // Note! We call ourself recursively if there are more than one "current_object".
        Move (Pos(Lowercase(CS_Current_Object), Lowercase(sLine))) to iPos
        If (iPos <> 0) Begin
            Get ChangeCurrent_ObjectToSelf (&sLine) "" to bFound
        End

        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces the obsolete Decr command with the modern Decrement command.
        Example: Decr iCount -> Decrement iCount
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnFamily = Increment and decrement }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: Decr }
        { SummaryText = Changed: 'Decr' command to 'Decrement' }
        """ }
    Function ChangeDecrToDecrement String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        Boolean bChanged
        Get _SwapCommandKeyword (&sLine) "Decr" "Decrement" to bChanged
        Function_Return bChanged
    End_Function

    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces the obsolete Define_Auto_Increment command in a data dictionary with the modern Set Field_Auto_Increment method.
        The two fields change places and get keywords: Define_Auto_Increment <src> To <dest> becomes Set Field_Auto_Increment Field <dest> File_Field <src>.
        Example: Define_Auto_Increment ITEM0030.Nr To DOCS0010.Nr -> Set Field_Auto_Increment Field DOCS0010.Nr File_Field ITEM0030.Nr
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4533 | DEFINE_AUTO_INCREMENT }
        { SummaryText = Changed: Define_Auto_Increment to Set Field_Auto_Increment }
        """ }
    Function ChangeDefineAutoIncrement String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        Boolean bFound
        Integer iPos iStart iCmdPos iA1Start iA1End iToPos iA2Start iA2End iKwLen
        tTokenizer TokenizerData
        String sOverFull sCh sSrc sDest sKeyWord

        Move "Define_Auto_Increment" to sKeyWord
        Move (Length(sKeyWord)) to iKwLen
        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData
        If (LineIsNotCode(&TokenizerData)) Function_Return False
        Get IsKeywordInLine sKeyWord TokenizerData.asLineTokens to bFound
        If (bFound = False) ;
            Function_Return False

        // Work on the MASKED line (indent + overstrike) - position-aligned with sLine, strings hidden.
        Move (TokenizerData.sIndentation + TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine) to sOverFull
        // Locate the command keyword FOLLOWED BY WHITESPACE (there is no "Define_Auto_Increment(" call form).
        Move 1 to iStart
        Move 0 to iCmdPos
        While (iCmdPos = 0)
            Get _FindKeywordPos sOverFull sKeyWord True iStart to iPos
            If (iPos = 0) ;
                Function_Return False
            Move (Mid(sOverFull, 1, (iPos + iKwLen))) to sCh
            If (sCh = CS_S or sCh = (Character(9))) ;
                Move iPos to iCmdPos
            Else ;
                Move (iPos + 1) to iStart
        Loop

        // Arg1 (SOURCE) = first non-blank after the keyword, up to the command "To".
        Move (iCmdPos + iKwLen) to iA1Start
        While (iA1Start <= Length(sOverFull) and (Mid(sOverFull, 1, iA1Start) = CS_S or Mid(sOverFull, 1, iA1Start) = (Character(9))))
            Increment iA1Start
        Loop
        Get _FindKeywordPos sOverFull CS_To True iA1Start to iToPos
        If (iToPos = 0 or iToPos <= iA1Start) ;
            Function_Return False
        Move (iToPos - 1) to iA1End
        While (iA1End >= iA1Start and (Mid(sOverFull, 1, iA1End) = CS_S or Mid(sOverFull, 1, iA1End) = (Character(9))))
            Decrement iA1End
        Loop
        If (iA1End < iA1Start) ;
            Function_Return False

        // Arg2 (DEST) = first non-blank after "To", ending at the next blank / EOL (a lone Table.Field token).
        Move (iToPos + Length(CS_To)) to iA2Start
        While (iA2Start <= Length(sOverFull) and (Mid(sOverFull, 1, iA2Start) = CS_S or Mid(sOverFull, 1, iA2Start) = (Character(9))))
            Increment iA2Start
        Loop
        Move iA2Start to iA2End
        While (iA2End <= Length(sOverFull) and Mid(sOverFull, 1, iA2End) <> CS_S and Mid(sOverFull, 1, iA2End) <> (Character(9)))
            Increment iA2End
        Loop
        Move (iA2End - 1) to iA2End
        If (iA2End < iA2Start) ;
            Function_Return False

        // Rebuild on the RAW line: <nose>Set Field_Auto_Increment Field <dest> File_Field <src><trailing>.
        // Fields SWAP + change keyword: iField (dest) via "Field", iSysFile+iSysField (src) via "File_Field".
        Move (Mid(sLine, (iA1End - iA1Start + 1), iA1Start)) to sSrc
        Move (Mid(sLine, (iA2End - iA2Start + 1), iA2Start)) to sDest
        Move (Left(sLine, (iCmdPos - 1)) + "Set Field_Auto_Increment Field " + sDest + " File_Field " + sSrc + Mid(sLine, CI_EOL, (iA2End + 1))) to sLine
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    /* ChangeDfTimerTocTimer - what the migration changes, and the cases it refuses, now live in
       the { Description }. Maintainer notes: the per-object rules live in _MigrateDfTimerBlock
       (cRefactorFuncLib); this function only finds the objects, applies that rule to each block
       and renames Timer_Active_State -> pbEnabled file-wide. See also the profiler gate at the
       top of the body - a raw text pre-scan keeps this pass off files that hold no timer at all.
    */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Converts legacy 'Object ... is a dfTimer' objects to the modern cTimer class.
        The class is changed, Auto_Start_State, Auto_Stop_State and Timer_object are removed, Timeout becomes piTimeout, the parameters of OnTimer are removed, and Timer_Active_State becomes pbEnabled in the whole file.
        A dfTimer that started by itself (it never sets Auto_Start_State to False) does not start by itself as a cTimer, so 'Set pbEnabled to True' is added for it.
        Objects that use Timer_Message, or the parameters of OnTimer, are not changed, but get a TODO comment, because those need to be changed by hand.
        { MethodType = eOtherFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4537 | class: DFTIMER }
        { SummaryText = Migrated: dfTimer -> cTimer }
        """ }
    Function ChangeDfTimerTocTimer String[] ByRef asCode String sParameter Returns Integer
        String[] asOut asBlock asMigrated asEmpty
        String sLine sLow sT0 sReason sIndent
        Integer iCount iSize iMigrated i iMax iDepth iPos iN iFound
        Boolean bInBlock bHasAny
        tTokenizer TD

        // GATE (run profiler 2026-07-04): this pass tokenizes EVERY line and measured ~420ms on
        // EVERY file (318s of a 27-min run) while most files hold no dfTimer at all. No "dftimer"
        // and no "timer_active_state" text ANYWHERE (raw scan - a comment hit just runs the normal
        // pass) = nothing to migrate; the file is returned untouched.
        Get _FileContainsText of Self (&asCode) "dftimer" to bHasAny
        If (bHasAny = False) Begin
            Get _FileContainsText of Self (&asCode) "timer_active_state" to bHasAny
        End
        If (bHasAny = False) Begin
            Function_Return 0
        End

        Move 0 to iMigrated
        Move False to bInBlock
        Move 0 to iDepth
        Move (SizeOfArray(asCode)) to iSize
        Decrement iSize
        For iCount from 0 to iSize
            // Per-file progress: nudge the status panel's bar every ~20 lines so the current file shows
            // progress during this whole-file pass (no-op in batch mode / the test runner, where the
            // panel isn't active). Mirrors cUnicodePointerToString.
            If (Mod(iCount, 20) = 0 and (Active_State(ghoStatusPanel) = True)) Begin
                Send Update_StatusPanel of ghoStatusPanel ("Source line:" * String(iCount + 1) * "of:" * String(iSize + 1))
                Set piPosition of ghoProgressBar to (iCount + 1)
            End
            Move asCode[iCount] to sLine
            // Timer_Active_State -> pbEnabled, file-wide (DFTimer-specific external on/off switch, so
            // safe everywhere). Done first so refs inside a timer object are covered too.
            Move (Lowercase(sLine)) to sLow
            Move (Pos("timer_active_state", sLow)) to iPos
            If (iPos > 0) ;
                Move (Left(sLine, (iPos - 1)) + "pbEnabled" + Mid(sLine, Length(sLine), (iPos + 18))) to sLine
            Get Tokenizer of Self sLine to TD
            Move (SizeOfArray(TD.asLineTokens)) to iN
            Move "" to sT0
            If (iN > 0) ;
                Move (Lowercase(TD.asLineTokens[0])) to sT0

            If (bInBlock = False) Begin
                Move (SearchArray("dfTimer", TD.asLineTokens, Desktop, RefFunc(DFSTRICMP))) to iFound
                If ((sT0 = "object") and (iFound > -1)) Begin
                    // Open a dfTimer object; remember its indentation for any //TODO flag line.
                    Move True to bInBlock
                    Move 1 to iDepth
                    Move asEmpty to asBlock
                    Move sLine to asBlock[0]
                    Move (Pos(Trim(sLine), sLine)) to iPos
                    If (iPos > 1) ;
                        Move (Left(sLine, (iPos - 1))) to sIndent
                    Else ;
                        Move "" to sIndent
                End
                Else If (Left(Lowercase(Trim(sLine)), 22) = "//todo dftimer->ctimer") Begin
                    // Drop a stale "//TODO dfTimer->cTimer" flag an earlier skip-only run left, instead of
                    // copying it through: re-running otherwise STACKED one more flag per pass above the same
                    // object (Nils 2026-07-06, the flags grew a line each run). A still-unmigratable object
                    // re-emits a fresh flag below; a now-migratable / hand-converted one correctly loses it -
                    // so ALL stale-flag removal lives here; neither the migrate nor the skip path repeats it.
                End
                Else Begin
                    Move sLine to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                End
            End
            Else Begin
                Move sLine to asBlock[SizeOfArray(asBlock)]
                If (sT0 = "object") ;
                    Increment iDepth
                If (sT0 = "end_object") Begin
                    Decrement iDepth
                    If (iDepth = 0) Begin
                        Get _MigrateDfTimerBlock of Self asBlock (&sReason) to asMigrated
                        If (sReason <> "") Begin
                            // Not safe to auto-migrate - leave the object verbatim, flag it for a human.
                            Move (sIndent + "//TODO dfTimer->cTimer (skipped): " + sReason) to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                            Move (SizeOfArray(asBlock) - 1) to iMax
                            For i from 0 to iMax
                                Move asBlock[i] to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                            Loop
                        End
                        Else Begin
                            // Any stale "//TODO dfTimer->cTimer" flag above this object was already dropped
                            // during the scan, so just append the migrated block.
                            Move (SizeOfArray(asMigrated) - 1) to iMax
                            For i from 0 to iMax
                                Move asMigrated[i] to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                            Loop
                            Increment iMigrated
                        End
                        Move False to bInBlock
                    End
                End
            End
        Loop

        // Unterminated object (no matching End_Object): flush the buffered lines unchanged.
        If (bInBlock = True) Begin
            Move (SizeOfArray(asBlock) - 1) to iMax
            For i from 0 to iMax
                Move asBlock[i] to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
            Loop
        End

        Move asOut to asCode
        Function_Return iMigrated
    End_Function

    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces the legacy DfTrue and DfFalse with True and False.
        Example: Move DFTrue to bFlag -> Move True to bFlag
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = DFTrue -> True }
        """ }
    Function ChangeDfTrueDfFalse String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        String sNewBoolean
        Integer iPos iPosFalse iLen
        tTokenizer TokenizerData

        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData

        If (LineIsNotCode(&TokenizerData)) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End

        // WHOLE WORDS on the overstrike line (2026-10-23, for the Deep Scan rules 9242/9243). The
        // token gate this replaces missed DFTrue inside an expression - "(bA = DFTrue)" is ONE
        // token - so the scan would list a row the fix never cleared; and the bare Pos it searched
        // with rewrote the first "dftrue" it met, so "bDfTrueSet" became "bTrueSet". The overstrike
        // line has no string literals and no end comment, and lines up with the trimmed line.
        Get _FindKeywordPos TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine CS_DfTrue True 1 to iPos
        Get _FindKeywordPos TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine CS_DfFalse True 1 to iPosFalse
        If (iPos = 0 and iPosFalse = 0) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End

        // Spliced with Left/Mid: the old Overstrike + Replace("||") removed the FIRST "||" on
        // the line, which could be one inside a string literal before the match.
        Move (Trim(sLine)) to sLine
        Repeat
            Move CS_True to sNewBoolean
            Move (Length(CS_DfTrue)) to iLen
            Get _FindKeywordPos TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine CS_DfTrue True 1 to iPos
            If (iPos = 0) Begin
                Move CS_False to sNewBoolean
                Move (Length(CS_DfFalse)) to iLen
                Get _FindKeywordPos TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine CS_DfFalse True 1 to iPos
            End
            If (iPos <> 0) Begin
                Move (Left(sLine, (iPos - 1)) + sNewBoolean + Mid(sLine, Length(sLine), (iPos + iLen))) to sLine
                Move (Left(TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine, (iPos - 1)) + sNewBoolean ;
                    + Mid(TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine, Length(TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine), (iPos + iLen))) to TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine
            End
        Until (iPos = 0)

        Move (TokenizerData.sIndentation + String(sLine)) to sLine
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    /* 'Error 25' -> 'Error DFERR_REC_NOT_FOUND' (2026-10-23, the Deep Scan rule DS 9244). The name is the
       target DataFlex's own, from its Pkg\errornum.inc, and is a #REPLACE of the same number, so nothing
       an error handler or a LastErr test sees changes. A number DataFlex does not name is left as it is
       (DS 9245 explains those), and so is every line while the names are not known - no install and no
       cache. Which lines count is _ErrorCommandNumber's call, the same one the scan makes. */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces the number in an Error command with the name that DataFlex gives that number.
        Example: Error 25 -> Error DFERR_REC_NOT_FOUND
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Error 25 -> Error DFERR_REC_NOT_FOUND }
        """ }
    Function ChangeErrorNumberToName String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        Integer iNum iNumPos iNumLen
        String sName sText
        tTokenizer TokenizerData

        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData

        If (LineIsNotCode(&TokenizerData)) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End

        Get _ErrorCommandNumber TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine (&iNumPos) (&iNumLen) to iNum
        If (iNum <= 0) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End
        Get ErrorNameForNumber iNum to sName
        If (sName = "") Begin
            Function_Return False
        End

        // The overstrike line lines up with the trimmed line, so the digits are at the same place.
        Move (Trim(sLine)) to sText
        Move (Left(sText, (iNumPos - 1)) + sName + Mid(sText, Length(sText), (iNumPos + iNumLen))) to sText
        Move (TokenizerData.sIndentation + sText) to sLine
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    /* 'Error 300 "Text"' -> 'Error DFERR_OPERATOR "Text"' (Nils 2026-11-05, the Deep Scan rule DS 9252). Before DFERR_OPERATOR
       and DFERR_PROGRAM, developers made up a number to hang a text on. On 84 lines of a real workspace 64 stood in
       Validate_Save or Validate_Delete, with texts for the person at the keyboard.

       WHY OPERATOR BY DEFAULT. A number outside the error handler's list of user errors shows the "Unhandled Program
       Error" dialog, with the number in its text (cWindowsErrorHandler.HandleError). DFERR_PROGRAM keeps that dialog and
       DFERR_OPERATOR is on the list, so the operator gets a plain message box. So what the user sees changes, on
       purpose. The parameter keeps DFERR_PROGRAM for a workspace whose texts are for programmers.

       What keeps its number, and why: _MadeUpErrorWhyNot. A number DataFlex names is ChangeErrorNumberToName's. */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces a number of your own in an Error command that has its own text with a general error number.
        Example: Error 300 "Text" -> Error DFERR_OPERATOR "Text"
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Error 300 "Text" -> Error DFERR_OPERATOR "Text" }
        { EnumList = "DFERR_OPERATOR, DFERR_PROGRAM" }
        { InitialValue = DFERR_OPERATOR }
        { ParamHelp = Valid Values;
        \nDFERR_OPERATOR - A plain message box. For a message to the person using the program,
        \nDFERR_PROGRAM - The Unhandled Program Error dialog. For a message to a programmer }
        """ }
    Function ChangeErrorNumberToGenericError String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        Integer iNum iNumPos iNumLen
        String sName sText sCode sWhy
        tTokenizer TokenizerData

        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData

        If (LineIsNotCode(&TokenizerData)) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End

        Get _ErrorCommandNumber TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine (&iNumPos) (&iNumLen) to iNum
        If (iNum <= 0) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End
        Get ErrorNameForNumber iNum to sName
        If (sName <> "" or ErrorNamesKnown(Self) = False) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End
        Move (Trim(sLine)) to sText
        Get _MadeUpErrorWhyNot sText to sWhy
        If (sWhy <> "") Begin
            Function_Return False
        End
        Get ErrorNameForNumber (If((Uppercase(Trim(sParameter)) = "DFERR_PROGRAM"), 4402, 4401)) to sName

        Get _CodeOnlyLine sText to sCode
        Get _ErrorCommandNumber sCode (&iNumPos) (&iNumLen) to iNum
        Move (Left(sText, (iNumPos - 1)) + sName + Mid(sText, Length(sText), (iNumPos + iNumLen))) to sText
        Move (TokenizerData.sIndentation + sText) to sLine
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    /* 'Procedure Foo for DFDesktop' -> 'Procedure Foo Desktop', and 'for Desktop' / 'for BaseClass' ->
       'for cObject' (2026-10-23, the Deep Scan rules DS 9247 / 9248, from the DataFlex docs' "Global /
       Desktop Methods" page). Both are exact, measured by a probe on DataFlex 26: VdfBase.pkg makes Desktop
       and BaseClass cObject (a 'for Desktop' method ran directly in a cObject that does not delegate), and a
       'for DFDesktop' method ran in the desktop only - exactly like one marked Desktop.

       The docs add that a 'for Desktop' method was often MEANT for the desktop; the parameter Desktop
       writes the Desktop keyword for those too. That one changes which objects understand the method, so
       it is the user's choice, never the default.

       A WHOLE-FILE function because every line it changes is a method header, and the tokenizer marks a
       header line finished (bLineIsReady), so a line function never sees one. It reads the file through
       _CodeOnlyFileLines instead - the way RemovePropertyPrivate reads declarations itself. */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces an obsolete 'for' target in a method header: for DFDesktop becomes the Desktop keyword, and for Desktop and for BaseClass become for cObject, which is the same class.
        Example: Procedure Exit_Application for BaseClass -> Procedure Exit_Application for cObject
        { MethodType = eOtherFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = for Desktop -> for cObject }
        { EnumList = "cObject, Desktop" }
        { InitialValue = cObject }
        { ParamHelp = What for Desktop and for BaseClass become;
        \ncObject - for cObject, the same class: nothing changes,
        \nDesktop - the Desktop keyword, for a method meant for the desktop: it changes which objects understand it.
        \nfor DFDesktop always becomes the Desktop keyword. }
        """ }
    Function ChangeObsoleteMethodTarget String[] ByRef asCode String sParameter Returns Integer
        String[] asCodeOnly
        String sTarget
        Integer i iLast iForPos iClassPos iCount
        Boolean bToKeyword

        Get _CodeOnlyFileLines (&asCode) to asCodeOnly
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sParameter)) = "desktop") to bToKeyword
        Move 0 to iCount
        Move (SizeOfArray(asCode) - 1) to iLast
        Send _StartFileLineProgress (iLast + 1)
        For i from 0 to iLast
            If (Mod(i, 20) = 0) ;
                Send _NudgeLineProgress i (iLast + 1) "Source line:"
            Get _ObsoleteMethodTarget asCodeOnly[i] (&iForPos) (&iClassPos) to sTarget
            If (sTarget <> "") Begin
                // The code-only line lines up with the raw one.
                If (sTarget = "dfdesktop" or bToKeyword = True) ;
                    Move (Left(asCode[i], (iForPos - 1)) + "Desktop" + Mid(asCode[i], Length(asCode[i]), (iClassPos + Length(sTarget)))) to asCode[i]
                Else ;
                    Move (Left(asCode[i], (iClassPos - 1)) + "cObject" + Mid(asCode[i], Length(asCode[i]), (iClassPos + Length(sTarget)))) to asCode[i]
                Increment iCount
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return iCount
    End_Function

    /* An old class name becomes its current one (2026-10-23, the Deep Scan rule DS 9249): 'is a DFControl'
       -> 'is a DfBaseControl', 'is a Message' -> 'is a cUIObject', 'U_ImageList' -> 'U_DfBaseImageList'. The
       table is DataFlex's own (Pkg\VdfBase.pkg) and every pair is the same class, measured by a probe on
       DataFlex 26, so nothing changes - a subclass included. ImageList becomes DfBaseImageList, the class it
       is, not cImageList, which adds behaviour. Which words count is _ObsoleteClassNameAt's call, the same
       one the scan makes. A whole-file function for the reason ChangeObsoleteMethodTarget is one: 'Procedure
       Paint for dfPanel' is a method header. An 'is an' in front of the old name becomes 'is a' (2026-10-24):
       ImageList is the one old name that starts with a vowel, and no new name does. Returns the number of
       lines changed. */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces an old DataFlex class name with its current name, which is the same class.
        Example: Class cMyControl is a DFControl -> Class cMyControl is a DfBaseControl
        It covers the 27 old names that DataFlex still defines (Message, BaseClass, DFObject, DFPanel, ImageList ...), after 'is a', after 'for' in a method header, in RefClass(), and as U_ names.
        { MethodType = eOtherFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Old class names -> current names }
        """ }
    Function ChangeObsoleteClassNames String[] ByRef asCode String sParameter Returns Integer
        String[] asCodeOnly
        String sNew sText sMasked
        Integer i iLast iStart iPos iLen iCount iArt
        Boolean bChanged

        Get _CodeOnlyFileLines (&asCode) to asCodeOnly
        Move 0 to iCount
        Move (SizeOfArray(asCode) - 1) to iLast
        Send _StartFileLineProgress (iLast + 1)
        For i from 0 to iLast
            If (Mod(i, 20) = 0) ;
                Send _NudgeLineProgress i (iLast + 1) "Source line:"
            Move asCodeOnly[i] to sMasked
            If (sMasked <> "") Begin
                // The code-only line lines up with the raw one; both are edited together.
                Move asCode[i] to sText
                Move False to bChanged
                Move 1 to iStart
                Repeat
                    Get _ObsoleteClassNameAt sMasked iStart (&iPos) (&iLen) to sNew
                    If (sNew <> "") Begin
                        Move (Left(sText, (iPos - 1)) + sNew + Mid(sText, Length(sText), (iPos + iLen))) to sText
                        Move (Left(sMasked, (iPos - 1)) + sNew + Mid(sMasked, Length(sMasked), (iPos + iLen))) to sMasked
                        // The article follows the new name: 'is an ImageList' -> 'is a DfBaseImageList'.
                        Get _AnArticleBefore sMasked iPos to iArt
                        If (iArt > 0 and Pos(Lowercase(Left(sNew, 1)), "aeiou") = 0) Begin
                            Move (Left(sText, iArt) + Mid(sText, Length(sText), (iArt + 2))) to sText
                            Move (Left(sMasked, iArt) + Mid(sMasked, Length(sMasked), (iArt + 2))) to sMasked
                            Decrement iPos
                        End
                        Move (iPos + Length(sNew)) to iStart
                        Move True to bChanged
                    End
                Until (sNew = "")
                If (bChanged = True) Begin
                    Move sText to asCode[i]
                    Increment iCount
                End
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return iCount
    End_Function

    /* Field_Main_Index -> File_Field_Index in a CALL (2026-10-23, the Deep Scan rule DS 9250, Review). The
       DataFlex docs' "New DD Methods" page asks for exactly this rename, but it is not exact: on a DD,
       File_Field_Index asks the DD that owns the table (its Field_Index), so it answers -1 when the table is
       not in the DD structure - Field_Main_Index gave the table's main index - and that DD's Ordering wins.
       On a Data_Set the answer is the same (DataFlex 26's Data_Set.pkg sends Field_Main_Index from its
       File_Field_Index). An augmentation ('Function Field_Main_Index') is never changed: DS 9251 explains
       it. */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces calls of the obsolete Field_Main_Index with File_Field_Index.
        Example: Get Field_Main_Index of hoDD iFile iField to iIndex -> Get File_Field_Index of hoDD iFile iField to iIndex
        In a data dictionary, the answer then comes from the data dictionary that owns the table: -1 when the table is not in the data dictionary structure, and that data dictionary's Ordering is used. A method of your own that augments Field_Main_Index is not changed.
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Field_Main_Index -> File_Field_Index }
        """ }
    Function ChangeFieldMainIndexToFileFieldIndex String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        Integer iPos iUse
        String sText sMasked
        Boolean bChanged
        tTokenizer TokenizerData

        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData

        If (LineIsNotCode(&TokenizerData)) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End

        // Same length (16), so the overstrike line stays lined up after each rename.
        Move (Trim(sLine)) to sText
        Move TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine to sMasked
        Move False to bChanged
        Repeat
            Get _FieldMainIndexUse sMasked (&iPos) to iUse
            If (iUse = 1) Begin
                Move (Left(sText, (iPos - 1)) + "File_Field_Index" + Mid(sText, Length(sText), (iPos + 16))) to sText
                Move (Left(sMasked, (iPos - 1)) + "File_Field_Index" + Mid(sMasked, Length(sMasked), (iPos + 16))) to sMasked
                Move True to bChanged
            End
        Until (iUse <> 1)
        If (bChanged = False) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End
        Move (TokenizerData.sIndentation + sText) to sLine
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces the legacy GetAddress command with the AddressOf() function.
        Example: GetAddress of sVal to aAddress --> Move (AddressOf(sVal)) to aAddress
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: GetAddress }
        { SummaryText = GetAddress -> (AddressOf( }
        """ }
    Function ChangeGetAddress String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        Boolean bFound
        tTokenizer TokenizerData

        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData

        // Deliberately NO bIsInFunction/bIsInProcedure guard here - those are the
        // lexer's sticky inside-a-body scope flags, and a GetAddress command lives
        // almost exclusively inside method bodies. The old guard disabled the
        // function on every real occurrence (cWinFunc.pkg was never changed), while
        // the single-line fixtures carry no scope, so the tests stayed green.
        If (TokenizerData.bLineIsReady = True or TokenizerData.bHasEndSemiColon = True or ;
            TokenizerData.bIsInCommand = True or ;
            TokenizerData.bIsInImage = True or TokenizerData.bIsVariableDeclaration = True) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End

        Get IsKeywordInLine (CS_GetAddress + CS_D) TokenizerData.asLineTokens to bFound
        If (bFound = False) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End

        // What DOES need protecting is a line that merely MENTIONS GetAddress - a
        // declaration ("Function GetAddress Returns String") or an expression use
        // ("Entry_Item (GetAddress(...))"). Require the COMMAND position - the FIRST
        // token - like ChangeSysdate; an If-prefixed nose form is left untouched on
        // purpose (the rebuild below is whole-line and would lose it).
        //
        // THE FIRST TOKEN, NOT FirstLeftCommand (2026-08-16). FirstLeftCommand only
        // recognises words in pasCommands, and that list is seeded from the TARGET's
        // language catalogue - which, targeting DF 26, no longer lists the obsolete
        // GetAddress. So this function converted every fixture in the DFUnit runner
        // (whose catalogue still carries the word) and ZERO lines in the FT app's plan
        // runs on WinServ ("0 lines GetAddress -> (AddressOf(" twice, 52 rows), while
        // the analyzer's dry-run - the same runner-side lexer - kept promising the fix.
        // An obsolete-construct converter must not depend on the obsolete word being in
        // the catalogue (the Indicate lesson, 2026-08-04): match the token directly.
        If (SizeOfArray(TokenizerData.asLineTokens) = 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        If (Lowercase(TokenizerData.asLineTokens[0]) <> Lowercase(CS_GetAddress)) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End

        // The rebuild needs both sides. The no-"of" variant ("GetAddress sVal to
        // pVal") leaves OfStatement empty and would emit a broken
        // "Move (AddressOf())" - leave that shape untouched.
        If (TokenizerData.OfStatement.sCode = "" or TokenizerData.ToStatement.sCode = "") Begin
            Function_Return False
        End

        If (TokenizerData.sEndComment <> "") Begin
            Move (TokenizerData.sIndentation + CS_Move * "(" + CS_AddressOf + "(" + TokenizerData.OfStatement.sCode + "))" * CS_To * String(TokenizerData.ToStatement.sCode) * String(TokenizerData.sEndComment)) to sLine
        End
        Else Begin
            Move (TokenizerData.sIndentation + CS_Move * "(" + CS_AddressOf + "(" + TokenizerData.OfStatement.sCode + "))" * CS_To * String(TokenizerData.ToStatement.sCode)) to sLine
        End

        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    /* ChangeGetFileExistsToFileExist (Nils 2026-07-16) - modernizes calls to the legacy DAW
       utility function Get_File_Exists (func.pkg: 'Function get_file_exists Global String
       filename$ Returns Integer') to the native File_Exist command. The argument forms it
       accepts and the shapes it bails on are now in the { Description } below.

       The <file-arg> is cut from the RAW line by overstrike positions + the stripped-indent
       offset: a literal may contain ' to ' or '//', and the overstrike masks those, so the
       boundary-safe 'to' search can never land inside one.

       Idempotence is free: the bail on a non-Get first token also makes the converted File_Exist
       line immune. A function-style expression call - Move (Get_File_Exists(sF)) to i - is
       deliberately left alone: rewriting inside an expression is not line-local. The Deep Scan
       rule (v14) routes the Get form here. */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces the legacy Get_File_Exists function (from func.pkg) with the File_Exist command.
        Example: Get Get_File_Exists sFileName to iExists  -->  File_Exist sFileName iExists
        File_Exist has no 'to' keyword. Only the plain Get form is converted; a call in an expression, such as (Get_File_Exists(sF)), must be changed by hand.
        The file argument (a variable, a string or an expression in parentheses) is copied exactly as it is, including its spaces, even when a string contains ' to ' or '//'.
        A call with an 'of' clause, a line that continues with ';', and a line that does not end with 'to <var>' are not changed. Running it again is safe, because a converted File_Exist line is no longer a Get statement.
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Changed: Get_File_Exists -> File_Exist command }
        """ }
    Function ChangeGetFileExistsToFileExist String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        tTokenizer TokenizerData
        String sOver sWord sArg sTarget sNew
        Integer iPosName iPosTo iStart iPos iIndentLen iArgStart iArgLen iWords

        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData
        If (TokenizerData.bLineIsReady = True or TokenizerData.bIsComment = True or ;
            TokenizerData.bIsInCommand = True or TokenizerData.bIsInImage = True or ;
            TokenizerData.bHasEndSemiColon = True) ;
            Function_Return False
        If (TokenizerData.bIsGetStatement = False) ;
            Function_Return False
        Move TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine to sOver
        // Word 2 must be the function name itself (word 1 is the 'Get' - bIsGetStatement above).
        Get _NthWord sOver 2 to sWord
        If (Lowercase(sWord) <> "get_file_exists") ;
            Function_Return False
        // No 'of' clause: a GLOBAL function call has none - anything else is not our shape.
        Get _FindKeywordPos sOver "of" True 1 to iPos
        If (iPos > 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        Get _FindKeywordPos sOver "get_file_exists" True 1 to iPosName
        If (iPosName = 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        // The LAST whole-word 'to' = the command tail (iStart-walk; masked literals can never
        // contribute a stray 'to').
        Move 0 to iPosTo
        Move 1 to iStart
        Repeat
            Get _FindKeywordPos sOver "to" True iStart to iPos
            If (iPos > 0) Begin
                Move iPos to iPosTo
                Move (iPos + 1) to iStart
            End
        Until (iPos = 0)
        If (iPosTo <= iPosName) ;
            Function_Return False
        // The target = the LAST word, and it must sit right after that 'to' (word n-1 = 'to').
        Move 0 to iWords
        Repeat
            Get _NthWord sOver (iWords + 1) to sWord
            If (sWord <> "") ;
                Increment iWords
        Until (sWord = "")
        If (iWords < 4) ;
            Function_Return False
        Get _NthWord sOver iWords to sTarget
        Get _NthWord sOver (iWords - 1) to sWord
        If (Lowercase(sWord) <> "to" or sTarget = "") ;
            Function_Return False
        // The file argument: RAW text between the function name and the 'to' - verbatim, so a
        // string literal's contents and inner spacing survive (overstrike position + indent
        // offset, per the splice-helper contract).
        Move (Length(TokenizerData.sIndentation)) to iIndentLen
        Move (iIndentLen + iPosName + 15) to iArgStart               // 15 = Length("get_file_exists")
        Move (iIndentLen + iPosTo - iArgStart) to iArgLen
        If (iArgLen <= 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        Move (Trim(Mid(sLine, iArgLen, iArgStart))) to sArg
        If (sArg = "") ;
            Function_Return False
        Move (TokenizerData.sIndentation + "File_Exist " + sArg + " " + sTarget) to sNew
        If (TokenizerData.sEndComment <> "") ;
            Move (sNew + "  " + TokenizerData.sEndComment) to sNew
        Move sNew to sLine
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    /* ChangeGetDateFormatToAttribute (Nils 2026-09-15) - the obsolete Get_Date_Format command to
       its Database-API replacement, Get_Attribute DF_DATE_FORMAT. The command still compiles on
       DF26 - FMAC wraps it in WarningEx DFERR_COMP_WARNING_OBSOLETE_CMD, the 4533 the System and
       Sysdate converters ride - so it has both lanes: the { ModRecipe } below for the compiler
       lane and the Deep Scan rule (v69) for the scan lane.

       VALUE-EXACT, MEASURED, not read off the docs: a probe on the DF26 x64 runtime set
       DF_DATE_FORMAT to each of the three values in turn and read it back through both forms -
       old=0 new=0, old=1 new=1, old=2 new=2 (DF_DATE_USA / DF_DATE_EUROPEAN / DF_DATE_MILITARY,
       #REPLACEd to 0 / 1 / 2 in FMAC). So the code that follows the read - a real workspace
       compares the variable with the bare literals 0 and 2 - keeps meaning exactly what it did.

       Built on _SwapLeadingCommandKeyword like System -> Abort: the keyword is replaced, the
       indentation and everything after it ('to <var>', a trailing comment) are carried verbatim.
       One guard on top of the swap's own: the tail must hold a whole-word 'to', because a
       Get_Date_Format with no target is not a shape the compiler accepts and rewriting it would
       only move the error. Idempotence is free - a converted line leads with Get_Attribute. */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces the obsolete Get_Date_Format command with Get_Attribute DF_DATE_FORMAT.
        Example: Get_Date_Format to iFormat  -->  Get_Attribute DF_DATE_FORMAT to iFormat
        The value is the same in both cases (DF_DATE_USA (0), DF_DATE_EUROPEAN (1) or DF_DATE_MILITARY (2)), so the code that reads the variable afterwards does not need to change.
        The indentation, the variable and any comment at the end of the line are kept as they are. Mentions in comments or strings are not changed, and neither is a line without a 'to <var>' ending. Running it again is safe.
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: Get_Date_Format }
        { SummaryText = Changed: Get_Date_Format -> Get_Attribute DF_DATE_FORMAT }
        """ }
    Function ChangeGetDateFormatToAttribute String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        tTokenizer TokenizerData
        Boolean bChanged
        Integer iPosTo

        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData
        // The tail must carry the 'to' - on the strings/comment-masked line, so a 'to' inside a
        // trailing comment cannot stand in for the real one.
        Get _FindKeywordPos TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine "to" True 1 to iPosTo
        If (iPosTo = 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        Get _SwapLeadingCommandKeyword (&sLine) TokenizerData "Get_Date_Format" "Get_Attribute DF_DATE_FORMAT" to bChanged
        Function_Return bChanged
    End_Function

    /* ChangeGetCurrentLockcountToAttribute (Nils 2026-09-15) - the obsolete Get_Current_Lockcount
       command to its Database-API replacement, Get_Attribute DF_TRAN_COUNT.

       ONE LANE, and that is the point. FMAC's #COMMAND GET_CURRENT_LOCKCOUNT carries no WarningEx -
       it compiles in silence, unlike the Get_FileList / Get_Number_Format siblings a few lines away
       in the same file - so there is no compiler message to hang a { ModRecipe } on, and the Deep
       Scan rule is the ONLY way this line is ever offered a fix. (Confirmed by building a probe that
       uses all three: the listing warns on the other two and says nothing about this one.)

       VALUE-EXACT, MEASURED, not read off the docs: a probe on the DF26 x64 runtime read the count
       through both forms with nothing held, inside one Lock, inside two, and after unwinding -
       0/0, 1/1, 2/2, 0/0. DataFlex counts a Lock and a transaction on the same nesting counter, so
       the code that compares the variable afterwards keeps meaning exactly what it did.

       Built on _SwapLeadingCommandKeyword like Get_Date_Format: the keyword is replaced and the
       indentation, the 'to <var>' tail and any trailing comment are carried verbatim. One guard on
       top of the swap's own - the tail must hold a whole-word 'to', because a Get_Current_Lockcount
       with no target is not a shape the compiler accepts and rewriting it would only move the
       error. Idempotence is free: a converted line leads with Get_Attribute. */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces the obsolete Get_Current_Lockcount command with Get_Attribute DF_TRAN_COUNT.
        Example: Get_Current_Lockcount to iLocks  -->  Get_Attribute DF_TRAN_COUNT to iLocks
        The number is the same in both cases (how many transactions or locks are active, 0 when there are none), so the code that reads the variable afterwards does not need to change.
        The indentation, the variable and any comment at the end of the line are kept as they are. Mentions in comments or strings are not changed, and neither is a line without a 'to <var>' ending. Running it again is safe.
        The compiler never warns about this command, so Analyze Source is what finds it.
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Changed: Get_Current_Lockcount -> Get_Attribute DF_TRAN_COUNT }
        """ }
    Function ChangeGetCurrentLockcountToAttribute String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        tTokenizer TokenizerData
        Boolean bChanged
        Integer iPosTo

        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData
        // The tail must carry the 'to' - on the strings/comment-masked line, so a 'to' inside a
        // trailing comment cannot stand in for the real one.
        Get _FindKeywordPos TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine "to" True 1 to iPosTo
        If (iPosTo = 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        Get _SwapLeadingCommandKeyword (&sLine) TokenizerData "Get_Current_Lockcount" "Get_Attribute DF_TRAN_COUNT" to bChanged
        Function_Return bChanged
    End_Function

    /* ChangeFileListCommandToAttribute (Nils 2026-09-15) - the obsolete Get_FileList and
       Set_FileList commands to their Database-API replacement, the DF_FILELIST_NAME attribute.
       One function for both: they are the read and the write of one global attribute.

       NO PROBE NEEDED HERE - FMAC settles it by construction. Both macros ARE the replacement:

           #COMMAND GET_FILELIST RL#UGDNI .
             WarningEx GENERAL DFERR_COMP_WARNING_OBSOLETE_CMD "Obsolete command: Get_FileList"
               GET_ATTRIBUTE DF_FILELIST_NAME TO !1

       and the Set twin the same way, so the conversion is what the compiler already does, written
       out. (Measured anyway while the probe was up: both forms hand back the same file list name.)

       MIND THE SYNTAX - the legacy commands take their argument with NO 'to' keyword. Set_FileList
       sName is the shape; Set_FileList to sName is Error 4345, because the macro declares one
       argument and no TO. So the swap INSERTS the keyword along with the attribute name, and that
       is also what makes it idempotent: a converted line leads with Get_Attribute / Set_Attribute
       and is never matched again. Note too that DF_FILELIST_NAME is a GLOBAL attribute - FMAC lists
       it under 'Attributes: Global' - so there is no 'of <File>.File_Number' part to carry.

       The guard is the tail: there must be an argument after the keyword, and it must not itself be
       the word 'to'. Without the first, the emitted line would be an attribute access with nothing
       to read into; the second is a line that was never this command. The tail is read off
       _CodeOnlyLine, so a trailing comment can never be mistaken for the argument.

       Both commands carry a WarningEx, so they have both lanes - the two { ModRecipe } tags below
       for the compiler's 4533, and the Deep Scan rules for the scan. */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces the obsolete Get_FileList and Set_FileList commands with the DF_FILELIST_NAME attribute.
        Examples: Get_FileList sName  -->  Get_Attribute DF_FILELIST_NAME to sName; Set_FileList sName  -->  Set_Attribute DF_FILELIST_NAME to sName
        The legacy commands have no 'to' keyword, so it is added together with the attribute name. The compiler makes the same change for these commands, so the behaviour does not change.
        DF_FILELIST_NAME is a global attribute, so there is no 'of <File>.File_Number' part.
        The indentation, the argument and any comment at the end of the line are kept as they are. Mentions in comments or strings are not changed, and neither is a line without an argument. Running it again is safe.
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: Get_FileList }
        { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: Set_FileList }
        { SummaryText = Changed: Get_/Set_FileList -> Get_/Set_Attribute DF_FILELIST_NAME }
        """ }
    Function ChangeFileListCommandToAttribute String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        tTokenizer TokenizerData
        Boolean bChanged
        String sCode sKeyword sNew sTail sFirst
        Integer iSpace

        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData
        // Strings blanked and a trailing comment cut, so neither can supply the argument.
        Get _CodeOnlyLine sLine to sCode
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sCode))) to sCode
        Move "" to sKeyword
        If (Left(sCode, 12) = "get_filelist") Begin
            Move "Get_FileList" to sKeyword
            Move "Get_Attribute DF_FILELIST_NAME to" to sNew
        End
        Else If (Left(sCode, 12) = "set_filelist") Begin
            Move "Set_FileList" to sKeyword
            Move "Set_Attribute DF_FILELIST_NAME to" to sNew
        End
        If (sKeyword = "") ;
            Function_Return False
        // An argument has to follow, and it may not be a 'to': the legacy commands take none, so a
        // line that has one is not this command and is left for a human to read.
        Move (Trim(Mid(sCode, (Length(sCode) - 12), 13))) to sTail
        If (sTail = "") ;
            Function_Return False
        Move (Pos(" ", sTail)) to iSpace
        If (iSpace > 0) ;
            Move (Left(sTail, (iSpace - 1))) to sFirst
        Else ;
            Move sTail to sFirst
        If (sFirst = "to") ;
            Function_Return False
        Get _SwapLeadingCommandKeyword (&sLine) TokenizerData sKeyword sNew to bChanged
        Function_Return bChanged
    End_Function

    /* ChangeIfchangeCommandToAttribute (Nils 2026-09-15, "yes, build the Ifchange converter as
       Review") - the obsolete Ifchange command to a DF_FILE_CHANGED read and the test it guarded.
       The transform, every guard, and the reasoning behind Review rather than Auto-fix live on
       _TransformIfchangeCommands (cRefactorFuncLib), which is also where the fixtures drive it.
       This is the published entry point and nothing else. */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces the obsolete Ifchange command with a read of DF_FILE_CHANGED and the test it was used for.
        Example: Ifchange pay gosub logchange  becomes  Boolean bDfrFileChanged / Get_Attribute DF_FILE_CHANGED of pay.File_Number to bDfrFileChanged / If bDfrFileChanged gosub logchange
        The Boolean is declared once in each method, just above where it is first used. The indentation, the command after the test and any comment at the end of the line are kept as they are.
        Review the result. Both forms have been tested to report the same condition, and the new form is the one the DataFlex framework itself uses. But this is the only converter that adds a declaration to the method, and it has not yet been run on real code.
        Lines that are not changed get a TODO comment with the reason: a line that continues a statement with ';', a command outside a procedure or function, a statement that continues on the next line, and a file argument that is not a plain table name. An Ifchange that is not the first word on its line is never changed.
        { MethodType = eOtherFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: IfChange | Suggest }
        { SummaryText = Changed: Ifchange -> Get_Attribute DF_FILE_CHANGED + If }
        """ }
    Function ChangeIfchangeCommandToAttribute String[] ByRef asCode String sParameter Returns Integer
        Integer iChanges

        Get _TransformIfchangeCommands (&asCode) to iChanges
        Function_Return iChanges
    End_Function

    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces the legacy IFNOT command, and the two-word 'If Not' form, with an expression.
        Examples: IFNOT Found Begin --> If (Not(Found)) Begin; If Not (Found) Begin --> If (Not(Found)) Begin (an unnecessary pair of parentheses is removed); If Not (iA = iB) Begin --> If (Not(iA = iB)) Begin.
        A condition with several terms and no parentheses (If Not a eq b) is not changed, because putting only the first term inside Not() would change it. Lines with indicators in square brackets are not changed either; the indicator converters handle those.
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = IFNOT / If Not command -> If (Not(...)) }
        """ }
    Function ChangeIfNotCommandToExpression String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        tTokenizer TokenizerData
        Integer iItem iPos iSize iCount iNext
        String sCode sToken sNext sRight sCond
        Boolean bChanged

        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData
        If (LineIsNotCode(&TokenizerData)) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End

        Move (SearchArray("IFNOT", TokenizerData.asLineTokens, Desktop, RefFunc(DFSTRICMP))) to iItem
        If (iItem = -1) Begin
            // Not the one-word IFNOT: try the two-word 'If Not <operand>' command form - the safe
            // single-operand subset (see the funclib helper for the exact rules and bail-outs).
            Get _ChangeIfNotPairToExpression (&sLine) to bChanged
            Function_Return bChanged
        End
        
        Move sLine to sCode
        // Locate IFNOT boundary-aware on the MASKED line (position-aligned
        // with sLine) via _FindKeywordPos - a bare Pos could match inside an
        // identifier or a string literal before the real IFNOT (2026-06-10
        // audit).
        Get _FindKeywordPos (TokenizerData.sIndentation + TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine) "ifnot" True 1 to iPos
        Move (Left(sCode, (iPos + Length("ifnot")))) to sCode
        If (iPos <> 0) Begin
            Move (Overstrike("|||||", sCode, iPos)) to sCode
            Move (Replace("|||||", sCode, CS_If)) to sCode
            Increment iItem
            Move TokenizerData.asLineTokens[iItem] to sToken
            // A [bracket] indicator condition is OWNED by the indicator converters: ChangeLegacyIndicators
            // turns 'ifnot [seqeof] begin' -> 'ifnot (seqeof) begin' (tested), then a later cycle modernises
            // the 'ifnot'. Wrapping the RAW bracket here emits the un-compilable 'If (Not([seqeof]))'. The
            // two-word path (_ChangeIfNotPairToExpression) already bails on a '[' operand - match it.
            If (Left(sToken, 1) = "[") ;
                Function_Return False
            // A legacy TEST COMMAND with an argument ('Ifnot Status <file> stmt') is not a Boolean
            // operand - see _IsLegacyFileTestCommand. Declined; the Status rule explains it.
            If (_IsLegacyFileTestCommand(Self, Lowercase(sToken))) ;
                Function_Return False
            Move (TokenizerData.iTokenCount) to iSize
            Decrement iSize
            /* THE OPERAND MUST BE THE WHOLE CONDITION (2026-09-07). 'ifnot A in B stmt' wrapped as
               'If (Not(A)) in B stmt' - a line that then READ as a valid If to ChangeInToContains, which
               swapped it into 'If ((B) contains (Not(A))) stmt': 29 silently inverted conditions in one
               run. So the token after the operand decides, as in _ChangeIfNotPairToExpression:
               - a lone legacy IN with single-token operands is composed the modern way HERE -
                 'If (Not(B Contains A)) stmt' - because IN is compile-invalid inside an expression and
                 ChangeInToContains never sees an IFNOT line (its gate wants an 'If');
               - any other continuation (a legacy comparison the operator pass has not yet parenthesised,
                 match, and/or, a modern operator) bails untouched: once the condition is one token, a
                 later pass converts it;
               - a parenthesised operand the source does not contain verbatim is the parser's own gluing
                 of a legacy comparison ('(sName eq "X")'): the same bail, for the same reason. */
            If (Left(sToken, 1) = "(" and Pos(sToken, sLine) = 0) ;
                Function_Return False
            Move (iItem + 1) to iNext
            Move "" to sCond
            If (iNext <= iSize) Begin
                Move (Lowercase(TokenizerData.asLineTokens[iNext])) to sNext
                If (sNext = "in") Begin
                    If (iNext + 1 > iSize) ;
                        Function_Return False
                    Move TokenizerData.asLineTokens[iNext + 1] to sRight
                    If (iNext + 2 <= iSize) Begin
                        Move (Lowercase(TokenizerData.asLineTokens[iNext + 2])) to sNext
                        If (_IsIfConditionContinuation(Self, sNext)) ;
                            Function_Return False
                    End
                    Move ("(" + sRight * CS_Contains * sToken + ")") to sCond
                    Move (iNext + 1) to iItem      // the statement starts after B
                End
                Else If (_IsIfConditionContinuation(Self, sNext)) ;
                    Function_Return False
            End
            If (sCond = "") ;
                Append sCode "(" CS_Not "(" sToken ")) "
            Else ;
                Append sCode "(" CS_Not sCond ") "
            For iCount from (iItem +1) to iSize
                Append sCode TokenizerData.asLineTokens[iCount] CS_S
            Loop
        End
        Move (RTrim(sCode)) to sCode
        If (sLine <> sCode) Begin
            Move sCode to sLine
            Function_Return True
        End
        Function_Return False
    End_Function

    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces the obsolete Incr command with the modern Increment command.
        Example: Incr iCount -> Increment iCount
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnFamily = Increment and decrement }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: Incr }
        { SummaryText = Changed: 'Incr' command to 'Increment' }
        """ }
    Function ChangeIncrToIncrement String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        Boolean bChanged
        Get _SwapCommandKeyword (&sLine) "Incr" "Increment" to bChanged
        Function_Return bChanged
    End_Function

    /* Text that was part of the Description, but was moved to here as it made the 
       Description meta-tag to large, leading to a compilation problem.
          Not touched: `Find EQ <table>.<field>` (constant form), `-1` (recnum
          mode), integer literals outside these contexts, comments, and string
          literals (the tokenizer overlays string contents so they are immune),
          and any line that does not reference the named table.
    */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Changes index numbers in Find, vFind, Constrained_Find, Constrained_Clear and For_all commands from one number to another, for one table only. This is useful when the indexes of a table have been renumbered.
        Both the Index.<N> form and the plain number are changed, in these places: the <N> after a mode keyword in Find, Send Find and Get Find (EQ, GT, LT, GE, LE, NE, FIRST, LAST, NEXT, PREV); the second argument of vFind <tbl> <N> <mode>; the <N> after 'by' in Constrained_Find and Constrained_Clear; and the <N> after by or down in For_all.
        The table is recognised whatever its case, either by its name, or by a data dictionary object named o<TableName>_DD or o<TableName>_DataDictionary, the two common DataFlex naming conventions. Other naming conventions are not recognised; change those by hand.
        The function's parameter is "<TableName>,<from>,<to>": the table name and two different positive numbers, for example "Customer,3,5".
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { EnumList = "ANY" }
        { SummaryText = Changed: index number in Find/Constrained/For_all commands }
        { ParamHelp = Three comma-separated values: the table name, the FROM index
        \nnumber, and the TO index number. Example "Customer,3,5" changes every
        \nIndex.3 / by 3 / vFind Customer.X 3 reference for the Customer table to
        \nuse 5 instead. Both must be positive - DataFlex indexes are 1-based, and
        \n-1 (recnum mode) is never modified. }
        """ }
    Function ChangeIndexNumber String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        String sOrgLine sFromStr sToStr sToken sTokenLC sRebuilt sNewLine sLineLC
        String sKwLC sLineLCNow sNextChar sTableName sTableNameLC sRemainder
        Integer iFromIndex iToIndex iPos iPos2 iSize iItem iCandidateIdx
        Integer iKwLen iSearchFrom iAbsPos iEndPos iPass
        Boolean bChanged bDone bRunPass bTableMatch
        tTokenizer TokenizerData

        // ---- Parse and validate sParameter (expect "<TableName>,<from>,<to>") ----
        Move (Trim(sParameter)) to sParameter
        Move (Pos(",", sParameter)) to iPos
        If (iPos = 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        Move (Trim(Left(sParameter, iPos - 1)))         to sTableName
        Move (Trim(Mid(sParameter, CI_EOL, iPos + 1))) to sRemainder
        Move (Pos(",", sRemainder)) to iPos2
        If (iPos2 = 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        Move (Trim(Left(sRemainder, iPos2 - 1)))         to sFromStr
        Move (Trim(Mid(sRemainder, CI_EOL, iPos2 + 1))) to sToStr
        If (sTableName = "") ;
            Function_Return False
        Move (Integer(sFromStr)) to iFromIndex
        Move (Integer(sToStr))   to iToIndex
        If (iFromIndex <= 0 or iToIndex <= 0 or iFromIndex = iToIndex) ;
            Function_Return False
        // Normalize back to canonical digit strings (strips any "+03" oddities).
        Move (String(iFromIndex)) to sFromStr
        Move (String(iToIndex))   to sToStr
        Move (Lowercase(sTableName)) to sTableNameLC

        // ---- Guard: skip blank/comment/image/in-command/variable-decl lines ----
        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData
        If (LineIsNotCode(&TokenizerData)) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End

        // ---- Quick line filter: must contain at least one of the five trigger
        //      keywords. Avoids accidentally renaming `Index.N` symbols in
        //      unrelated lines. Listed explicitly (vfind / constrained_find are
        //      technically redundant against `contains "find"` since "find" is a
        //      substring of both, but listed for clarity of intent). ----
        Move (Lowercase(TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine)) to sLineLC
        If (not(sLineLC contains "find")             and ;
            not(sLineLC contains "vfind")            and ;
            not(sLineLC contains "constrained_find") and ;
            not(sLineLC contains "constrained_clear") and ;
            not(sLineLC contains "for_all")) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End

        // ---- Table scoping: line must reference sTableName as either a
        //      direct token OR via the DDO naming convention. ----
        Get _HasWordInLine sLineLC sTableNameLC to bTableMatch
        If (bTableMatch = False) Begin
            Get _HasWordInLine sLineLC ("o" + sTableNameLC + "_dd") to bTableMatch
        End
        If (bTableMatch = False) Begin
            Get _HasWordInLine sLineLC ("o" + sTableNameLC + "_datadictionary") to bTableMatch
        End
        If (bTableMatch = False) ;
            Function_Return False

        Move sLine to sOrgLine
        Move False to bChanged
        Move (SizeOfArray(TokenizerData.asLineTokens)) to iSize
        Decrement iSize
        If (iSize < 0) ;
            Function_Return False

        // ---- Walk tokens. Two independent checks per token: ----
        //   (a) Token IS `Index.<from>` -> rewrite digit, preserve "Index." case.
        //   (b) Token IS the leading keyword of a recognized context (Find /
        //       vFind / Constrained_Find / Constrained_Clear / For_all)
        //       whose index slot holds the bare integer <from>.
        For iItem from 0 to iSize
            Move TokenizerData.asLineTokens[iItem] to sToken
            Move (Lowercase(sToken)) to sTokenLC

            // (a) Index.<from>  ->  Index.<to>
            If (sTokenLC = ("index." + sFromStr)) Begin
                Move (Pos(".", sToken)) to iPos
                Move (Left(sToken, iPos) + sToStr) to TokenizerData.asLineTokens[iItem]
                Move True to bChanged
            End

            // (b1) Find (Send Find / Get Find / inline Find): walk forward to
            //      a mode keyword; the token after the mode is the index slot.
            If (sTokenLC = Lowercase(CS_Find)) Begin
                Get _IndexSlotAfterKeyword TokenizerData.asLineTokens (iItem + 1) iSize "|eq|gt|lt|ge|le|ne|first|last|next|prev|" to iCandidateIdx
                If (iCandidateIdx > 0) Begin
                    If (TokenizerData.asLineTokens[iCandidateIdx] = sFromStr) Begin
                        Move sToStr to TokenizerData.asLineTokens[iCandidateIdx]
                        Move True to bChanged
                    End
                End
            End

            // (b2) vFind <tbl> <N> <mode>  -> index is exactly iItem+2.
            If (sTokenLC = "vfind") Begin
                If ((iItem + 2) <= iSize) Begin
                    If (TokenizerData.asLineTokens[iItem + 2] = sFromStr) Begin
                        Move sToStr to TokenizerData.asLineTokens[iItem + 2]
                        Move True to bChanged
                    End
                End
            End

            // (b3) Constrained_Find / Constrained_Clear / For_all
            //      -> walk forward for `by` (or `down` for For_all);
            //         the token after is the index slot. -1 (recnum) is
            //         never touched because the FROM check requires a
            //         positive integer match.
            If (sTokenLC = "constrained_find" or sTokenLC = "constrained_clear" or sTokenLC = "for_all") Begin
                If (sTokenLC = "for_all") ;
                    Get _IndexSlotAfterKeyword TokenizerData.asLineTokens (iItem + 1) iSize "|by|down|" to iCandidateIdx
                Else ;
                    Get _IndexSlotAfterKeyword TokenizerData.asLineTokens (iItem + 1) iSize "|by|" to iCandidateIdx
                If (iCandidateIdx > 0) Begin
                    If (TokenizerData.asLineTokens[iCandidateIdx] = sFromStr) Begin
                        Move sToStr to TokenizerData.asLineTokens[iCandidateIdx]
                        Move True to bChanged
                    End
                End
            End
        Loop

        // ---- Reassemble line if anything actually changed via tokens ----
        If (bChanged = True) Begin
            Get RebuildLineFromTokens TokenizerData.asLineTokens 0 to sRebuilt
            Get AssembleOutputLine sRebuilt TokenizerData to sNewLine
            If (sNewLine <> sOrgLine) Begin
                Move sNewLine to sLine
            End
        End

        // ---- Textual fallback for `by <from>` / `down <from>` patterns. ----
        // The framework tokenizer bundles legacy-operator expressions
        // (e.g. on "Constrained_Clear EQ MyTable by 3" the EQ-mode causes
        // "EQ MyTable by 3" to collapse into a single expression token),
        // so the per-token walk above can't see `by` as a separate token.
        // Find / vFind have a tokenizer-level exclusion so they aren't
        // bundled; Constrained_Find / Constrained_Clear / For_all don't.
        // We catch the missed cases here with a direct case-insensitive
        // search on sLine, only for lines that already passed the
        // Constrained_*/For_all line filter. Digit-boundary check on
        // the trailing char prevents `by 30` from matching `by 3`.
        If (sLineLC contains "constrained_find" or ;
            sLineLC contains "constrained_clear" or ;
            sLineLC contains "for_all") Begin
            // Two passes: " by " always, then " down " only for For_all lines.
            For iPass from 0 to 1
                If (iPass = 0) Begin
                    Move " by " to sKwLC
                    Move True to bRunPass
                End
                Else Begin
                    Move " down "                     to sKwLC
                    Move (sLineLC contains "for_all") to bRunPass
                End
                If (bRunPass = True) Begin
                    Move (Length(sKwLC)) to iKwLen
                    Move (Lowercase(sLine)) to sLineLCNow
                    Move 1 to iSearchFrom
                    Move False to bDone
                    While (iSearchFrom <= Length(sLineLCNow) and bDone = False)
                        Move (Pos(sKwLC + sFromStr, Mid(sLineLCNow, CI_EOL, iSearchFrom))) to iAbsPos
                        If (iAbsPos = 0) Begin
                            Move True to bDone
                        End
                        Else Begin
                            Move (iAbsPos + iSearchFrom - 1) to iAbsPos
                            Move (iAbsPos + iKwLen + Length(sFromStr)) to iEndPos
                            Move "" to sNextChar
                            If (iEndPos <= Length(sLine)) Move (Mid(sLine, 1, iEndPos)) to sNextChar
                            If (sNextChar = "" or not("0123456789" contains sNextChar)) Begin
                                Move (Left(sLine, iAbsPos + iKwLen - 1) + sToStr + Mid(sLine, CI_EOL, iEndPos)) to sLine
                                Move (Lowercase(sLine)) to sLineLCNow
                                Move (iAbsPos + iKwLen + Length(sToStr)) to iSearchFrom
                            End
                            Else Begin
                                Move (iAbsPos + 1) to iSearchFrom
                            End
                        End
                    Loop
                End
            Loop
        End

        Function_Return (sLine <> sOrgLine)
    End_Function

    // The token after the first token in iFrom..iLast that is one of the "|"-fenced keywords (lowercased),
    // or 0 when there is none - the index slot of a Find / Constrained_Find / For_all line: the token that
    // follows its mode, or its 'by' / 'down'.
    Function _IndexSlotAfterKeyword String[] asTokens Integer iFrom Integer iLast String sKeywordsLC Returns Integer
        Integer iNext iSlot
        Boolean bFound

        Move 0 to iSlot
        Move False to bFound
        Move iFrom to iNext
        While (iNext <= iLast and bFound = False)
            If (Pos("|" + Lowercase(asTokens[iNext]) + "|", sKeywordsLC) > 0) Begin
                Move True to bFound
                If ((iNext + 1) <= iLast) ;
                    Move (iNext + 1) to iSlot
            End
            Increment iNext
        Loop
        Function_Return iSlot
    End_Function

    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces the IN command with the Contains operator. The two values change places, and parentheses are added.
        Example: If sSub in sAlphabet -> If (sAlphabet Contains sSub)
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Changed: 'In' to 'Contains' }
        """ }
    Function ChangeInToContains String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        String sOrg sPattern sMatched sRemain sHost sSub sText sAfter sBefore sExpression sToken
        Integer iSize iCount iItem
        Boolean bCondCtx
        tTokenizer TokenizerData

        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData
        If (LineIsNotCode(&TokenizerData)) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End
        Move (SearchArray(CS_Insert, TokenizerData.asLineTokens, Desktop, RefFunc(DFSTRICMP))) to iItem
        If (iItem <> -1) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End
        Move (SearchArray(CS_Pos, TokenizerData.asLineTokens, Desktop, RefFunc(DFSTRICMP))) to iItem
        If (iItem <> -1) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End
        Move (SearchArray(CS_In, TokenizerData.asLineTokens, Desktop, RefFunc(DFSTRICMP))) to iItem
        If (iItem = -1) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End
        /* THE REPLACE COMMAND'S OWN "in" IS NOT AN OPERATOR - and only ITS "in" (Nils 2026-09-13).
           A "With" anywhere on the line used to bail the whole line. Right for
           'If (x) Replace "," in s with "."', where the one IN belongs to the command; wrong for
           'If "," IN temp Replace "," IN temp WITH "."', where the FIRST IN is a legacy condition
           standing in front of the command. Twenty-eight of those in one report view, and each
           converter waited for the other: this one bailed on the With, the Replace converter bails
           on a command that does not follow ')' or ']' - the board offered the Run, the Run declined,
           every Check. The precise test: a Replace keyword BEFORE the IN being converted means that
           IN is the command's and is left to ChangeReplaceCommandToFunction; a Replace AFTER it is
           the command riding along behind the condition, exactly as any inline statement does. Once
           the condition reads '(temp contains ",")' the Replace converter finds its ')' and finishes
           the line - the two compose, in either plan order. */
        For iCount from 0 to (iItem - 1)
            If (Lowercase(Trim(TokenizerData.asLineTokens[iCount])) = "replace") ;
                Function_Return False
        Loop
        // BOUNDARY GUARDS (Nils's LegacyApp3 run 2026-07-11, engine err 4509 x3): a ';'-continuation
        // can split an expression so that IN opens the next physical segment ('If sChar ;' /
        // '   In sSet Begin') or closes the first one. A line-by-line function only ever sees ONE
        // segment, so an operand is missing and there is nothing convertible - and the unguarded
        // [iItem - 1] / [iItem + 1] reads faulted the engine. Same when the neighbour token IS the
        // continuation ';': the real operand lives on the next line. All three shapes bail.
        Move (SizeOfArray(TokenizerData.asLineTokens)) to iSize
        If (iItem = 0 or iItem >= (iSize - 1)) ;
            Function_Return False
        // CONTEXT GATE (Nils 2026-07-25, LegacyApp wslibrary.pkg): "In" is a legal IDENTIFIER -
        // 'Function Add_Hours tDateTime In Number nHours' names a PARAMETER that - while the legacy
        // IN is an OPERATOR. Matching the bare token alone read 'Move In to Response' as an IN
        // expression, taking the COMMAND keyword as its left operand and the 'to' as its right:
        // '(to contains Move) Response'. Two things are true of every real IN and of no identifier:
        //   1. it lives in a CONDITION - it is compile-invalid inside a parenthesised expression
        //      (Error 52, verified 2026-07-13), so it can only follow If / While / Until, or sit in
        //      an 'Indicate <target> as ...' (either may be preceded by a [bracket] indicator);
        //   2. its LEFT operand is a real operand - an identifier, literal, table.field or
        //      parenthesised call - NEVER a structural keyword ('If In eq 5' reads a variable).
        // Both must hold; otherwise the token is a NAME and the line is left verbatim.
        Move False to bCondCtx
        For iCount from 0 to (iItem - 1)
            Move (Lowercase(Trim(TokenizerData.asLineTokens[iCount]))) to sToken
            If (sToken = "if" or sToken = "while" or sToken = "until" or sToken = "indicate") ;
                Move True to bCondCtx
        Loop
        If (bCondCtx = False) ;
            Function_Return False
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(TokenizerData.asLineTokens[iItem - 1]))) to sToken
        If (sToken = "if"       or sToken = "while" or sToken = "until" or sToken = "else" or ;
            sToken = "not"      or sToken = "and"   or sToken = "or"    or sToken = "in"   or ;
            sToken = "indicate" or sToken = "as"    or sToken = "move"  or sToken = "set"  or ;
            sToken = "get"      or sToken = "send"  or sToken = "to"    or sToken = "begin") ;
            Function_Return False

        Move sLine to sOrg
        Move sLine to sText
        Move TokenizerData.asLineTokens[iItem -1] to sAfter
        Move TokenizerData.asLineTokens[iItem +1] to sBefore
        If (sAfter = ";" or sBefore = ";") ;
            Function_Return False
        Decrement iItem
        Move ("(" + sBefore * CS_Contains * String(sAfter) + ")") to sExpression
        // Set the "Before" array item to the expression
        Move sExpression            to TokenizerData.asLineTokens[iItem]
        // Add expression to the expression array:
        Move (SizeOfArray(TokenizerData.aExpressions)) to iSize
        Move sExpression            to TokenizerData.aExpressions[iSize].sExpression
        Move (Length(sExpression))  to TokenizerData.aExpressions[iSize].iLength
        Move (Pos(sAfter, sOrg))    to TokenizerData.aExpressions[iSize].iStartPos
        If (TokenizerData.BooleanIndicator.AsBoolean.sCode <> "") Begin
            Move sExpression to TokenizerData.BooleanIndicator.AsBoolean.sCode
        End
        Increment iItem
        // Remove the two items we have concatenated.
        Move (RemoveFromArray(TokenizerData.asLineTokens, iItem)) to TokenizerData.asLineTokens
        Move (RemoveFromArray(TokenizerData.asLineTokens, iItem)) to TokenizerData.asLineTokens
        Move (SizeOfArray(TokenizerData.asLineTokens)) to TokenizerData.iTokenCount
        Get RebuildLineFromTokens TokenizerData.asLineTokens 0 to sText

        Set pTokenizer to TokenizerData
        If (sText <> sOrg) Begin
            Get AssembleOutputLine (Trim(sText)) TokenizerData to sLine
        End

        Function_Return (sLine <> sOrg)
    End_Function

    /* ChangeLegacyClassToModern - swap a legacy class for the modern one, driven by a parameter, so
       ONE function serves every "third-party package the runtime has since absorbed" case instead
       of needing a converter per package.

       The case that prompted it (Nils 2026-07-30): a donated 2002 package wrapping the Win32
       SysMonthCal32 control, used throughout a workspace. DataFlex now ships cMonthCalendar - the
       SAME control, maintained, already written in the modern idiom. Modernising the old package
       would mean the hardest legacy family we have (Type/ZeroType/Put/GetBuff buffers handed to
       Win32 by address, where a Struct's padding is not a Type's packing). Swapping the class makes
       all of it go away with the file.

       PARAMETER - comma-separated pairs, the class first and the rest optional:
           <OldClass>=<NewClass>            required, first
           use:<old.pkg>=<new.pkg>          rewrite the Use line
           get:<Old>=<New>                  Get/Function member rename
           send:<Old>=<New>                 Send/Procedure member rename
           set:<OldProp>=<NewProcedure>     "Set X of o to v" -> "Send <new> of o v"

       For the calendar:
           MyCalendar=cMonthCalendar,use:MyCalendar.pkg=cMonthCalendar.pkg,
           get:Current_Item=SelectedDate,set:Current_Item=SetSelectedDate

       WHAT IT WILL NOT DO, on purpose. It renames declarations and member uses; it cannot know that
       the new class's OnChange takes a second argument, or that a property became read-only. A
       member use it cannot attribute to one of THIS file's converted objects is reported rather
       than rewritten - a handle variable or a cross-file reference is exactly where a blind rename
       does its damage. A SUBCLASS of the old class is never re-based either: that changes what
       someone's overrides are overriding, the same call the grid converters make.
    */

    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces a legacy class with its modern equivalent: the object declarations, the Use line and renamed members. A parameter controls what is replaced, so one function can be used for any third-party package that is now part of DataFlex.
        The parameter starts with 'Old=New', optionally followed by 'use:a.pkg=b.pkg', 'get:Old=New', 'send:Old=New' and 'set:OldProp=NewProcedure' (a property that became a procedure).
        Anything it cannot identify is reported instead of guessed, and a subclass never gets a new base class.
        { MethodType = eOtherFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Swapped a legacy class for its modern equivalent }
        { ParamHelp = Comma-separated pairs. The FIRST is the class and is required:
        \nMyOldClass=cTheModernClass
        \nThen any of these, in any order:
        \nuse:MyOld.pkg=TheModern.pkg - rewrite the Use line,
        \nget:OldMember=NewMember - rename a Get / Function member,
        \nsend:OldMember=NewMember - rename a Send / Procedure member,
        \nset:OldProperty=NewProcedure - a property that became a procedure,
        \nso 'Set X of o to v' becomes 'Send NewProcedure of o v'.
        \nAnything it cannot attribute to a converted object is reported, not changed. }
        """ }
    Function ChangeLegacyClassToModern String[] ByRef asCode String sParameter Returns Integer
        Handle  ho
        Integer iRetval

        Get Create (RefClass(cLegacyClassSwap)) to ho
        If (ho = 0) Begin
            Error "Could not create cLegacyClassSwap object. (Function: ChangeLegacyClassToModern)"
            Function_Return 0
        End

        Get ChangeLegacyClassToModern of ho (&asCode) sParameter to iRetval
        Send Destroy of ho
        Function_Return iRetval
    End_Function

    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces the legacy comparison operators gt, ge, lt, le, eq and ne with >, >=, <, <=, = and <>.
        Operators in, for example, Find commands are not changed.
        Example: If (iA Eq iB) -> If (iA = iB). LT LE EQ NE GT GE become < <= = <> > >=
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Changed: lt, le, eq, ne, gt, ge to: <, <=, =, <>, >, >= }
        """ }
    Function ChangeLegacyOperators String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        String sExpression sOrg sOrgNS sOverStrikeLine sChar sToken sChainPat sSecondWord sTokBefore
        Boolean bFound bChanged bHasEndComment bLeadingIfNot bLeadingIf bCondChanged
        Integer iItem iPos iLeftParanthesis iRightParanthesis iToken iBodyIdx iTokenMax iSearch iNotPos iIdentEnd iIfPos iIfIdx iCmdIdx
        String[] asCommands asScopeStart asReadCmds asToks
        tTokenizer TokenizerData TDRepaired
        tOperatorsInfo OperatorInfo

        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData
        If (LineIsNotCode(&TokenizerData)) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End
        If (TokenizerData.OperatorsInfo.bIsLegacy = False) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End
        // --- 'If (<cond>) Indicate <target> as <comparison>' (Nils 2026-08-17, WinServ) -----------
        // The one shape the generic rebuild below CANNOT do: the extractor swallows '(<cond>) Indicate
        // <target> as <left>' into one fabricated token, and the rebuild emitted 'If ((select) Indicate
        // Select as DRAFT_A.STATUS = "A")' - an Indicate command inside an expression, Error 52 on 30+
        // lines. _ModernizeIfCondIndicateOps converts the operators where they live (condition + the
        // 'as' side wrapped as the '(expression)' Indicate form). When it recognises the shape it OWNS
        // the line - a "" back means nothing to convert -> unchanged, NEVER the generic path.
        Get _ModernizeIfCondIndicateOps TokenizerData (&bLeadingIf) to sExpression
        If (bLeadingIf = True) Begin
            If (sExpression <> "" and sExpression <> sLine) Begin
                Get _WrapDateComparisonConstants sExpression to sLine   // date-string operands -> Date("...") (see helper)
                Function_Return True
            End
            Function_Return False
        End
        // --- Multi-line ';'-continued expression (Nils 2026-07-14) -------------------------------
        // A parenthesised condition split across physical lines with ';' leaves each line with
        // UNBALANCED parens: the opening line 'If (a EQ x or ... or ;' has an unclosed '(', and a
        // continuation line 'b EQ y or ...) Move ...' has a ')' with no matching '('. Neither is a
        // complete expression, so the aExpressions-gated conversion below never fires (line 1 extracts
        // nothing), AND a continuation line starting '<operand> <op>' also trips the db-mode shape
        // guard just below. Paren IMBALANCE uniquely marks such a split line - a COMPLETE statement
        // (including a Find/Constrain db-mode line, whose parens balance) never has it - so every legacy
        // operator on the line is a comparison. Convert them in place and return, bypassing the
        // balanced-paren wrap machinery (a split statement cannot feed it). Guarded to lines with NO
        // string literal, so a stray ' eq ' inside a string is never touched; every existing single-line
        // test (including the trailing-';' ones) is BALANCED and so never takes this path.
        Get _RemoveEndComment TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine to sOverStrikeLine
        Move 0 to iLeftParanthesis
        Get _NumberOfParanthesis sOverStrikeLine (&iLeftParanthesis) to iRightParanthesis
        If (iLeftParanthesis <> iRightParanthesis and SizeOfArray(TokenizerData.asStringConstant) = 0) Begin
            /* EXCESS CLOSING parens on a line that STARTS with a command keyword (If / While / Until / Else)
               is a MALFORMED legacy line - a dropped '(' that old char-mode DataFlex tolerated: 'If Cust.Code
               eq sCode) ;', 'If Cust.Code eq sOther) Begin' (four such lines in one workspace, 2026-09-05).
               Converting 'eq'->'=' IN PLACE would emit 'If Cust.Code = sCode)', whose modern '=' lacks the
               required wrap while the stray ')' is still unbalanced. Until 2026-09-05 the line was left
               VERBATIM for a hand fix - and the board could then offer nothing but "Ignore all". With exactly
               ONE stray ')' the author's intent is not in doubt: drop it and convert the line as the balanced
               legacy condition it was meant to be (_WithoutStrayClosingParen finds the one that takes the
               depth below zero, on the masked line). More than one stray is hand work and stays verbatim;
               the analyzer's dry-run verdict then names the imbalance (cModAnalyzer.VerifyAutoFix). A genuine
               multi-line ';'-CONTINUATION line with excess ')' is a bare condition tail and never starts with
               a command keyword, so the split case below still runs. */
            If (iRightParanthesis > iLeftParanthesis and SizeOfArray(TokenizerData.asLineTokens) > 0) Begin
                Move (Lowercase(TokenizerData.asLineTokens[0])) to sToken
                If (sToken = "if" or sToken = "while" or sToken = "until" or sToken = "else") Begin
                    If (iRightParanthesis - iLeftParanthesis = 1) Begin
                        Get _WithoutStrayClosingParen sLine to sOrgNS
                        If (sOrgNS <> "" and sOrgNS <> sLine) Begin
                            // The repaired line is balanced, so the call below takes the ordinary path. It
                            // reads pTokenizer, which Tokenizer sets; the original's tokens go back if it declines.
                            Get Tokenizer sOrgNS to TDRepaired
                            Get ChangeLegacyOperators (&sOrgNS) to bChanged
                            If (bChanged = True) Begin
                                Move sOrgNS to sLine
                                Function_Return True
                            End
                            Set pTokenizer to TokenizerData
                        End
                    End
                    Function_Return False
                End
            End
            Move sLine to sOrg
            Get ReplaceAllLegacyOperators sLine to sOrgNS
            If (sOrg <> sOrgNS) Begin
                Move sOrgNS to sLine
                Get _WrapDateComparisonConstants sLine to sLine   // date-string operands -> Date("...") (see helper)
                Function_Return True
            End
            Function_Return False
        End
        // SHAPE guard (Nils 2026-07-13): a database navigation command whose relational MODE is its
        // SECOND word - 'Constrained_Clear LE Anst By index.1', 'Constrained_Find GE ...',
        // 'Find GT Customer BY ...'. A VALID statement can NEVER start with '<operand> <relational-op>'
        // (comparisons live inside If/While/Move/parens, where the second word is an operand, not an
        // operator), so if word 2 IS a legacy relational operator the first word is a COMMAND and the
        // operator is its MODE -> leave the whole line untouched. Robust across the entire Find /
        // Constrained_* family without an exhaustive keyword list; the substring check below only caught
        // names CONTAINING 'Find', so 'Constrained_Find' bailed but 'Constrained_Clear' was wrapped into
        // the illegal '(Constrained_Clear <= Anst)' expression.
        Get _NthWord TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine 2 to sSecondWord
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sSecondWord))) to sSecondWord
        If (sSecondWord = "gt" or sSecondWord = "ge" or sSecondWord = "lt" or sSecondWord = "le" or ;
            sSecondWord = "eq" or sSecondWord = "ne") Begin
            Function_Return False
        End
        // MESSAGE-ARGUMENT guard (Nils 2026-07-24): on a Send/Get dispatch line a legacy relational
        // word OUTSIDE all parens is an ARGUMENT - typically the Integer find-MODE of a user-defined
        // message ('Send AddConstrain of hSqlConstrain "MACHINES.MNO" GE from_mnr', against
        // 'Procedure AddConstrain String sField Integer mode String sConstrain') - never a
        // comparison: DataFlex only allows an expression in argument position inside parens. The
        // tokenizer FABRICATES parens around the shape ('"X" GE var' arrives as '("X" GE var)'), so
        // without this bail the rebuild below collapses three arguments into ONE boolean
        // ('("MACHINES.MNO" >= from_mnr)') - err 14 at compile time when an operand is numeric, and
        // silently broken dispatch for the rest (LegacyApp avtalsfakt.vw: 33 constraint lines).
        // A Send/Get whose operators all sit inside REAL source parens still converts as before.
        Get _NthWord TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine 1 to sToken
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sToken))) to sToken
        If (sToken = "send" or sToken = "get") Begin
            Get _LegacyOpAtParenDepthZero sOverStrikeLine to bFound
            If (bFound = True) ;
                Function_Return False
        End
        // GT/GE/LT/LE/EQ are ALSO the relational MODES of a database Find/Read/Constrain, so on such a
        // line the keyword after the table.field is a MODE, not a comparison operator, and must NOT be
        // converted. But an 'If <condition> <db-command> ...' line (Nils 2026-07-13:
        // 'If iTexttyp eq 0 Constrain Term2.Typ eq 0') has a real boolean CONDITION before the command
        // whose operators SHOULD modernise. The tokenizer already separates the two: it fabricates the
        // condition's comparison into a parenthesised token ('(iTexttyp eq 0)') while the command's mode
        // stays a BARE token ('eq'). So we convert operators PER-TOKEN on the tokens BEFORE the command -
        // the parenthesised condition token has ' eq ' (spaces) and converts; the bare mode token is just
        // 'eq' (no surrounding spaces) which ReplaceAllLegacyOperators cannot match, so it stays legacy.
        // A bare db statement (no leading If) has no such condition -> left untouched, as before.
        Move CS_Find        to asReadCmds[0]
        Move "Request_Read" to asReadCmds[1]
        Move "Read"         to asReadCmds[2]
        Move "ReRead"       to asReadCmds[3]
        Move "FindByRecnum" to asReadCmds[4]
        Move CS_Constrain   to asReadCmds[5]
        /* VConstrain - a DataFlex command too (DataFlex.lng: VConstrain {file#} {field#} {mode} {value}),
           the by-NUMBER twin of Constrain, and its mode word is likewise never a comparison. Nils
           2026-08-17, WinServ GeneralDBFunctions.pkg: 'VConstrain iFile iField EQ vValue' was wrapped
           into 'VConstrain iFile (iField = vValue)' = E 4345 "#$, ARGUMENT REQUIRED", twice - the error
           count went UP after the run. Listed AFTER Constrain so the 0..4 masked-line probe below is
           unchanged; the token search covers every entry. */
        Move "VConstrain"   to asReadCmds[6]
        // Earliest db-command TOKEN index (whole word, case-insensitive).
        Move -1 to iCmdIdx
        For iToken from 0 to (SizeOfArray(asReadCmds) - 1)
            Move (SearchArray(asReadCmds[iToken], TokenizerData.asLineTokens, Desktop, RefFunc(DFSTRICMP))) to iSearch
            If (iSearch <> -1 and (iCmdIdx = -1 or iSearch < iCmdIdx)) ;
                Move iSearch to iCmdIdx
        Loop
        // A Find/Read the tokenizer keeps OUT of asLineTokens (its "Send <msg> of <obj>" handling extracts
        // an of-expression, e.g. "If (Found) Send REQUEST_READ of hoServer > hFile"): detect on the masked
        // CODE line so it still bails - that shape cannot be split cleanly (Nils 2026-07-07 mangle repro).
        Move 0 to iItem
        For iToken from 0 to 4
            If (iItem = 0) ;
                Get _FindKeywordPos TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine asReadCmds[iToken] True 1 to iItem
        Loop
        If (iItem <> 0 and iCmdIdx = -1) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End
        // A leading 'If' (one 'Else' allowed) whose condition we can modernise while the command mode stays.
        Move False to bLeadingIf
        If (SizeOfArray(TokenizerData.asLineTokens) > 0) Begin
            If (Lowercase(TokenizerData.asLineTokens[0]) = "if") ;
                Move True to bLeadingIf
            Else If (Lowercase(TokenizerData.asLineTokens[0]) = "else" and SizeOfArray(TokenizerData.asLineTokens) > 1 and Lowercase(TokenizerData.asLineTokens[1]) = "if") ;
                Move True to bLeadingIf
        End
        // A db-command line with NO leading If is a bare statement - leave the whole line untouched.
        If (iCmdIdx <> -1 and bLeadingIf = False) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End
        Get HasLegacyOperator TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine to bFound
        If (bFound = False) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End

        Move (Ltrim(sLine)) to sOrg
        If (iCmdIdx <> -1) Begin
            // 'If <cond> <db-command> ...': convert operators in the CONDITION tokens (before the command)
            // only, then rebuild - the command's bare mode token is never a ' op ' with surrounding spaces.
            Move TokenizerData.asLineTokens to asToks
            Move False to bCondChanged
            For iToken from 0 to (iCmdIdx - 1)
                Move asToks[iToken] to sTokBefore
                Get ReplaceAllLegacyOperators asToks[iToken] to asToks[iToken]
                If (asToks[iToken] <> sTokBefore) ;
                    Move True to bCondChanged
            Loop
            // Nothing to modernise in the CONDITION -> return WITHOUT rebuilding. RebuildLineFromTokens
            // would materialise the tokenizer's FABRICATED comparison token into the db-message ARGS -
            // 'if lhDDO send request_read to lhDDO GT file# ord#' has GT as the Request_Read find MODE
            // (Procedure Request_Read Integer eFindMode ...), which the tokenizer fabricates into
            // '(lhDDO GT file#)'; the rebuild then wrongly wrapped it '... to (lhDDO GT file#) ord#'.
            // A real condition operator (bCondChanged) still rebuilds as before. (Nils 2026-07-15.)
            If (bCondChanged = False) ;
                Function_Return False
            Get RebuildLineFromTokens asToks 0 to sOrgNS
        End
        Else Begin
            Get RebuildLineFromTokens TokenizerData.asLineTokens 0 to sOrgNS
            If (SizeOfArray(TokenizerData.aExpressions) <> 0) Begin
                Get ReplaceAllLegacyOperators sOrgNS to sOrgNS
            End
        End

        // A compound If chain (e.g. "If (a = b) If c Eq 5 Begin") causes the expression
        // extractor to include the secondary "If" keyword inside the extracted expression.
        // "If" is illegal inside an expression and must be replaced with "and".
        // The pattern ") If " (closing paren + If) uniquely identifies a secondary "If"
        // inside an expression; "Else If" and the leading "If" command are not affected.
        //
        // GUARD (Nils 2026-07-13): when the FIRST condition of the chain is a bare legacy
        // 'If not (X)' - the 'not' sits OUTSIDE the parens, not yet the modern '(Not(X))' - this
        // ') If ' -> ') and ' combine plus the outer-paren wrap below (which only fires when the
        // first condition starts with '(') produce the INVALID 'If not (X) and (Y)' (no wrap, bare
        // not): e.g. 'If not (MORE_ROWS) If (Orders.Emp_Nr EQ Emp.Emp_Nr) Goto NextOrder'. Skip the
        // combine for that shape - the nested Ifs stay (operators still modernised = valid legacy).
        // ChangeIfNotCommandToExpression, which sorts BEFORE this, wraps the not to '(Not(X))' when
        // co-selected, and the combine then runs correctly on the wrapped form (both unit-verified).
        Move 0 to iIfIdx
        If (SizeOfArray(TokenizerData.asLineTokens) > 0 and Lowercase(TokenizerData.asLineTokens[0]) = "else") ;
            Move 1 to iIfIdx
        Move False to bLeadingIfNot
        If (SizeOfArray(TokenizerData.asLineTokens) > iIfIdx + 1) Begin
            If (Lowercase(TokenizerData.asLineTokens[iIfIdx]) = "if" and Lowercase(TokenizerData.asLineTokens[iIfIdx + 1]) = "not") ;
                Move True to bLeadingIfNot
        End
        // Case-INSENSITIVE (Nils 2026-07-13, real find 'if (frvflex_0_tid) IF w_at_ordinarie& eq "X"
        // move ...'): the secondary keyword may be 'IF'/'if'/'If' in legacy source. A case-sensitive
        // Replaces(') If ') missed ') IF ' and left the illegal 'IF' COMMAND inside the expression.
        // Splice ') and ' at every ') if ' (located on a lowercased copy - positions align); ') and '
        // holds no ') if ', so the loop terminates and it catches all Ifs of a multi-If chain.
        If (bLeadingIfNot = False) Begin
            Move (Lowercase(") " + CS_If + CS_S)) to sChainPat
            Move (Pos(sChainPat, Lowercase(sOrgNS))) to iPos
            While (iPos > 0)
                Move (Left(sOrgNS, iPos - 1) + ") and " + Mid(sOrgNS, Length(sOrgNS), iPos + Length(sChainPat))) to sOrgNS
                Move (Pos(sChainPat, Lowercase(sOrgNS))) to iPos
            Loop
        End

        // The compiler rejects `If (a) and (b) Begin` and requires an outer pair of parens:
        // `If ((a) and (b)) Begin`. Walk the parenthesised sub-expressions joined by " and "
        // / " or " starting at the "If " keyword; if we found at least one such connector,
        // wrap the whole chain with outer parens.
        // Locate the leading "If " CASE-INSENSITIVELY (Nils 2026-07-13, real find
        // 'if summa_frv_komp gt 0 if balance.aterf1 gt 0 begin'): a lowercase 'if' made the old
        // case-sensitive Pos("If ") return 0, so the wrap was SKIPPED and the bare 'and' at command
        // level failed to compile ('if (a) and (b) begin' -> Error 4298 "Command not found AND").
        // The positions align with the real line, and the connector checks below already Lowercase.
        Move (Pos(Lowercase(CS_If + CS_S), Lowercase(sOrgNS))) to iPos
        If (iPos > 0) Begin
            Move (iPos + Length(CS_If) + 1) to iLeftParanthesis
            If (Mid(sOrgNS, 1, iLeftParanthesis) = "(") Begin
                Move iLeftParanthesis to iPos
                Move 0 to iRightParanthesis
                Move False to bFound
                Move False to bChanged
                While (bChanged = False)
                    Move (Mid(sOrgNS, 1, iPos)) to sChar
                    If (sChar <> "(") Begin
                        Move True to bChanged
                    End
                    Else Begin
                        Get _FindMatchingParentheses sOrgNS iPos to iRightParanthesis
                        If (Lowercase(Mid(sOrgNS, 6, iRightParanthesis + 1)) = " and (") Begin
                            Move True to bFound
                            Move (iRightParanthesis + 6) to iPos
                        End
                        Else If (Lowercase(Mid(sOrgNS, 5, iRightParanthesis + 1)) = " or (") Begin
                            Move True to bFound
                            Move (iRightParanthesis + 5) to iPos
                        End
                        Else If (Lowercase(Mid(sOrgNS, 5, iRightParanthesis + 1)) = " and " ;
                              or Lowercase(Mid(sOrgNS, 4, iRightParanthesis + 1)) = " or ") Begin
                            // Compound chain whose second sub-expression is NOT parenthesised
                            // (e.g. `If (X) and Not iType Set ...`). Use asLineTokens — still
                            // unchanged here — to locate the body command, then wrap up to the
                            // position just before that command.
                            // Note: iTokenMax / iSearch are kept SEPARATE from iItem because the
                            // For loop's `to` bound is re-evaluated each iteration, so reusing
                            // the bound variable for SearchArray results would terminate the loop
                            // prematurely the first time the result is -1.
                            Get pasCommands   to asCommands
                            Get pasScopeStart to asScopeStart
                            Move -1 to iBodyIdx
                            Move (SizeOfArray(TokenizerData.asLineTokens)) to iTokenMax
                            Decrement iTokenMax
                            For iToken from 1 to iTokenMax
                                Move TokenizerData.asLineTokens[iToken] to sToken
                                If (Lowercase(sToken) <> Lowercase(CS_If)) Begin
                                    Move (SearchArray(sToken, asCommands, Desktop, RefFunc(DFSTRICMP))) to iSearch
                                    If (iSearch = -1) Begin
                                        Move (SearchArray(sToken, asScopeStart, Desktop, RefFunc(DFSTRICMP))) to iSearch
                                    End
                                    If (iSearch <> -1) Begin
                                        Move iToken to iBodyIdx
                                        Move (iTokenMax + 1) to iToken   // jump out of For loop
                                    End
                                End
                            Loop
                            If (iBodyIdx > 0) Begin
                                Move TokenizerData.asLineTokens[iBodyIdx] to sToken
                                Move (Pos(CS_S + sToken + CS_S, sOrgNS)) to iItem
                                If (iItem = 0) Begin
                                    Move (Pos(CS_S + sToken, sOrgNS)) to iItem
                                End
                                If (iItem > iRightParanthesis) Begin
                                    // First, replace any remaining inner ` If ` with ` and ` in
                                    // the boolean range. The earlier `) If ` -> `) and ` Replaces
                                    // at line 1107 only catches Ifs that follow a `)`. A third
                                    // compound If (DataFlex allows up to 3 on one line) preceded
                                    // by a bare token, e.g. `If (a) If b If c body`, leaves the
                                    // third ` If ` in place — and the compiler rejects ` If ` once
                                    // we wrap the whole thing in outer parens. Case-insensitive so
                                    // " If "/" if "/" IF " all qualify.
                                    Move iLeftParanthesis to iIfPos
                                    Repeat
                                        Move 0 to iSearch
                                        If (iIfPos < iItem) Begin
                                            Move (Pos(" if ", Lowercase(Mid(sOrgNS, iItem - iIfPos, iIfPos)))) to iSearch
                                        End
                                        If (iSearch > 0) Begin
                                            Move (iIfPos + iSearch - 1) to iIfPos
                                            Move (Left(sOrgNS, iIfPos - 1) + " and " + Mid(sOrgNS, CI_EOL, iIfPos + 4)) to sOrgNS
                                            Increment iItem   // body shifted by the inserted char
                                            Move (iIfPos + 5) to iIfPos
                                        End
                                    Until (iSearch = 0)

                                    // Then transform `not <ident>` -> `not(<ident>)` in the boolean
                                    // range. DataFlex accepts `If Not X Begin` standalone, but
                                    // inside a parenthesised compound (`If ((Y) and Not X) Begin`)
                                    // the command-style `Not X` is rejected. Case-insensitive so
                                    // both " Not " and " not " are caught; the original case is
                                    // preserved in the output (just `(` and `)` inserted).
                                    Move iLeftParanthesis to iNotPos
                                    Repeat
                                        Move 0 to iSearch
                                        If (iNotPos < iItem) Begin
                                            Move (Pos(" not ", Lowercase(Mid(sOrgNS, iItem - iNotPos, iNotPos)))) to iSearch
                                        End
                                        If (iSearch > 0) Begin
                                            Move (iNotPos + iSearch - 1) to iNotPos
                                            If (Mid(sOrgNS, 1, iNotPos + 5) <> "(") Begin
                                                Move (iNotPos + 5) to iIdentEnd
                                                While (iIdentEnd <= Length(sOrgNS) and Mid(sOrgNS, 1, iIdentEnd) <> CS_S)
                                                    Increment iIdentEnd
                                                Loop
                                                Move (Left(sOrgNS, iNotPos + 3) + "(" + ;
                                                      Mid(sOrgNS, iIdentEnd - iNotPos - 5, iNotPos + 5) + ")" + ;
                                                      Mid(sOrgNS, CI_EOL, iIdentEnd)) to sOrgNS
                                                Increment iItem   // body shifted by the inserted ')'
                                                Move (iIdentEnd + 1) to iNotPos
                                            End
                                            Else Begin
                                                Move (iNotPos + 5) to iNotPos
                                            End
                                        End
                                    Until (iSearch = 0)

                                    Move (iItem - 1) to iRightParanthesis   // last char of boolean expr
                                    Move True to bFound
                                End
                            End
                            Move True to bChanged
                        End
                        Else Begin
                            Move True to bChanged
                        End
                    End
                Loop
                If (bFound = True) Begin
                    Move (Left(sOrgNS, iLeftParanthesis - 1) + ;
                          "(" + ;
                          Mid(sOrgNS, iRightParanthesis - iLeftParanthesis + 1, iLeftParanthesis) + ;
                          ")" + ;
                          Mid(sOrgNS, CI_EOL, iRightParanthesis + 1)) to sOrgNS
                End
            End
        End

        Move (TokenizerData.sIndentation + String(sOrgNS)) to sOrgNS
        If (sOrg <> sOrgNS) Begin
            Move sOrgNS to sLine
            Get _WrapDateComparisonConstants sLine to sLine   // date-string operands -> Date("...") (see helper)
            If (TokenizerData.sEndComment <> "" and not(sLine contains TokenizerData.sEndComment)) Begin
                Append sLine CS_S TokenizerData.sEndComment
            End
        End
        Function_Return (sOrg <> sOrgNS)
    End_Function

    /* A keyword-led legacy condition with ONE stray ')' and no '(' to match it - "If Cust.Code eq sCode) ;",
       "If Cust.Code eq sOther) Begin": a dropped '(' the old char-mode compiler tolerated (four such lines
       in one workspace, 2026-09-05). The stray is the first ')' that takes the paren depth below zero, found
       on the masked line (_MaskedCodeLine keeps positions, so a ')' inside a string or a comment is never
       the one). Returns the line without it, "" when no such ')' exists. */
    Function _WithoutStrayClosingParen String sLine Returns String
        String  sMasked sChar
        Integer i iLen iDepth

        Get _MaskedCodeLine sLine to sMasked
        Move (Length(sMasked)) to iLen
        Move 0 to iDepth
        For i from 1 to iLen
            Move (Mid(sMasked, 1, i)) to sChar
            If (sChar = "(") Begin
                Increment iDepth
            End
            Else If (sChar = ")") Begin
                Decrement iDepth
                If (iDepth < 0) ;
                    Function_Return (Left(sLine, (i - 1)) + Right(sLine, (Length(sLine) - i)))
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return ""
    End_Function

    /* PURE. The line with every string literal's content overstruck by '_' (quotes kept) and the
       end comment cut off - the SAME LENGTH as the real line up to that cut, so a position found on
       the masked line is the position on the real one. The tokenizer's own sOverstrikeLine is not
       built for that (it is re-spaced and trimmed along the way - the first cut of the repair below
       spliced the line three characters off). Block comments are not handled: LineIsNotCode keeps
       comment lines out before this is asked. */
    Function _MaskedCodeLine String sLine Returns String
        String  sOut sChar sQuote
        Integer i iLen

        Move "" to sOut
        Move "" to sQuote
        Move (Length(sLine)) to iLen
        For i from 1 to iLen
            Move (Mid(sLine, 1, i)) to sChar
            If (sQuote <> "") Begin
                If (sChar = sQuote) Begin
                    Move "" to sQuote
                    Append sOut sChar
                End
                Else ;
                    Append sOut "_"
            End
            Else If (sChar = '"' or sChar = "'") Begin
                Move sChar to sQuote
                Append sOut sChar
            End
            Else If (sChar = "/" and Mid(sLine, 1, (i + 1)) = "/") Begin
                Move iLen to i
            End
            Else ;
                Append sOut sChar
        Loop
        Function_Return sOut
    End_Function

    /* PURE. Where a command was swallowed into a condition, on the MASKED code line (strings
       overstruck, end comment removed - positions align with the real line, see _MaskedCodeLine):

           If (<a> <op> <b> [and|or <a> <op> <b>]... <Word> ...)
                                                   ^ returned (1-based), 0 when the line is not this shape

       The shape, exactly: a leading If / Else If / While / Until; then ONE group that closes at the
       very end of the code; inside it a FLAT chain of comparisons (an operand, a comparison operator,
       an operand, joined by and / or / iand / ior), and after the last operand a bare word that starts
       like an identifier and is not an operator. Everything else returns 0: a group that closes
       early ('If (a = b) Begin'), no comparison ('If (bFlag) Foo'), an operator after the operand
       ('If (a = b + 1)', 'If (a = b max c)'), a nested chain ('If ((a = b) and (c = d))'). A valid
       line never has a bare identifier after a complete comparison inside parentheses, so the shape
       is the compile error's own (52 "(operator) <WORD>"). iOpen / iClose come back as the group's
       parentheses so the caller can rebuild the line at the same positions. */
    Function _SwallowedCommandSplitPos String sMasked Integer ByRef iOpen Integer ByRef iClose Returns Integer
        String   sLow sChar sTok
        String[] asTok asCompare asConnect asNever
        Integer[] aiPos
        Integer  iLen iPos iKeyEnd iDepth i iState iTokStart iTok iLast

        Move 0 to iOpen
        Move 0 to iClose
        Move (Lowercase(sMasked)) to sLow
        Move (Length(sLow)) to iLen
        // the keyword
        Move 1 to iPos
        While (iPos <= iLen and Mid(sLow, 1, iPos) = " ")
            Increment iPos
        Loop
        Move 0 to iKeyEnd
        If (Mid(sLow, 8, iPos) = "else if ") ;
            Move (iPos + 7) to iKeyEnd
        Else If (Mid(sLow, 3, iPos) = "if ") ;
            Move (iPos + 2) to iKeyEnd
        Else If (Mid(sLow, 6, iPos) = "while ") ;
            Move (iPos + 5) to iKeyEnd
        Else If (Mid(sLow, 6, iPos) = "until ") ;
            Move (iPos + 5) to iKeyEnd
        If (iKeyEnd = 0) ;
            Function_Return 0
        // one outer group, and nothing after its closer
        Move iKeyEnd to iPos
        While (iPos <= iLen and Mid(sLow, 1, iPos) = " ")
            Increment iPos
        Loop
        If (Mid(sLow, 1, iPos) <> "(") ;
            Function_Return 0
        Move iPos to iOpen
        Get _FindMatchingParentheses sMasked iOpen to iClose
        If (iClose <= iOpen) ;
            Function_Return 0
        If (Trim(Mid(sLow, (iLen - iClose), (iClose + 1))) <> "") ;
            Function_Return 0
        // the group's depth-0 tokens, with their positions
        Move 0 to iDepth
        Move 0 to iTokStart
        For i from (iOpen + 1) to (iClose - 1)
            Move (Mid(sLow, 1, i)) to sChar
            If (sChar = " " and iDepth = 0) Begin
                If (iTokStart > 0) Begin
                    Move (Mid(sLow, (i - iTokStart), iTokStart)) to asTok[SizeOfArray(asTok)]
                    Move iTokStart to aiPos[SizeOfArray(aiPos)]
                    Move 0 to iTokStart
                End
            End
            Else Begin
                If (iTokStart = 0) ;
                    Move i to iTokStart
                If (sChar = "(") ;
                    Increment iDepth
                Else If (sChar = ")") ;
                    Decrement iDepth
            End
        Loop
        If (iTokStart > 0) Begin
            Move (Mid(sLow, (iClose - iTokStart), iTokStart)) to asTok[SizeOfArray(asTok)]
            Move iTokStart to aiPos[SizeOfArray(aiPos)]
        End
        Move (StrSplitToArray("=|<>|<|>|<=|>=|eq|ne|gt|ge|lt|le", CS_D)) to asCompare
        Move (StrSplitToArray("and|or|iand|ior", CS_D)) to asConnect
        // words that can legally follow an operand - never a command
        Move (StrSplitToArray("+|-|*|/|^|max|min|contains|matches|not|.|&", CS_D)) to asNever
        // 0 = expect left operand, 1 = expect comparison, 2 = expect right operand, 3 = comparison complete
        Move 0 to iState
        Move (SizeOfArray(asTok) - 1) to iLast
        For iTok from 0 to iLast
            Move asTok[iTok] to sTok
            Case Begin
                Case (iState = 0)
                    If (sTok <> "not") ;
                        Move 1 to iState
                    Case Break
                Case (iState = 1)
                    If (SearchArray(sTok, asCompare) = -1) ;
                        Function_Return 0
                    Move 2 to iState
                    Case Break
                Case (iState = 2)
                    Move 3 to iState
                    Case Break
                Case (iState = 3)
                    If (SearchArray(sTok, asConnect) <> -1) Begin
                        Move 0 to iState
                    End
                    Else Begin
                        If (SearchArray(sTok, asCompare) <> -1 or SearchArray(sTok, asNever) <> -1) ;
                            Function_Return 0
                        Move (Left(sTok, 1)) to sChar
                        If ((sChar >= "a" and sChar <= "z") or sChar = "_") ;
                            Function_Return aiPos[iTok]
                        Function_Return 0
                    End
                    Case Break
            Case End
        Loop
        Function_Return 0
    End_Function

    /* THE COMMAND SWALLOWED INTO A CONDITION (a workspace, 2026-09-05: one report file, six lines).

       A legacy one-line statement 'If Cust.Flag EQ "1" MyWritePos "*" 18.7 (FONT_DEFAULT)' was
       modernised as 'If (Cust.Flag = "1" MyWritePos "*" 18.7 (FONT_DEFAULT))' - the command inside
       the parentheses - which DataFlex 26 rejects with error 52 "(operator) MYWRITEPOS". The word the
       compiler names is exactly where the expression should have ended. The converter defect behind it
       is fixed (the operator-list seeding); this function is the repair for the lines it left behind
       and the second line of defence for the same wrap from any other source - the third such case since
       August (Indicate 08-17, Character 08-18, a user #COMMAND 09-05: every time a word no keyword list
       knew).

       Line-by-line, by SHAPE (see _SwallowedCommandSplitPos above): it moves the closing parenthesis
       to just after the last operand of the flat comparison chain and puts the command back after it.
       The analyzer offers it as the Auto-fix for error 52 "(operator)" and dry-runs it against the
       warned line (VerifyAutoFix): a 52 that is not this shape - a stray operator, 'Get Func() to var' -
       stays Explain with the general advice. Spacing inside the group is kept as it stands; the end
       comment, if any, stays where it was. */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Moves a command out of a condition that it was placed in by mistake: 'If (a = b Command args)' becomes 'If (a = b) Command args'.
        This causes compile error 52, "(operator) <COMMAND>", when a legacy one-line 'If a EQ b Command' was converted with the command inside the parentheses.
        Only a line that starts with If, Else If, While or Until is changed, and only when its parentheses close at the end of the line and contain a simple comparison followed by a single word. All other lines are not changed.
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Split a command back out of a wrapped condition (compile error 52) }
        { ModRecipe = 52 | operator | AutoFix }
        """ }
    Function SplitCommandOutOfCondition String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        tTokenizer TokenizerData
        String  sMasked sHead sCmd sTail
        Integer iSplit iOpen iClose

        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData
        If (LineIsNotCode(&TokenizerData)) ;
            Function_Return False
        Get _MaskedCodeLine sLine to sMasked
        Get _SwallowedCommandSplitPos sMasked (&iOpen) (&iClose) to iSplit
        If (iSplit = 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        // The masked line keeps every position of the real one, so the cut points carry over.
        Move (RTrim(Left(sLine, (iSplit - 1)))) to sHead
        Move (Trim(Mid(sLine, (iClose - iSplit), iSplit))) to sCmd
        Move (Mid(sLine, (Length(sLine) - iClose), (iClose + 1))) to sTail
        Move (sHead + ") " + sCmd + sTail) to sLine
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces Shadow_State and Object_Shadow_State with Enabled_State.
        Example: Set Shadow_State [of oObject] to True -> Set Enabled_State [of oObject] to False, and Set Object_Shadow_State [of oObject] to True -> Set Enabled_State [of oObject] to False
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Changed: Shadow_State to Enabled_State }
        """ }
    Function ChangeLegacyShadow_State String ByRef sLine Returns Boolean
        String sToState sText sLineLC sEndComment sSpaces
        Boolean bFound
        Integer iPos
        tTokenizer TokenizerData

        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData
        If (LineIsNotCode(&TokenizerData)) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End

        Get IsKeywordInLine CS_Object_Shadow_State TokenizerData.asLineTokens to bFound
        If (bFound = False) Begin
            Get IsKeywordInLine CS_Shadow_State TokenizerData.asLineTokens to bFound
        End
        If (bFound = False) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End

        If (TokenizerData.sEndComment <> "") Begin
            Get EndComment sLine to sEndComment
            Move (Replace(sEndComment, sLine, "")) to sLine
            Move (Rtrim(sLine)) to sText
            Move (Replace(sText, sLine, "")) to sSpaces
            Move (sSpaces + String(sEndComment)) to sEndComment
            Move (Rtrim(sLine)) to sLine
        End

        Move (Lowercase(sLine)) to sLineLC
        Move (Pos(Lowercase(CS_Object_Shadow_State), sLineLC)) to iPos
        If (iPos <> 0) Begin
            Get ReplaceLineToken TokenizerData sLine CS_Object_Shadow_State CS_Enabled_State to sLine
        End
        Else Begin
            Move (Pos(Lowercase(CS_Shadow_State), sLineLC)) to iPos
            If (iPos <> 0) Begin
                Get ReplaceLineToken TokenizerData sLine CS_Shadow_State CS_Enabled_State to sLine
            End
        End
        Move (Lowercase(sLine)) to sLineLC

        // We also need to negate any "True" or "False" in the "to" part of the line
        Move TokenizerData.ToStatement.sCode to sToState
        If (Lowercase(sToState) = Lowercase(CS_True) or Lowercase(sToState) = Lowercase(CS_False)) Begin
            Get IsKeywordInLine CS_True TokenizerData.asLineTokens to bFound
            If (bFound = True) Begin
                Move (Pos(Lowercase(CS_True), sLineLC)) to iPos
                If (iPos <> 0) Begin
                    Get ReplaceLineToken TokenizerData sLine CS_True CS_False to sLine
                End
            End
            Else Begin
                Get IsKeywordInLine CS_False TokenizerData.asLineTokens to bFound
                Move (Pos(Lowercase(CS_False), sLineLC)) to iPos
                If (iPos <> 0) Begin
                    Get ReplaceLineToken TokenizerData sLine CS_False CS_True to sLine
                End
            End
        End
        // Or any self defined boolean variable at end of line:
        Else Begin
            Move (Pos(Lowercase(CS_Not), Lowercase(sToState))) to iPos
            // Add "not" keyword
            If (iPos = 0) Begin
                Move (Replace(sToState, sLine, ("(" + CS_Not + "(" + sToState + "))"))) to sLine
            End
            // Remove "not" keyword and its parantheses. Note that it can contain spaces.
            Else Begin
                Move (Replace("(", sToState, "")) to sText
                Move (Replace(Lowercase(CS_Not), sText, "")) to sText
                Move (Pos(Lowercase(CS_Not), Lowercase(sToState))) to iPos
                Move (Overstrike("   ", sToState, iPos)) to sToState
                Move (Replaces("(", sToState, "")) to sToState
                Move (Replaces(")", sToState, "")) to sToState
                Move (Trim(sToState))              to sToState
                Move (Pos(Lowercase(CS_S + CS_To + CS_S), sLineLC)) to iPos
                If (iPos <> 0) Begin
                    Move (Left(sLine, (iPos -1))) to sLine
                    Append sLine CS_S CS_To CS_S sToState
                End
            End
        End
        Move (Rtrim(sLine)) to sLine
        If (sEndComment <> "") Begin
            Append sLine sEndComment
        End
        Else If (TokenizerData.sEndComment <> "") Begin
            Append sLine CS_S TokenizerData.sEndComment
        End

        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces a read of a legacy Workspace path with a read of the modern cWorkspace property.
        Example: Get CurrentProgramPath of ghoWorkspace to sPath --> Get psProgramPath of (phoWorkspace(ghoApplication)) to sPath.
        It covers all the paths of the obsolete Workspc.pkg (Data, Program, Bitmap, Help, AppSrc, DdSrc, SystemDf, Df and FileList). A name without a modern equivalent, such as CurrentModuleName, is not changed.
        The modern properties read the opened .ws configuration, so check whether the code expects a separator at the end of a path when you review the result.
        It is used together with RefactorWorkspaceTocApplication, which converts the Workspace object itself; this function changes the reads in all files.
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4328 | GHOWORKSPACE | Suggest }
        { SummaryText = Changed: Workspace path read -> phoWorkspace(ghoApplication) }
        """ }
    Function ChangeLegacyWorkspaceAccessors String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        tTokenizer TokenizerData
        String sNew sCode sName
        Integer iSize iCount iUse

        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData
        If (TokenizerData.bLineIsReady = True or TokenizerData.bIsInCommand = True or ;
            TokenizerData.bHasEndSemiColon = True or ;
            TokenizerData.bIsInImage = True or TokenizerData.bIsVariableDeclaration = True) ;
            Function_Return False
        Move (TokenizerData.iTokenCount) to iSize
        If (iSize < 6) ;
            Function_Return False
        // The exact command shape only: 'Get <LegacyPathName> of ghoWorkspace to <dest ...>' with Get
        // in command position. The real-world corpus is exclusively this shape; an expression use
        // ('Move (CurrentProgramPath(ghoWorkspace)) to x') or any other object bails untouched.
        // The Deep Scan's rows ask the same function (DS 9253 / 9254).
        Get _LegacyWorkspaceRead sLine (&sName) (&sNew) to iUse
        If (iUse <> 1) ;
            Function_Return False
        Move (TokenizerData.sIndentation + "Get " + sNew + " of (phoWorkspace(ghoApplication)) to") to sCode
        Decrement iSize
        For iCount from 5 to iSize
            Move (sCode * TokenizerData.asLineTokens[iCount]) to sCode
        Loop
        If (TokenizerData.sEndComment <> "") ;
            Move (sCode * TokenizerData.sEndComment) to sCode
        Move (RTrim(sCode)) to sCode
        If (sCode <> sLine) Begin
            Move sCode to sLine
            Function_Return True
        End
        Function_Return False
    End_Function

    /* ChangeLegacyTypeCast - DF26 rejects a TYPE NAME used as a conversion function. The user-facing
       half of this comment - the before/after examples and the ADDRESS caveat - moved into the
       { Description } on 2026-08-23, where the generated help can reach it.

       EIGHTEEN type names are affected, each erroring 4328 "Undefined symbol in argument". Integer,
       String, Number, Date and Real remain legal conversion functions and are left alone. The first
       pass (2026-08-19) probe-compiled five and called them the set; re-probing the whole type
       vocabulary on 2026-08-30 found thirteen more. The list and the how-to-extend-it recipe live with
       the core, cRefactorFuncLib._LegacyTypeCastInLine - add nothing without compiling it first.

       THE ROW IS UNREADABLE WITHOUT THIS (Nils, WinServ): the compiler reports the type's INTERNAL name,
       so "Handle(Self)" is flagged as an undefined symbol called LONGPTR and "Pointer(x)" as ADDRESS.

       SIX ALIASES GET NO RECIPE, deliberately - ADDRESS, WORD, SHORT, CHAR, BYTE, VARIANT. Each is also
       a perfectly ordinary DB COLUMN name, so a 4328 naming one is more often a missing field than a
       cast, and a row offering a fix that cannot apply is the thing this whole family of fixes exists to
       stop. Address set the precedent (2026-08-19); the other five joined it when the type list grew on
       2026-08-30, by the same test - could a schema plausibly hold a column of this name? Those rows
       keep the generic 4328 hint, which names the cast case. THE CONVERTER STILL REWRITES THEM when it
       runs: the exclusion is about what the analyzer OFFERS, never about what the function does.

       Line-by-line, because the rewrite is purely local. The pure core - masked-line scanning, paren
       matching, re-tokenizing after each rewrite - is cRefactorFuncLib._LegacyTypeCastInLine, where the
       idempotency and declaration-vs-call reasoning is documented.

       THE TAGS SAY 'symbol: <type>' (2026-09-14): the 4328 message names the type's INTERNAL name as
       one whole symbol, and a plain match text is a substring - 'DATETIME' fired on "Undefined symbol
       in argument DWHIGHDATETIME", a bare Type member on an 'Add 10 to ...' line, and the plan offered
       a cast rewrite the run found nothing to rewrite. 'symbol: X' makes the matcher take X whole
       (cModAnalyzer.MatchTextHits); Guard F in VerifyAutoFix then keeps the row only when the type's
       SOURCE name is called on the line.
    */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces a legacy cast with a type name, which DataFlex 26 does not accept (Handle(x), Pointer(x), DWord(x) and fifteen more), with the modern Cast(x, Type) form. Integer, String, Number, Date and Real are valid casts and are not changed.
        This fixes error 4328, where the compiler names the type's internal name (LONGPTR for Handle, ADDRESS for Pointer) rather than the word in the source code.
        Examples:
        - Send exportGridToExcel (Handle(Self)) becomes Send exportGridToExcel (Cast(Self, Handle))
        - Move (Pointer(0)) to p becomes Move (Cast(0, Pointer)) to p
        Without this function the error is hard to understand: it names an undefined symbol called LONGPTR or ADDRESS, words that appear nowhere in the source code, with a hint about missing database fields and Type members.
        When error 4328 names ADDRESS, the Analyzer does not offer this function as an automatic fix, because Address is also a common database column name, so the error is more often a missing field than a Pointer() cast. If you run this function, it still changes every Pointer() cast it finds.
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4328 | symbol: LONGPTR }
        { ModRecipe = 4328 | symbol: UCHAR }
        { ModRecipe = 4328 | symbol: DATETIME }
        { ModRecipe = 4328 | symbol: BOOLEAN }
        { ModRecipe = 4328 | symbol: UBIGINT }
        { ModRecipe = 4328 | symbol: UINTEGER }
        { ModRecipe = 4328 | symbol: BIGINT }
        { ModRecipe = 4328 | symbol: ROWID }
        { ModRecipe = 4328 | symbol: TIMESPAN }
        { SummaryText = Rewrote: legacy type-name casts to Cast(value, Type) }
        """ }
    Function ChangeLegacyTypeCast String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        Integer iChanged
        tTokenizer TokenizerData

        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData
        If (TokenizerData.bLineIsReady = True or TokenizerData.bIsInImage = True) ;
            Function_Return False
        // A declaration line ("Handle hMenu") carries no call, and the core requires a "(" anyway - but
        // skipping it here keeps this in step with the other line-by-line converters.
        If (TokenizerData.bIsVariableDeclaration = True) ;
            Function_Return False
        Get _LegacyTypeCastInLine (&sLine) to iChanged
        Function_Return (iChanged > 0)
    End_Function

    /* ChangeObjectFunctionCallToGet - DF26 call-syntax modernization.
       CORRECTED 2026-08-19, by probe: DF26 has NOT withdrawn the expression form. "Name(Self, args)"
       compiles there exactly as before - verified with a method whose name DataFlex does not use. What
       breaks it is a NAME COLLISION: when the new release owns the same name (Split arrives with the
       runtime cRegEx in DF 26, so no package declares it), the call resolves to DataFlex's function and
       the argument count no longer fits - error 4379, or 4544 where the return types disagree. The Get
       command "Get Name args to dest" is a message send, so it binds to YOUR method and settles both.
       Only methods declared (without Global) in the SAME file are rewritten, which is what keeps a
       global-function call (GlobalFn(Self, x)) - where Self is a real parameter - out of scope, and
       unresolved names are left alone rather than guessed at.
       Surfaces the common source of this pattern in modernized code: legacy (db)Grid item-display /
       method bodies the grid->cCJGrid converter relocates verbatim. The tool itself compiles under DF25,
       where the expression form is still correct - this pass rewrites the OUTPUT / target code for DF26+.
       The user-facing half (the before/after examples, what only gets a //TODO, and why the collision
       matters) now lives in the { Description } - the manual is generated from that.
    */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces calls of object methods in an expression (Name(Self, args)) with the Get command (Get Name args to dest), which DataFlex 26 requires.
        The simple form 'Move (Name(Self[, simple args])) to dest' is changed automatically, for methods declared without Global in the same file. Every other use (inside a larger expression, with complex arguments, not in a Move, or where the first argument is not Self) gets a TODO comment for a manual change.
        Calls of global functions, and names that cannot be found in the file, are not changed, and neither is a Move into an indicator such as Found, because Get cannot write to an indicator.
        Examples: 'Move (FormatValue(Self, sRaw)) to sOut' becomes 'Get FormatValue sRaw to sOut', and 'Move (RowCount(Self)) to iN' becomes 'Get RowCount to iN'.
        Why this matters: the expression form compiles until a DataFlex release adds a function with the same name (for example Split, which comes with the cRegEx class in DataFlex 26). The call then uses DataFlex's own function, the number of arguments or the return type no longer match, and you get error 4379 or 4544. The Get form sends a message to the object, so it uses the workspace's method again.
        { MethodType = eOtherFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4544 }
        { ParamHelp = Optional method name(s), separated by ; or , - e.g. Split. Use it when the method is declared in ANOTHER file (an inherited or shared-library method): the compiler named the line, so the name is evidence the same-file scan cannot see. Blank = only methods declared in the file being converted. }
        { SummaryText = Converted object function calls to Get form (DF26) }
        """ }
    Function ChangeObjectFunctionCallToGet String[] ByRef asCode String sParameter Returns Integer
        String[] asOut asLocalFns asNamed
        String sLine sNew sTodo sIndent sParam
        Integer iCount iSize iChanged iAction iPos iName iNameLast
        tLineInfo[] aInfo
        Boolean bHasAny

        // Fast gate: no "(self" text anywhere -> no object-method expression call to convert.
        Get _FileContainsText of Self (&asCode) "(self" to bHasAny
        If (bHasAny = False) ;
            Function_Return 0

        // Pass 1: which Function names in THIS file are object methods (declared without Global)?
        Get _CollectLocalFunctionNames of Self (&asCode) to asLocalFns
        /* ...PLUS any name the CALLER supplied (2026-08-19). The same-file rule is a safety rule, not the
           definition of an object method: it exists so that GlobalFn(Self, x) - where Self is a genuine
           first argument - is never rewritten. But a method inherited from a base class, or declared in a
           shared library package, is called this way in files that declare nothing, and there the rule
           silently converts nothing at all. Nils's WinServ: nine identical
           "Move (Split(Self, ":-:", saData[i])) to saValues" lines across six files, error 4379, with
           Split declared once in wslibrary.pkg - the converter that exists for exactly this shape could
           not touch one of them.

           A supplied name is stronger evidence than a same-file declaration, because it comes off a line
           the COMPILER rejected. It is a parameter rather than a workspace-wide harvest so that nothing
           is guessed at: the caller (the findings row) names what it read. */
        Move (Trim(sParameter)) to sParam
        If (sParam <> "") Begin
            Move (Replaces(",", sParam, ";")) to sParam
            Move (StrSplitToArray(sParam, ";")) to asNamed
            Move (SizeOfArray(asNamed) - 1) to iNameLast
            For iName from 0 to iNameLast
                If (Trim(asNamed[iName]) <> "" and SearchArray(Trim(asNamed[iName]), asLocalFns, Desktop, RefFunc(DFSTRICMP)) = -1) ;
                    Move (Trim(asNamed[iName])) to asLocalFns[SizeOfArray(asLocalFns)]
            Loop
        End
        If (SizeOfArray(asLocalFns) = 0) ;
            Function_Return 0

        // Pass 2: rewrite the clean shape, flag the rest. Only auto-fixes are counted (a re-run drops then
        // re-emits an unchanged flag, so the count stays clean and the //TODOs never stack).
        Get ScanSourceFile (&asCode) to aInfo
        Move 0 to iChanged
        Move (SizeOfArray(asCode)) to iSize
        Decrement iSize
        For iCount from 0 to iSize
            If (Mod(iCount, 20) = 0 and (Active_State(ghoStatusPanel) = True)) Begin
                Send Update_StatusPanel of ghoStatusPanel ("Source line:" * String(iCount + 1) * "of:" * String(iSize + 1))
                Set piPosition of ghoProgressBar to (iCount + 1)
            End
            Move asCode[iCount] to sLine

            // Drop a stale flag from a prior run first (a still-unfixable call re-emits one below).
            If (Pos("//todo df26 (get form)", Lowercase(Trim(sLine))) = 1) Begin
                // dropped - do not copy through
            End
            Else Begin
                /* PER-LINE FAST GATE (2026-08-15). The whole-file gate above already knows that a
                   line without "(self" cannot be an object-method expression call - _ObjExprCallToGet
                   rewrites exactly that pattern - yet every line was tokenised regardless. Measured
                   on WinServ's VDFTranslator.pkg: 2,087 lines, 58 carry "(self", so 97% of the
                   ~2.2 s dry-run was spent on lines that could not match. That dry-run runs once per
                   (file, function) inside the analyzer's 4544 whole-file probe, and a first Check
                   on the fresh WinServ copy sat 230 s+ at 97% CPU building 463 rows because of it.
                   Same test the file gate uses, so the two cannot disagree; a line that fails it
                   copies through untouched, exactly as iAction = 0 did.
                   ...AND ONLY CODE (2026-10-29). The gate means the tokenizer sees these lines alone, so a
                   string or a block comment that one of them opened stayed open for the next, however far
                   down: an @-string opened on a "(self" line hid every later call. The file's own walk
                   (ScanSourceFile) says which lines are code, and each of those starts outside any string or
                   comment; a string's text and its closing line, a comment, a report image and a #HEADER
                   line are copied through. */
                Move 0 to iAction
                If (Pos("(self", Lowercase(sLine)) > 0 and (aInfo[iCount].eKind = LK_Code or aInfo[iCount].eKind = LK_StringOpener)) Begin
                    Set piMultiLineStringType of Self to MLS_None
                    Set pbIsInDF23MultiLineComment of Self to False
                    Get _ObjExprCallToGet of Self sLine asLocalFns (&iAction) to sNew
                End
                If (iAction = 1) Begin
                    Move sNew to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                    Increment iChanged
                End
                Else If (iAction = 2) Begin
                    Move (Pos(Trim(sLine), sLine)) to iPos
                    If (iPos > 1) ;
                        Move (Left(sLine, (iPos - 1))) to sIndent
                    Else ;
                        Move "" to sIndent
                    Move (sIndent + "//TODO DF26 (Get form): object function called in an expression - convert to 'Get ... to ...' by hand.") to sTodo
                    Move sTodo to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                    Move sLine to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                End
                Else Begin
                    Move sLine to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                End
            End
        Loop

        Move asOut to asCode
        // Per-RUN tally on top of the per-file return value: the engine calls this once per file, so a
        // caller that wants "what did this run do" (the 4379 row action) cannot use the return value.
        Set piObjCallsConverted to (piObjCallsConverted(Self) + iChanged)
        Function_Return iChanged
    End_Function

    /* DropSurplusCallArguments - the ARITHMETIC half of E 4379 (Nils 2026-09-04, a view).
       ChangeObjectFunctionCallToGet above is the cure for the other half, a name collision. On his
       line - "Entry_Item (fheadvalue(Self, plandetail.ref, plandetail.porder))" against a fHeadValue
       declaring ONE parameter - a Get rewrite would have compiled clean and dropped the second
       argument without a word (probe-compiled: a message send does not count arguments). The board
       therefore withholds the Get there and offers THIS instead, because it is the one change that is
       provably nothing at run time: 19.1 passed the surplus and the method never saw it.
       Never a {ModRecipe}: the compiler's 4379 cannot tell this shape from the collision, and a
       recipe cannot see the line (feedback_compiler_recipe_cannot_see_the_line_shape). The guided
       board offers it only after DeclaredParamCountIn measured the declaration and
       _DropSurplusArgsInLine confirmed it would change the row's own line. The core is that pure
       helper in cRefactorFuncLib; this is the file walk, the progress nudge and the tally.
    */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Removes the extra arguments at the end of an object method call, in the expression form Name(Self, args), when the call passes more arguments than the method declares.
        DataFlex 19.1 and earlier accepted the extra arguments without checking, and the method never read them, so removing them changes nothing when the program runs. DataFlex 26 counts them and reports error 4379. Only the argument list is changed; the declaration is not.
        Example: with fHeadValue declaring one parameter, 'Entry_Item (fheadvalue(Self, plandetail.ref, plandetail.porder))' becomes 'Entry_Item (fheadvalue(Self, plandetail.ref))'.
        Not changed: a call whose extra argument contains another call (which could have side effects), a call that continues over several lines, and a call whose first argument is not Self. If the method was meant to receive the extra value, add the parameter by hand and do not run this function.
        { MethodType = eOtherFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ParamHelp = Name|Count - the object method's name and the parameter count it declares, for example fHeadValue|1. A guided Check fills it in from the file's own declaration. Blank = nothing is changed. }
        { SummaryText = Dropped surplus call arguments (E 4379) }
        """ }
    Function DropSurplusCallArguments String[] ByRef asCode String sParameter Returns Integer
        String sParam sName sKeep sLine sNew
        Integer iBar iKeep iCount iSize iChanged iDropped
        Boolean bHasAny

        Move (Trim(sParameter)) to sParam
        Move (Pos("|", sParam)) to iBar
        If (iBar = 0) Begin
            If (ghoStatusLog <> 0) ;
                Send AddRunNote "DropSurplusCallArguments" "SKIPPED - needs a parameter of the form Name|Count: the object method's name and the parameter count it declares, for example fHeadValue|1. A guided Check fills it in from the file's own declaration."
            Function_Return 0
        End
        Move (Trim(Left(sParam, (iBar - 1))))               to sName
        Move (Trim(Right(sParam, (Length(sParam) - iBar)))) to sKeep
        Move sKeep to iKeep
        // A count that did not read back as itself ("x", "") would drop EVERY argument - refuse it.
        If (sName = "" or sKeep = "" or String(iKeep) <> sKeep or iKeep < 0) Begin
            If (ghoStatusLog <> 0) ;
                Send AddRunNote "DropSurplusCallArguments" ("SKIPPED - the parameter must be Name|Count with a whole-number count, as in fHeadValue|1; got '" + sParam + "'.")
            Function_Return 0
        End
        // Fast gate: the name is never called in this file -> nothing to read.
        Get _FileContainsText of Self (&asCode) (Lowercase(sName) + "(") to bHasAny
        If (bHasAny = False) ;
            Function_Return 0
        Move 0 to iChanged
        Move (SizeOfArray(asCode)) to iSize
        Decrement iSize
        For iCount from 0 to iSize
            If (Mod(iCount, 20) = 0 and (Active_State(ghoStatusPanel) = True)) Begin
                Send Update_StatusPanel of ghoStatusPanel ("Source line:" * String(iCount + 1) * "of:" * String(iSize + 1))
                Set piPosition of ghoProgressBar to (iCount + 1)
            End
            Move asCode[iCount] to sLine
            Get _DropSurplusArgsInLine of Self sLine sName iKeep (&iDropped) to sNew
            If (iDropped > 0) Begin
                Move sNew to asCode[iCount]
                Increment iChanged
            End
        Loop
        // Per-RUN tally on top of the per-file return value (the engine calls this once per file).
        Set piSurplusArgsDropped to (piSurplusArgsDropped(Self) + iChanged)
        Function_Return iChanged
    End_Function

    /* ChangeParseFileToExtractFileName - Parse_File() -> ExtractFileName().

       Both are global String->String functions; Parse_File is marked
       Visibility=Private Obsolete=True in the runtime's parsefn.pkg, and
       ExtractFileName lives in GlobalFunctionsProcedures.pkg (Nils 2026-07-29).
       (Written without the braces on purpose: a brace-tag inside a comment is still
       read by the meta-tag scanner - it cost two ModAnalyzer test failures here.)

       They agree on every real file specification - "c:\Ide\Test\AbData.pkg" gives
       "AbData.pkg" either way, and a bare name with no separator, or a drive-relative
       "c:file.txt", also agree. The modern one is a pure string operation, while
       Parse_File reaches Parse_Path, which OPENS A DIRECTORY CHANNEL to decide whether
       the last segment is a folder. So the rename also takes disk I/O out of what
       reads like string handling.

       ONE case differs and it is worth knowing: given a path whose last segment is an
       EXISTING DIRECTORY, Parse_File returns "" (its probe finds the folder and treats
       the whole spec as a path) where ExtractFileName returns that segment's name.
       Code that passed directories to Parse_File and relied on the blank was relying
       on a disk probe; that is a behaviour worth losing, but it IS a behaviour change.

       Its sibling Parse_Path -> ExtractFilePath is deliberately NOT offered - see the
       note there. */

    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Renames the obsolete global function Parse_File() to ExtractFileName().
        The result is the same for any real file name. The modern function only works on the text, while Parse_File checks the disk (through Parse_Path) to find out whether the last part is a folder.
        One difference: for a path whose last part is an existing folder, Parse_File returned an empty value, and ExtractFileName returns the folder name.
        The related Parse_Path is not converted, because ExtractFilePath does not return the same result: both the separator at the end and the drive letter are different.
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4534 | PARSE_FILE }
        { SummaryText = Renamed obsolete Parse_File() to ExtractFileName() }
        """ }
    Function ChangeParseFileToExtractFileName String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        String   sNewLine sOverFull
        String[] asFrom asTo
        tTokenizer TokenizerData

        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData
        If (LineIsNotCode(&TokenizerData)) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End

        Move "Parse_File"      to asFrom[0]
        Move "ExtractFileName" to asTo[0]

        // The masked line has to be position-aligned with sLine before the rename -
        // sOverstrikeLine is LEFT-trimmed, with the indent held separately.
        Move (TokenizerData.sIndentation + TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine) to sOverFull
        Get _RenameMaskedCalls sLine sOverFull asFrom asTo to sNewLine
        /* BOTH CALL SHAPES, or the converter finds nothing (Nils 2026-07-29). The paren form
           "(Parse_File(x))" is what _RenameMaskedCalls handles; DataFlex also allows the command
           form with no parentheses at all -

               Get Parse_File  sFilename   to sFilename

           and that is the ONLY form his workspace uses. The analyzer listed five files, the plan
           ran them, and the log said "0 lines" - a converter that quietly matches nothing while
           the run reports success. Try the command shape whenever the paren shape found nothing. */
        If (sNewLine = sLine) ;
            Get _RenameMaskedGetCall sLine sOverFull "Parse_File" "ExtractFileName" to sNewLine
        If (sNewLine = sLine) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End
        Move sNewLine to sLine
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    /* ChangeParsePathPairToExtractFilePath - the legacy three-part path split, collapsed. The
       user-facing half of this comment (the before/after block, the trailing-separator change and
       the refusal rule) moved into the { Description } on 2026-08-23.

       WHY A PAIR, never two renames: ExtractFilePath is not a drop-in for Parse_Path. Parse_Path
       strips the drive, trims the trailing separator, and probes the DISK to decide whether the
       last segment is a folder. Only with the drive put back - which is exactly what the
       Parse_Drive line beside it does - do the two agree, so the unit of conversion is the pair
       plus the place their results are rejoined.

       THE ONE BEHAVIOUR CHANGE, accepted by Nils 2026-07-29 ("trailing backslash acceptable"):
       Parse_Drive + Parse_Path gives "c:\Ide\Test", ExtractFilePath gives "c:\Ide\Test\". Four
       of his six sites display that string, so a trailing backslash becomes visible. Generating
       code to strip it again would be uglier than the idiom being replaced.

       The guard list that decides a refusal lives on cParsePathPairToFilePath.ParseFile.
    */

    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces the legacy way of splitting a path in three parts with one modern call: "Get Parse_Path x to sPath" and "Get Parse_Drive x to sDrive" become "Get ExtractFilePath x to sPath", and any "(sDrive + sPath)" becomes sPath.
        This is not a simple rename, because ExtractFilePath alone does not return the same as Parse_Path, which removes the drive, removes the separator at the end, and checks the disk.
        Before and after:
        Get Parse_Path  <spec> to <sPath>    ->   Get ExtractFilePath <spec> to <sPath>
        Get Parse_Drive <spec> to <sDrive>   ->   (line deleted)
        ... (sDrive + sPath) ...             ->   ... sPath ...
        Note: the old code returned the folder without a separator at the end, and ExtractFilePath returns it with one. Where that text is shown to the user, the extra separator is visible; no code is added to remove it.
        When the drive variable is used in a way that cannot be fully checked, nothing is changed, so a conversion is never left half done.
        { MethodType = eOtherFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4534 | PARSE_PATH }
        { ModRecipe = 4534 | PARSE_DRIVE }
        { SummaryText = Collapsed Parse_Path + Parse_Drive pairs to ExtractFilePath }
        """ }
    Function ChangeParsePathPairToExtractFilePath String[] ByRef asCode String sParameter Returns Integer
        Handle  ho
        Integer iRetval

        Get Create (RefClass(cParsePathPairToFilePath)) to ho
        If (ho = 0) Begin
            Error "Could not create cParsePathPairToFilePath object. (Function: ChangeParsePathPairToExtractFilePath)"
            Function_Return 0
        End

        Get ChangeParsePathPairToExtractFilePath of ho (&asCode) to iRetval
        Send Destroy of ho
        Function_Return iRetval
    End_Function

    /* ChangeReplaceDirectiveToDefine.

       The five measured differences between the directive and the command, and the reason an
       identifier value is left alone although it would compile, live in the block comment above
       _ReplaceDirectiveValueKind (cRefactorFuncLib), which heads the worker. In short, from a
       DataFlex 26 scratch program on 2026-09-08:

         - a compile-time '#IF <name>' reads a Define exactly as it reads a #REPLACE;
         - '#IFDEF' does NOT: it is blind to a #REPLACE and sees a Define, so converting a name the
           file tests would switch on an arm that is dead today;
         - inside an Enum_List a #REPLACE leaves the running counter alone while a Define WITH a
           value resets it, renumbering every entry below;
         - the directive takes the REST OF THE LINE, so an unquoted value with spaces has no Define
           form (error 4345);
         - a Define resolves its value AT ITS OWN LINE while the directive stays lazy text, so a
           parenthesised value naming symbols declared later is error 4328 - which is why only
           LITERALS convert;
         - a reserved word as the name is error 4330, and redefining a Define for a different value
           is error 4332 (the same value is silent).

       Never touched: the #HEADER region, a Language_*.inc, and macro code, which the engine masks
       away before this function is handed the file. */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces the legacy '#REPLACE <name> <value>' compiler directive with the modern 'Define <name> for <value>'. The indentation, the comment at the end of the line, and the column of the value are kept, so a block of aligned constants stays aligned.
        Example: #REPLACE CP_UTF8   65001   // UTF-8  ->  Define CP_UTF8 for 65001   // UTF-8
        Only what a Define can safely express is converted: a name that is not a reserved word, is not CURRENT$WORKSPACE and starts with a letter, with a fixed value (a number, a negative number, or a string in quotes).
        Not changed, because they are text substitutions rather than constants: a value that is a name or an expression in parentheses, part of a macro, a reserved word as the name, CURRENT$WORKSPACE, the #HEADER section, and Language_*.inc files, which DataFlex uses to compile translated applications.
        A TODO comment "// TODO (DFRefactor): NOT converted - ..." is added where a line looks convertible but is not: inside an Enum_List, where a Define with a value would renumber the entries below it; where the value is unquoted text with spaces; and where the file tests the name with #IFDEF or #IFNDEF, which do not see a #REPLACE but do see a Define.
        {MethodType = eOtherFunction}
        {FnGroup = Modernization}
        {SummaryText = Changed: #REPLACE directive to Define}
        """ }
    Function ChangeReplaceDirectiveToDefine String[] ByRef asCode String sParameter Returns Integer
        Integer iRetval

        Get _ReplaceDirectiveToDefine (&asCode) to iRetval
        Function_Return iRetval
    End_Function

    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces the old Return command for subroutines with the modern Gosub_Return.
        The two are the same command (Return is the spelling from DataFlex 1), so this is a rename that does not change what the code does, and it removes one warning per line.
        Example: return -> Gosub_Return
        It also makes ConvertGosubToProcedure possible, because that function finds where a subroutine ends by its Gosub_Return lines, and cannot convert a block without them.
        Only a line whose first word of code is Return is changed. Not changed: Return used as an argument (Gosub_Return Return), a legacy name that ends with the word (cfg.key.return, p$return.max), a label that starts with it (return$flag:), Procedure_Return and Function_Return, and a one-line If (If (x) return), which must be changed by hand.
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: Return }
        { SummaryText = Changed: 'Return' command to 'Gosub_Return' }
        """ }
    Function ChangeReturnToGosubReturn String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        Integer iPos
        tTokenizer TokenizerData

        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData
        If (LineIsNotCode(&TokenizerData)) ;
            Function_Return False
        Get _ReturnCommandPos (TokenizerData.sIndentation + TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine) to iPos
        If (iPos = 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        // The masked line is position-aligned with the raw one, so splice the RAW line: the
        // indentation ahead of the command and anything behind it (an end comment) are kept.
        Move (Left(sLine, (iPos - 1)) + "Gosub_Return" + Mid(sLine, Length(sLine), (iPos + 6))) to sLine
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    /* ChangeStrLenToExpression / ChangeStrMarkToExpression - the derivation table, the refusal
       reasons and the run-time facts behind them live in the two { Description }s and in the block
       comment above _StrVarIdentChar (cRefactorFuncLib), which heads the shared engine:
       _StrVarLineInfo reads one line, _StrVarProcessMethod decides and applies one method,
       _StrVarToExpression walks the file. The two published functions differ only in the variable
       they hand it. The engine masks macro code before either runs, and writes the file whenever
       the array changed - so a TODO marker alone still reaches the disk while the count stays
       honest (a marker is not a conversion).
    */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces the legacy STRLEN variable with an expression based on the string command before it.
        After 'Pos x in s' or 'Replace x in s with y', a read becomes (Length(s) - Pos(x, s) + 1), the length from the found position to the end, or Length(s) + 1 when nothing was found. After Right or Mid it becomes their count argument.
        A method that only uses strlen as a temporary number gets a local variable instead (Integer iStrLen; a value that is written but never read goes to iVoid).
        Functions Pos() and Replace() never set strlen, so a read after a converted command already had an outdated value. Such a read is converted the same way, and named in the run notes.
        After Replace, the value only exists before the replacement, so a plain 'Move strlen to x' is moved above the Replace line; any other form keeps its line, with a TODO comment.
        A read also keeps its line, with the reason in the TODO comment, when no string command comes before it in the method, when that command is conditional or not supported (Sysdate, File_Size, Page, Index_Def), when a value it uses changes in between, when a block boundary lies in between, or when the method passes strlen to a Right or Mid without a count. The string command line itself is kept, because it may still set Found.
        { MethodType = eOtherFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Changed: strlen reads to expressions }
        """ }
    Function ChangeStrLenToExpression String[] ByRef asCode String sParameter Returns Integer
        Integer iRetval

        Get _StrVarToExpression (&asCode) "strlen" "ChangeStrLenToExpression" to iRetval
        Function_Return iRetval
    End_Function

    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces the legacy STRMARK variable with an expression based on the string command before it.
        After 'Pos x in s' or 'Replace x in s with y', a read becomes Pos(x, s), which is 0 when nothing was found, as before. After Left, Pad, Insert or Mid it becomes their count or position argument.
        A method that only uses strmark as a temporary number gets a local variable instead (Integer iStrMark; a value that is written but never read goes to iVoid).
        Functions Pos() and Replace() never set strmark, so a read after a converted command already had an outdated value. Such a read is converted the same way, and named in the run notes.
        After Replace, the value only exists before the replacement, so a plain 'Move strmark to x' is moved above the Replace line; any other form keeps its line, with a TODO comment.
        A read also keeps its line, with the reason in the TODO comment, when no string command comes before it in the method, when that command is conditional or not supported (Sysdate, File_Size, Page, Index_Def), when a value it uses changes in between, when a block boundary lies in between, or when the method passes strmark to a Left, Mid or Pad without a count. The string command line itself is kept, because it may still set Found.
        { MethodType = eOtherFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Changed: strmark reads to expressions }
        """ }
    Function ChangeStrMarkToExpression String[] ByRef asCode String sParameter Returns Integer
        Integer iRetval

        Get _StrVarToExpression (&asCode) "strmark" "ChangeStrMarkToExpression" to iRetval
        Function_Return iRetval
    End_Function

    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces the obsolete Sub command with the modern Subtract command.
        Example: Sub iAmount to iTotal -> Subtract iAmount from iTotal
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnFamily = Increment and decrement }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: Sub }
        { SummaryText = Changed: 'Sub' command to 'Subtract' }
        """ }
    Function ChangeSubToSubtract String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        String sNose sArg1 sMid sArg2
        Integer iItem iPos
        tTokenizer TokenizerData

        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData
        If (LineIsNotCode(&TokenizerData)) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End
        Move (SearchArray("Sub", TokenizerData.asLineTokens, Desktop, RefFunc(DFSTRICMP))) to iItem
        If (iItem = -1) ;
            Function_Return False
        // Legacy syntax is "Sub <value> to|from <variable>" -> needs 4 tokens from the keyword.
        If (SizeOfArray(TokenizerData.asLineTokens) < (iItem + 4)) ;
            Function_Return False
        Move TokenizerData.asLineTokens[iItem + 1] to sArg1
        Move TokenizerData.asLineTokens[iItem + 2] to sMid
        Move TokenizerData.asLineTokens[iItem + 3] to sArg2
        // The middle word MUST be "to" or "from", else this is not the legacy Sub command.
        If ((Lowercase(sMid) <> "to") and (Lowercase(sMid) <> "from")) ;
            Function_Return False
        // Locate the keyword boundary-aware on the MASKED line (position-
        // aligned with sLine) via _FindKeywordPos - a bare Pos("sub", ...)
        // matched INSIDE an earlier identifier (the "sub" in
        // "If (bSubmitted) Sub 1 from iCount") and spliced the line there
        // (2026-06-10 audit).
        Get _FindKeywordPos (TokenizerData.sIndentation + TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine) "Sub" True 1 to iPos
        If (iPos = 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        // Keep the indent + any nose before the command; modern Subtract always uses "from".
        Move (Left(sLine, (iPos - 1))) to sNose
        Move (sNose + "Subtract " + sArg1 + " from " + sArg2) to sLine
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    /* ChangeSysdate - the user-facing behaviour, the example and the limitations now live in
       the { Description }. Maintainer notes: matching goes through FirstLeftCommand, which is
       what keeps a variable named "sysdate", a passing mention and an inline-If nose form out
       of scope.
       (Renamed 2026-07-14 from ChangeSysdate4, which only did the Sysdate4 -> Sysdate half-step -
       trading one obsolete command for another; it now owns BOTH commands and truly modernises.)
    */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces the obsolete Sysdate and Sysdate4 commands with the modern CurrentDateTime() function.
        Example: Sysdate dToday -> Move (Date(CurrentDateTime())) to dToday
        The less common date and time form (Sysdate dDate tTime) gets a // TODO (DFRefactor) comment for a manual change, because its time variable cannot be set by the same CurrentDateTime() call.
        Only the command is matched, so a variable named 'sysdate', or the word in other text, is never changed. A one-line If (If (x) Sysdate dDate) is left for a manual change; the TODO comment on the date and time form also stops the Analyzer from reporting that line.
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: Sysdate4 }
        { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: Sysdate }
        { SummaryText = Changed: Sysdate / Sysdate4 command -> Move (CurrentDateTime()) }
        """ }
    Function ChangeSysdate String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        Integer iPos iItem iArgCount i iSize iDestPos
        String sKeyword sDest sTok sNose sTail sBlock sIndent
        String[] asArgs
        tTokenizer TokenizerData

        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData
        If (LineIsNotCode(&TokenizerData)) Function_Return False

        // Already carries a DFRefactor marker (a prior date+time bail) - leave it (idempotent).
        If (Lowercase(sLine) contains "(dfrefactor)") ;
            Function_Return False

        // Match Sysdate4 first (the longer word), then Sysdate, as a whole token - and only at
        // COMMAND position (the FIRST token). That skips a variable named "sysdate", a "Move x to
        // sysdate" use, and an inline-If nose form, all left for manual work. Matching the token
        // directly (not FirstLeftCommand) does NOT depend on the keyword being in the recognised-
        // command list - only "sysdate4" is guaranteed to be there, "sysdate" may not be.
        Move (SearchArray(CS_Sysdate4, TokenizerData.asLineTokens, Desktop, RefFunc(DFSTRICMP))) to iItem
        Move CS_Sysdate4 to sKeyword
        If (iItem = -1) Begin
            Move (SearchArray(CS_Sysdate, TokenizerData.asLineTokens, Desktop, RefFunc(DFSTRICMP))) to iItem
            Move CS_Sysdate to sKeyword
        End
        If (iItem <> 0) ;               // not found, or not the leading command
            Function_Return False

        // Count the REAL argument tokens after the keyword (stopping at a trailing comment). One
        // receiving var = the common date form; a second real token is the date+time form, which
        // can't fold into a single CurrentDateTime() call.
        Move (SizeOfArray(TokenizerData.asLineTokens)) to iSize
        Move 0 to iArgCount
        Move "" to sDest
        Move (iItem + 1) to i
        While (i < iSize)
            Move TokenizerData.asLineTokens[i] to sTok
            If (Left(sTok, 1) = "/" or Left(sTok, 1) = "#") Begin
                Move iSize to i          // trailing comment (// or overstrike-masked) - stop scanning
            End
            Else Begin
                If (Trim(sTok) <> "") Begin
                    Increment iArgCount
                    If (iArgCount = 1) ;
                        Move sTok to sDest
                    Move sTok to asArgs[SizeOfArray(asArgs)]
                End
                Increment i
            End
        Loop
        If (iArgCount = 0) ;             // no receiving var - not the command form
            Function_Return False

        /* THE DATE+TIME FORM IS CONVERTIBLE AFTER ALL (Nils 2026-07-30). It used to get a TODO
           comment and nothing else, on the grounds that it "can't fold into a single
           CurrentDateTime() call" - true, but it folds into FOUR, and the legacy syntax says
           exactly which is which:

               SysDate {dtDate} [{iHour} [{iMinute} [{iSecond}]]]

           so the positions ARE the meaning. Emitted as a block, with the original left commented
           above it so the conversion can be eyeballed against what it replaced:

               //                    Sysdate sDate sHour sMin sSec
                                     Move (Date(CurrentDateTime())) to sDate
                                     Move (DateGetHour(CurrentDateTime())) to sHour
                                     Move (DateGetMinute(CurrentDateTime())) to sMin
                                     Move (DateGetSecond(CurrentDateTime())) to sSec

           WHY FOUR CLOCK READS RATHER THAN ONE. Deriving the parts from the first variable -
           "Move (DateGetHour(sDate)) to sHour" - would be one atomic read and is what instinct
           reaches for. It is WRONG here: SysDate's first argument is conventionally declared Date,
           not DateTime, so assigning CurrentDateTime() to it TRUNCATES the time and every part
           would come back 0. Silently. Four independent reads always give the right values
           whatever the declaration, which is the trade worth making - the cost is that a block
           straddling a second boundary could pair an hour and minute from one second with a
           second from the next. Microseconds wide, and visible in the commented original if it
           ever matters.

           A multi-line result from a line-by-line function is established: DeclareRefactoredIndicators
           builds a C_NewLine-joined block and assigns it to sLine the same way.

           More than four arguments is not the documented syntax, so it keeps the old TODO rather
           than guessing at what the extras mean. */
        If (iArgCount > 1 and iArgCount <= 4) Begin
            Move TokenizerData.sIndentation to sIndent
            // "//" + the ORIGINAL line, so the text under the comment is byte-identical.
            Move ("//" + sLine) to sBlock
            Append sBlock C_NewLine sIndent "Move (Date(CurrentDateTime())) to " asArgs[0]
            If (iArgCount >= 2) ;
                Append sBlock C_NewLine sIndent "Move (DateGetHour(CurrentDateTime())) to " asArgs[1]
            If (iArgCount >= 3) ;
                Append sBlock C_NewLine sIndent "Move (DateGetMinute(CurrentDateTime())) to " asArgs[2]
            If (iArgCount >= 4) ;
                Append sBlock C_NewLine sIndent "Move (DateGetSecond(CurrentDateTime())) to " asArgs[3]
            Move sBlock to sLine
            Function_Return True
        End

        // Reached only with MORE than four arguments now - not the documented syntax, so the old
        // leave-it-and-say-so behaviour still applies rather than a guess at what the extras are.
        If (iArgCount > 1) Begin
            // Date+time form: mark it for a hand conversion (the trailing DFRefactor TODO also keeps
            // Deep Scan quiet). No behaviour is lost - the line stays until the user acts. BUT the
            // Sysdate4 -> Sysdate half-step still applies (Nils 2026-07-19): Sysdate4 is the DEAD
            // name while plain Sysdate survives (dates are always 4-digit now), so the waiting line
            // is left on the living command instead of parking an obsolete one behind the TODO.
            If (sKeyword = CS_Sysdate4) Begin
                Get _FindKeywordPos (TokenizerData.sIndentation + TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine) sKeyword True 1 to iPos
                If (iPos > 0) ;
                    Move (Left(sLine, (iPos - 1)) + CS_Sysdate + Mid(sLine, CI_EOL, (iPos + Length(sKeyword)))) to sLine
            End
            Move (sLine + " // TODO (DFRefactor): Sysdate date+time form - convert by hand to CurrentDateTime()") to sLine
            Function_Return True
        End

        /* WHY Date(CurrentDateTime()) AND NOT CurrentDateTime() (Nils 2026-07-30, on the converter
           that had already shipped): SysDate assigns a DATE. Whether the receiver is one is not
           knowable from a single line, and it decides the result:

               Date     dVar   - the assignment truncates anyway; the wrapper is redundant, harmless
               DateTime dtVar  - unwrapped keeps a time SysDate never set
               String   sVar   - unwrapped writes "07/30/2026 14:23:45" where SysDate wrote the date

           The String case is the one that bites, and it fails silently: the value looks right until
           something compares or parses it. Wrapping matches SysDate for every declaration, so the
           converter no longer depends on a type it cannot see. */
        // Single-var form -> Move (Date(CurrentDateTime())) to <var>. Keep the indentation (the nose) AND
        // any trailing end-comment (whatever followed the date var on the original line).
        Get _FindKeywordPos (TokenizerData.sIndentation + TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine) sKeyword True 1 to iPos
        If (iPos = 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        Move (Left(sLine, (iPos - 1))) to sNose
        // Isolate the tail after the date var: take the line past the keyword, then past the var.
        Move (Mid(sLine, CI_EOL, (iPos + Length(sKeyword)))) to sTail
        Move (Pos(sDest, sTail)) to iDestPos
        If (iDestPos > 0) Begin
            Move (Mid(sTail, CI_EOL, (iDestPos + Length(sDest)))) to sTail   // " // comment" or ""
        End
        Else Begin
            Move "" to sTail
        End
        Move (sNose + "Move (Date(CurrentDateTime())) to " + sDest + sTail) to sLine
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces the obsolete 'System' command with the modern 'Abort' command.
        Example: System  -->  Abort
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: System }
        { SummaryText = System command -> Abort }
        """ }
    Function ChangeSystemToAbort String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        Boolean bChanged
        tTokenizer TokenizerData
        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData
        Get _SwapLeadingCommandKeyword (&sLine) TokenizerData "System" "Abort" to bChanged
        Function_Return bChanged
    End_Function

    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Replaces the legacy Get Create U_Class with Get Create (RefClass(Class)).
        Example: Get Create U_Array to hoArray -> Get Create (RefClass(Array)) to hoArray
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Changed: 'Create U_Class' to 'Create (RefClass(Class))' }
        """ }
    Function ChangeUClassToRefClass String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        Boolean bChanged bValid bIgnore
        String sTest sClassName sOldStyle sNewStyle
        Integer iPos
        tTokenizer TokenizerData

        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData
        If (LineIsNotCode(&TokenizerData)) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End
        Move False to bChanged
        Move "" to sClassName
        Move (Lowercase(sLine)) to sTest
        Move (Replaces(Character(9), sTest, CS_S)) to sTest // tab
        Get TrimInsideSpaces sTest to sTest
        // Two lines which are mostly for protection when running DfRefactor on the DfRefactor code itself
        Get _RemoveEndComment sTest to sTest                // Don't rewrite this in a comment
        Get RemoveStringsFromLine sTest to sTest            // If this is in a string then obviously leave it alone too
        If (Pos(" create u_", sTest)) Begin
            Move (Lowercase(sLine)) to sTest
            // Locate the U_ class token on the MASKED line (position-aligned
            // with sLine) with a LEFT boundary only - "u_" is a PREFIX, so
            // bWholeWord is False. A bare Pos could land inside an earlier
            // identifier ("hU_x") or a string literal and rewrite the wrong
            // text (2026-06-10 audit).
            Get _FindKeywordPos (TokenizerData.sIndentation + TokenizerData.sOverstrikeLine) "u_" False 1 to iPos
            Get IsValidClassCharacter sTest iPos to bValid
            While (bValid)
                Move (sClassName + Mid(sLine, 1, iPos)) to sClassName
                Increment iPos
                Get IsValidClassCharacter sTest iPos to bValid
            Loop
        End
        If (sClassName <> "") Begin
            Move True to bChanged
            Move sClassName to sOldStyle
            Move (Right(sClassName, Length(sClassName) - 2)) to sClassName // strip away the "U_"
            Move ("(RefClass(" + sClassName + "))") to sNewStyle
            Move (Replace(sOldStyle, sLine, sNewStyle)) to sLine
        End
        Function_Return bChanged
    End_Function

    /* ConvertGosubToProcedure - Phase 2 of the Gosub work (GosubGotoReport is the Phase-1 worksheet).
       Converts the SAFE subset only: a subroutine is CONVERTIBLE when its control flow is self-contained
       AND every subroutine it calls is likewise convertible - see _GosubConvertPlan, which decides that
       and hands back the block bounds. 'Send' is a runtime message dispatch, so a converted procedure may
       be forward-referenced: the still-flat entry code above it calls it fine, which is why the extracted
       Procedures can be emitted in place instead of being hoisted. A FILE-SCOPE variable referenced inside
       a converted subroutine is promoted to Global_Variable (so the extracted Procedure can see it) and
       tagged with a review marker; nothing is ever renamed or deleted, which is what keeps the pass
       reversible via the engine backup. Non-convertible subroutines, their labels and their gosubs are
       left untouched. Each line is read as the file walk reads it (_WalkedCodeLine), here and in GosubGotoReport:
       the text of a comment, a report image or a multi-line string is no code and passes through as written (raw
       lines until 2026-10-30).
       The per-line cores are pure + unit-tested (oConvertGosubToProcedure-Tests.pkg).
       The user-facing half of this note (what it converts, and its limitations) now lives in the
       { Description } - the manual is generated from that, not from this comment.
    */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Converts legacy Gosub subroutines to global procedures, but only the ones that can be converted safely.
        A subroutine with simple flow (plain Gosub_Return lines, no Goto out of it) that only calls other convertible subroutines becomes 'Procedure <label>_Sub GLOBAL ... End_Procedure'. 'Gosub X' becomes 'Send X_Sub', 'Gosub_Return' becomes 'Procedure_Return', and 'ON..GOSUB' becomes a Case block of Send calls.
        Subroutines that cannot be converted are not changed; GosubGotoReport shows why.
        Variables at file level that a converted subroutine uses become Global_Variable declarations, with a comment for you to review. Nothing is renamed or deleted. The text of comments, report images and multi-line strings is never read as code.
        Limitation: a shared variable that is declared inside a converted subroutine becomes a local variable of that procedure. If another converted subroutine also uses it, make it global by hand.
        This is a first, careful version: check the result on a real Gosub workspace. The change can be undone from the backup.
        { MethodType = eOtherFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Converted: Gosub subroutines -> global Procedures (safe subset) }
        """ }
    Function ConvertGosubToProcedure String[] ByRef asCode String sParameter Returns Integer
        String[] asOut asCandLabels asCandOrig asRefNames asBody asEmpty
        Integer[] aiCandStart aiCandEnd
        String sLine sLowTrim sIndent sBlockIndent sTail sTailLine sCodePart sTypeWord sTypeLow sTok sNames sComment sCh
        Integer iLast iLine iPlan iConverted iPromoted iN i k iStartIdx iInEnd iObjDepth iProcDepth iStructDepth iWord iColon iEnd
        Boolean bHasGosub bInBlock bPromoted1 bReferenced bAllDigits
        tLineInfo[] aInfo

        // Perf gate: no 'gosub' text anywhere (raw scan) means nothing to convert.
        Get _FileContainsText of Self (&asCode) "gosub" to bHasGosub
        If (bHasGosub = False) ;
            Function_Return 0

        // The conversion plan: which subroutines are safe to extract, and their block bounds.
        Get _GosubConvertPlan of Self (&asCode) (&asCandLabels) (&asCandOrig) (&aiCandStart) (&aiCandEnd) to iPlan
        If (iPlan = 0) ;
            Function_Return 0

        // The file walk: what each line is, so that only code is read as code (_WalkedCodeLine).
        Get ScanSourceFile of Self (&asCode) to aInfo

        // Names referenced inside the convertible blocks - drives the Global_Variable promotion.
        Get _GosubCollectBlockNames of Self (&asCode) (&aInfo) (&aiCandStart) (&aiCandEnd) (&asRefNames) to iN

        Move (SizeOfArray(asCode) - 1) to iLast
        Move 0  to iConverted
        Move 0  to iPromoted
        Move -1 to iInEnd
        Move 0  to iObjDepth
        Move 0  to iProcDepth
        Move 0  to iStructDepth
        For iLine from 0 to iLast
            // Per-file progress bar: the canonical ~20-line nudge (no-op without an active panel).
            If (Mod(iLine, 20) = 0 and (Active_State(ghoStatusPanel) = True)) Begin
                Send Update_StatusPanel of ghoStatusPanel ("Source line:" * String(iLine + 1) * "of:" * String(iLast + 1))
                Set piPosition of ghoProgressBar to (iLine + 1)
            End
            Move asCode[iLine] to sLine

            // Is this line the START of a convertible subroutine?
            Move -1 to iStartIdx
            For i from 0 to (iPlan - 1)
                If (aiCandStart[i] = iLine) ;
                    Move i to iStartIdx
            Loop

            If (iStartIdx <> -1) Begin
                // Open the extracted Procedure in place of the label; the label line is consumed.
                Move (Left(sLine, (Length(sLine) - Length(LTrim(sLine))))) to sBlockIndent
                Move (sBlockIndent + "Procedure " + asCandOrig[iStartIdx] + "_Sub GLOBAL   // [DFRefactor] converted from Gosub subroutine '" + asCandOrig[iStartIdx] + "'") to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                Move (aiCandEnd[iStartIdx]) to iInEnd
                Increment iConverted
                // A label may carry trailing code on the same line ('done: Move 1 to iX') - keep it as
                // the first body line (transformed like any other in-block line).
                Move (Pos(":", sLine)) to iColon
                If (iColon > 0) Begin
                    Move (Mid(sLine, Length(sLine), (iColon + 1))) to sTail
                    If (Trim(sTail) <> "") Begin
                        Move (sBlockIndent + "    " + Trim(sTail)) to sTailLine
                        Get _GosubTransformBodyLine of Self sTailLine True (&asCandLabels) (&asCandOrig) (&asBody) to iN
                        For k from 0 to (iN - 1)
                            Move asBody[k] to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                        Loop
                    End
                End
                // Single-line block: close it immediately.
                If (iInEnd = iLine) Begin
                    Move (sBlockIndent + "End_Procedure") to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                    Move -1 to iInEnd
                End
            End
            Else Begin
                /* ONLY THE LINE'S CODE, as the file walk read it (2026-10-30). Read raw, a report image's line at file
                   scope that started with a type word ("Date ...") and held a name a converted subroutine uses was
                   "promoted" - the printed image changed - and an "Object" or a "Procedure" opening a comment's or an
                   image's line moved the scope depths. A line with no code passes through as written. */
                Get _WalkedCodeLine of Self sLine aInfo[iLine] to sCodePart
                Move (Lowercase(Trim(Replaces(Character(9), sCodePart, " ")))) to sLowTrim
                Move (iInEnd >= 0) to bInBlock
                Move False to bPromoted1
                If (sLowTrim = "") Begin
                    // No code: a blank line, or a comment's, a report image's or a string's text
                    Move sLine to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                End
                Else Begin
                    // Scope bookkeeping over the ORIGINAL structure (my injected Procedures are only in
                    // asOut, never re-scanned) - only depth-0 lines outside a converted block are file scope.
                    Send _TrackScopeDepth of Self sLowTrim (&iObjDepth) (&iProcDepth) (&iStructDepth)

                    If (bInBlock = False and iObjDepth = 0 and iProcDepth = 0 and iStructDepth = 0) Begin
                        // File-scope bare variable declaration referenced by a converted sub -> promote it.
                        Get _NthWord of Self sCodePart 1 to sTypeWord
                        Move (Lowercase(sTypeWord)) to sTypeLow
                        If (sTypeLow = "string" or sTypeLow = "integer" or sTypeLow = "number" or ;
                            sTypeLow = "date" or sTypeLow = "boolean") Begin
                            Move "" to sNames
                            Move False to bReferenced
                            Move 2 to iWord
                            Repeat
                                Get _NthWord of Self sCodePart iWord to sTok
                                If (sTok <> "") Begin
                                    Move True to bAllDigits
                                    For i from 1 to (Length(sTok))
                                        Move (Mid(sTok, 1, i)) to sCh
                                        If (sCh < "0" or sCh > "9") ;
                                            Move False to bAllDigits
                                    Loop
                                    Move (sNames + " " + sTok) to sNames
                                    If (bAllDigits = False) Begin
                                        If (SearchArray(Lowercase(sTok), asRefNames) <> -1) ;
                                            Move True to bReferenced
                                    End
                                End
                                Increment iWord
                            Until (sTok = "")
                            If (sNames <> "" and bReferenced = True) Begin
                                // The trailing comment survives: the code ends at its last character (positions
                                // align), so the rest of the ORIGINAL line is the comment - a '//' one, which the
                                // code cuts, or a block comment, which it blanks.
                                Move (Length(RTrim(sCodePart))) to iEnd
                                Move (Mid(sLine, Length(sLine), (iEnd + 1))) to sComment
                                Move (Left(sLine, (Length(sLine) - Length(LTrim(sLine))))) to sIndent
                                Move (sIndent + "// [DFRefactor] promoted to Global_Variable so the extracted Gosub procedure(s) can reference it - review its lifetime/scope.") to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                                If (Trim(sComment) <> "") ;
                                    Move (sIndent + "Global_Variable " + sTypeWord + sNames + "   " + Trim(sComment)) to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                                Else ;
                                    Move (sIndent + "Global_Variable " + sTypeWord + sNames) to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                                Increment iPromoted
                                Move True to bPromoted1
                            End
                        End
                    End

                    If (bPromoted1 = False) Begin
                        Get _GosubTransformBodyLine of Self sLine bInBlock (&asCandLabels) (&asCandOrig) (&asBody) to iN
                        For k from 0 to (iN - 1)
                            Move asBody[k] to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                        Loop
                    End
                End

                // Reached the last line of a convertible block? close the Procedure.
                If (iInEnd >= 0 and iLine = iInEnd) Begin
                    Move (sBlockIndent + "End_Procedure") to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                    Move -1 to iInEnd
                End
            End
        Loop

        // Unterminated block (should not happen - the last block ends at the last line): close it.
        If (iInEnd >= 0) ;
            Move (sBlockIndent + "End_Procedure") to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]

        If (iConverted > 0 or iPromoted > 0) ;
            Move asOut to asCode
        Function_Return iConverted
    End_Function

    /* Deep Scan (cDeepScanner.pkg): the source-driven complement to the Modernization Analyzer.
       The Analyzer triages what the COMPILER reports - but most of the retired language surface
       compiles silently (If Not, Chain, Cmdline, the Reg_* family, char-mode leftovers) or the
       user wants a map BEFORE compiling anything. This report scans the selected files against
       a catalog seeded from the docs' "Obsolete Commands, Functions & Other Symbols" list (187
       symbols incl. each page's replacement guidance, harvested 2026-07-10). Detection is
       context-aware (see cDeepScanner.pkg's header): command-form-only symbols match in command
       position only, comparison modes only on condition lines, everything comment/literal-safe
       via _CodeOnlyLine + boundary-aware matching. Findings carry the SAME eMod* triage
       categories as the Analyzer so a later phase can feed the analyze grid / fix-plan flow;
       for now the report names the routed refactor function (or the documented replacement)
       per finding. NO {ModRecipe} - there is no compiler message to route; that absence is
       this function's reason to exist.
       UNPUBLISHED BY DESIGN (Nils 2026-07-12): { Published = False } is PERMANENT, not merely the
       Alpha 3 gate. Deep Scan is a DEDICATED flow - the dashboard button runs RunDeepScan, which
       builds cDeepScanner directly, so this function is never on that path. As a report-only entry
       in the MANUAL function picker it would only DUPLICATE the button and confuse. Keep it False
       even when CB_DeepScanEnabled goes True for Alpha 4. (No {ModRecipe} -> no recipe-table effect.)
       See the gate notes on CB_DeepScanEnabled in RefactorConstants.h.pkg. */
    { Published = False }
    { Description = """
        Reports only. Makes no changes to the source code.
        Analyze Source (Deep Scan): scans the selected source files for obsolete DataFlex commands, functions and symbols, without compiling, so the code that the compiler never warns about is found at its line.
        Text in comments and strings is never matched, and symbols that exist as both commands and functions (Left, Mid, Pos, Trim ...) are only matched as commands; the function forms are modern and are ignored.
        Each finding names the refactoring function that converts it, or the modern replacement to make by hand.
        { MethodType = eReportFunctionAll }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Deep Scan: obsolete-symbol report written (see log) }
        """ }
    Function DeepScanObsoleteSymbols String[] ByRef asFiles String sParameter Returns Integer
        Handle ho
        String sHomePath sLogFile sFilePath sRoute
        String[] asCode
        tDeepScanRule[] aRules
        tDeepScanHit[] aHits
        Integer iCh iFile iFileLast iTotal iN i
        Boolean bPanel bCancel

        Get Create (RefClass(cDeepScanner)) to ho
        If (ho = 0) Begin
            Error "Could not create cDeepScanner object. (Function: DeepScanObsoleteSymbols)"
            Function_Return 0
        End
        Get LoadDeepScanRules of ho (DeepScanRulesJsonPath(ho)) to aRules

        Get psHomeTarget of ghoApplication to sHomePath
        Move (vFolderFormat(sHomePath) + CS_BackupFolder + CS_DirSeparator + CS_DeepScanReportLogFile) to sLogFile
        Get rfOpenOutput sLogFile to iCh
        If (iCh < 0) Begin
            Send Destroy of ho
            Function_Return 0
        End

        Writeln channel iCh "Analyze Source - obsolete commands, functions & other symbols  (report only - no changes made)."
        Writeln channel iCh "Source-driven: found by analyzing the code itself, not the compiler output - most of these"
        Writeln channel iCh "constructs produce NO compiler warning. Categories:"
        Writeln channel iCh "  [Auto-fix]  an existing refactor function converts it - run the named function"
        Writeln channel iCh "  [Review]    a refactor function/report addresses it - review, then run the named function"
        Writeln channel iCh "  [Explain]   no safe automatic transform - the modern replacement is described"
        Writeln channel iCh ""

        Move 0 to iTotal
        Move False to bCancel
        Move (SizeOfArray(asFiles)) to iFileLast
        Decrement iFileLast
        // This report loops files itself (eReportFunctionAll), so drive the status panel per
        // file - otherwise it looks frozen when this is the only function run.
        Get _ReportPanelStart iFileLast to bPanel
        For iFile from 0 to iFileLast
            Move asFiles[iFile] to sFilePath
            Send _ReportPanelFile bPanel "Analyze Source - File No:" "Analyzing source for obsolete symbols:" sFilePath iFile iFileLast
            Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray(sFilePath)) to asCode
            Get ScanLines of ho (&asCode) (&aRules) to aHits
            Move (SizeOfArray(aHits)) to iN
            If (iN > 0) Begin
                Writeln channel iCh ("=== " + sFilePath + " ===")
                For i from 0 to (iN - 1)
                    If (aHits[i].sRefactorFn <> "") ;
                        Move ("-> run " + aHits[i].sRefactorFn + "   (" + aHits[i].sHint + ")") to sRoute
                    Else ;
                        Move ("-> " + aHits[i].sHint) to sRoute
                    Writeln channel iCh ("Ln " + Pad(String(aHits[i].iLine), 6) + "[" + DSCategoryText(ho, aHits[i].eCategory) + "]  " + Pad(aHits[i].sSymbol, 28) + sRoute)
                Loop
                Writeln channel iCh ""
                Add iN to iTotal
            End
            // Mid-run cancel (the Cancel button): a *All function gets ONE call for all files,
            // so the engine's per-file Cancel_Check cannot fire while we scan. Poll the safe
            // accessor (NEVER Cancel_Check - it consumes the signal) and bail; the engine's
            // outer Cancel_Check then runs the confirm pipeline once.
            Get pbUserCancelRequested of ghoEngine to bCancel
            If (bCancel = True) ;
                Move iFileLast to iFile
        Loop

        Send Destroy of ho
        Send _ReportFinish iCh sLogFile iTotal bCancel bPanel " finding(s)."
        Function_Return iTotal
    End_Function

    /* FunctionReturnMismatchReport - the DECLARATION-level view of DF 25/26 error 4544 ("ambiguous
       return type in expression"). DataFlex message names are workspace-global: every Function /
       Property / Register_Function declaration REGISTERS a return type on the shared message id, and
       when two declarations of ONE name disagree, the newer compiler rejects every expression-style
       call of that name - in files that have nothing whatever to do with the class responsible. A
       clean DF 19.1 build never showed it.

       Which is why this reports DECLARATIONS, not the lines the compiler points at. The LegacyApp case
       (Nils, 2026-07-22): a third-party hash-table library declares Value as Variant / Variant / Number
       across three classes, DataFlex declares it String in nine of its own packages, and roughly 765
       expression call sites spread over 141 files go 4544 - while the library that caused it is
       referenced by twelve. Rewriting the 765 is the workaround; renaming the three is the fix.

       The install's OWN declarations are read live (InstallNameDeclsForNames) because without them the
       advice is wrong in the commonest case. "Align every declaration to one return type" is useless
       when DataFlex holds the name with a different type - you cannot change its side, so the only
       thing that clears the error is renaming yours. Getting that backwards costs a day.

       REPORT ONLY - renaming touches (often third-party) library code and is a human decision, so
       nothing is rewritten. Logs to the Backup folder; surfaced via the 'Other Log Files' toolbar
       button. Mirrors TypeToStructReport.

       Was NameCollisionReport, whose PART 2 inventoried error-4390 backports off a source scan of the
       install packages. That detector could not see names the RUNTIME owns (Split) and so contradicted
       the compiler; the guided findings list drives 4390 off the compiler message instead, which is
       where that job now lives. Dropping PART 2 is what let this report be named for what it does. */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Reports only. Makes no changes to the source code.
        Finds the declarations behind error 4544 in DataFlex 25 and 26: one message name that is declared with more than one return type.
        It lists every declaration of each such name, both in the workspace and in the target DataFlex, so that you can fix the few declarations that cause the error, instead of the hundreds of calls that it reports.
        Names whose declarations are all marked Overloaded are listed separately, and are not counted: DataFlex chooses between them by the number of parameters, so several return types are valid, and neither 4544 fix is needed.
        The results are written to a log file; open it with Tools > Logs > Other Log Files.
        { MethodType = eReportFunctionAll }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Report: declarations that disagree on a return type (DF25 error 4544) }
        """ }
    Function FunctionReturnMismatchReport String[] ByRef asFiles String sParameter Returns Integer
        String sHomePath sLogFile sFilePath sNameOrig sParam sMcWhat sMcWhy sMajority sDeclClass
        String[] asCode asColNames asColNamesLc asOtherLogFiles asKeep asEmptyNames asInstNameLc asMcDecls asMcOdd asOwnerClasses asMcTypes
        String[] asOvlNames
        Integer iCh iFile iFileLast i j k iLast iCollisions iErroring iLatent
        Integer iOwnTypes iDfDecls iDfTypes iOwnDecls iGlobalDecls iGuardVer iOvlState m iOvlLast
        Integer[] aiCallCounts aiFileCounts
        Boolean bPanel bCancel bScanFailed bExtDup bIsComProxy
        tWorkspacePaths WS
        tNameDecl[] aAllDecls aFileDecls aInstDecls aOwn aDf aOvl
        String[] asEmptyCode

        Get psHomeTarget of ghoApplication to sHomePath
        Move (vFolderFormat(sHomePath) + CS_BackupFolder + CS_DirSeparator + CS_ReturnMismatchReportLogFile) to sLogFile
        Get rfOpenOutput sLogFile to iCh
        If (iCh < 0) ;
            Function_Return 0
        Move (Trim(sParameter)) to sParam
        // The workspace's own version, resolved ONCE and exactly as the guard converters resolve it -
        // so the duplicate-external advice below shows the precise directive they would write.
        Get pTargetWS of ghoApplication to WS
        Get DfVersionToMacroNumber WS.sDfVersion to iGuardVer

        Writeln channel iCh "Contested declaration report  (report only - no changes made)."
        Writeln channel iCh ""
        Writeln channel iCh "Two things can make a declaration contested, and this report covers both:"
        Writeln channel iCh "  RETURN-TYPE MISMATCH (error 4544) - one name declared with more than one type."
        Writeln channel iCh "  NAME ALREADY IN USE  (error 4390) - the name belongs to something else already;"
        Writeln channel iCh "                        when both sides are External_Functions, the same Windows"
        Writeln channel iCh "                        API is simply declared twice."
        Writeln channel iCh "Each block below says which one it is; the fix is different for each."
        Writeln channel iCh ""
        Writeln channel iCh "DataFlex message names are workspace-global. When two declarations of ONE name disagree"
        Writeln channel iCh "on the return type, DF 25/26 rejects EVERY expression-style call of that name (error 4544),"
        Writeln channel iCh "including in files that never touch the class responsible. DF 19.1 resolved it silently."
        Writeln channel iCh ""
        Writeln channel iCh "So the fix is at the few DECLARATIONS below, NOT at the (often hundreds of) call sites the"
        Writeln channel iCh "compiler points at. Each name lists every declaration of it - the ones DataFlex ships first,"
        Writeln channel iCh "then the workspace's - because DataFlex's side cannot be changed. If it holds the name with a"
        Writeln channel iCh "different type, aligning the workspace's declarations with EACH OTHER will not clear the error."
        Writeln channel iCh "Renaming them will, and every other call site then compiles untouched."
        Writeln channel iCh ""
        // ... with ONE exception, said here because this paragraph is read before any block below and
        // otherwise sends the reader looking for declarations that are none of their business.
        Writeln channel iCh "THE EXCEPTION is a name the workspace never declares - a DataFlex COM-wrapper method such as"
        Writeln channel iCh "ComItem, say - where every disagreeing declaration is DataFlex's own. Nothing there can be"
        Writeln channel iCh "renamed or aligned, and that name's block below says so: for those, the fix IS at the call"
        Writeln channel iCh "sites (each one becomes a Get statement)."
        Writeln channel iCh ""
        Writeln channel iCh "ERRORING = the name is used in an expression somewhere, so it 4544s today."
        Writeln channel iCh "LATENT   = a real conflict, but no expression call site found - harmless until there is one."
        Writeln channel iCh ""
        Writeln channel iCh "Note: if the source tree holds more than one copy or version of a library (VpeBase3/5/6,"
        Writeln channel iCh "two vwin32fh.pkg), only the COMPILED copy matters - a name listed only across such twins is"
        Writeln channel iCh "not a real collision. Check against the Use graph."
        Writeln channel iCh ""

        Move (SizeOfArray(asFiles)) to iFileLast
        Decrement iFileLast
        Move (ghoStatusPanel <> 0 and Active_State(ghoStatusPanel) = True) to bPanel
        If (bPanel = True) Begin
            // Two file passes are budgeted: harvest declarations, then (if any collide) count call
            // sites. The install pass budgets itself on top when it runs.
            Set piMaximumTot of ghoStatusPanel to (2 * (iFileLast + 1))
            Set piPosition   of ghoProgressBar to 0
            Set piAdvanceBy  of ghoProgressBar to 1
        End

        // ---- Pass 1: harvest every Function / Property / Register_Function declaration ----
        Move False to bCancel
        Move "" to sDeclClass
        For iFile from 0 to iFileLast
            Move asFiles[iFile] to sFilePath
            If (bPanel = True) Begin
                // "File No: x of: y" - an eReportFunctionAll gets all files at once, so we set the
                // caption ourselves (the engine only does it for line-by-line functions).
                Set Caption_Text   of ghoStatusPanel to ("Declarations - File No:" * String(iFile + 1) * "of:" * String(iFileLast + 1))
                Set Title_Text     of ghoStatusPanel to "Scanning declarations:"
                Set TableName_Text of ghoStatusPanel to sFilePath
                Send DoAdvanceTot of ghoStatusPanel
            End
            /* A GENERATED COM PROXY DECLARES NOTHING OF YOURS. These files sit in the workspace
               (Excel9.pkg, MSWORD.pkg, msoutl.pkg, the Codejock wrappers), so every declaration in
               them counted as one of YOUR OWN - and ComItem, a name DataFlex generates and owns,
               was therefore offered as a one-click rename. It renamed 214 declarations across nine
               vendor packages and unresolved every "Set ComItem" in the workspace (WinServ,
               2026-08-26).

               Harvest nothing from them and the classification comes right on its own: with no
               declarations of its own the name is neither renameable nor alignable, and the flow
               falls through to the call-site rewrite - which is exactly what this report's own
               header has always promised for "a name you never declare yourself". The file is not
               even read: an empty array walks to nothing below. */
            Get _IsDataFlexCOMProxyClassesFile sFilePath to bIsComProxy
            If (bIsComProxy = True) Begin
                Move asEmptyCode to asCode
            End
            Else Begin
                Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray(sFilePath)) to asCode
            End
            // Scale the per-file bar to THIS file's lines; the walkers below step it as they go.
            If (bPanel = True) ;
                Send _StartFileLineProgress (SizeOfArray(asCode))
            Get _NameDeclsFromLines of Self asCode sFilePath to aFileDecls
            // Which CLASS (if any) declares the narrowed name here - the rename converter walks class
            // families, so a name declared only at global scope is out of its reach and must not be
            // offered as a one-click rename (Nils 2026-07-22: "SPLITByKey" on a global function).
            If (sParam <> "" and sDeclClass = "") Begin
                Get _ClassesDeclaringName of Self asCode sParam to asOwnerClasses
                If (SizeOfArray(asOwnerClasses) > 0) ;
                    Move (asOwnerClasses[0]) to sDeclClass
            End
            Move (SizeOfArray(aFileDecls) - 1) to iLast
            For j from 0 to iLast
                Move aFileDecls[j] to aAllDecls[SizeOfArray(aAllDecls)]
            Loop
            // Mid-run cancel: poll the safe accessor (NEVER Cancel_Check - it consumes the signal).
            Get pbUserCancelRequested of ghoEngine to bCancel
            If (bCancel = True) ;
                Move iFileLast to iFile
        Loop
        If (bPanel = True) ;
            Set Title_Text of ghoStatusPanel to ""

        // The contested set: names declared with >= 2 distinct return types (the pure, tested core).
        Get _CollidingNames of Self aAllDecls to asColNames
        // A caller can NARROW the report to one name (sParameter). The findings list does this: its rows
        // are per-name, so opening the same workspace-wide report from each of them told the user
        // nothing about the row they actually clicked (Nils 2026-07-21).
        If (sParam <> "") Begin
            Move asEmptyNames to asKeep
            Move (SizeOfArray(asColNames) - 1) to iLast
            For i from 0 to iLast
                If (Lowercase(Trim(asColNames[i])) = Lowercase(sParam)) ;
                    Move asColNames[i] to asKeep[SizeOfArray(asKeep)]
            Loop
            Move asKeep to asColNames
        End
        // NARROWED TO A NAME THE WORKSPACE DOES NOT SELF-COLLIDE ON: keep it anyway. The findings rows
        // are driven by what the COMPILER said, and the commonest 4544 shape is exactly this - ONE
        // workspace declaration against DataFlex's own. Dropping it here is what made the report read
        // as a flat contradiction of the row that opened it (Nils 2026-07-21); the install pass below
        // now has the declarations needed to explain it properly.
        If (sParam <> "" and SizeOfArray(asColNames) = 0) ;
            Move sParam to asColNames[0]
        Move (SizeOfArray(asColNames) - 1) to iLast

        // ---- Pass 2: count EXPRESSION-STYLE call sites per contested name, to split ERRORING (used in
        //      an expression -> actually 4544s today) from LATENT (a real conflict, but never
        //      expression-called, so harmless until it is). Only worth a re-read when names collide. ----
        For i from 0 to iLast
            Move (Lowercase(asColNames[i])) to asColNamesLc[i]
            Move 0 to aiCallCounts[i]
        Loop
        If (SizeOfArray(asColNames) > 0 and bCancel = False) Begin
            For iFile from 0 to iFileLast
                Move asFiles[iFile] to sFilePath
                If (bPanel = True) Begin
                    Set Caption_Text   of ghoStatusPanel to ("Call sites - File No:" * String(iFile + 1) * "of:" * String(iFileLast + 1))
                    Set Title_Text     of ghoStatusPanel to "Counting expression call sites:"
                    Set TableName_Text of ghoStatusPanel to sFilePath
                    Send DoAdvanceTot of ghoStatusPanel
                End
                Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray(sFilePath)) to asCode
                If (bPanel = True) ;
                    Send _StartFileLineProgress (SizeOfArray(asCode))
                Get _CountNameCallsInLines of Self asCode asColNamesLc to aiFileCounts
                For i from 0 to iLast
                    Move (aiCallCounts[i] + aiFileCounts[i]) to aiCallCounts[i]
                Loop
                Get pbUserCancelRequested of ghoEngine to bCancel
                If (bCancel = True) ;
                    Move iFileLast to iFile
            Loop
            If (bPanel = True) ;
                Set Title_Text of ghoStatusPanel to ""
        End

        // ---- Pass 3: what the TARGET DataFlex declares for these same names. The whole difference
        //      between advice that works and advice that merely sounds right (see the header). ----
        If (SizeOfArray(asColNames) > 0 and bCancel = False) Begin
            If (bPanel = True) ;
                Set Title_Text of ghoStatusPanel to "Reading the target DataFlex's own declarations:"
            For i from 0 to iLast
                Move (Lowercase(Trim(asColNames[i]))) to asInstNameLc[i]
            Loop
            Get InstallNameDeclsForNames of Self asInstNameLc bPanel to aInstDecls
            Set psMismatchDeclClass to sDeclClass
            Get pbInstallScanFailed to bScanFailed
            If (bPanel = True) ;
                Set Title_Text of ghoStatusPanel to ""
        End

        // ---- One block per contested name: who declares it, how badly, and what to actually do ----
        Move 0 to iCollisions
        Move 0 to iErroring
        Move 0 to iLatent
        For i from 0 to iLast
            Move asColNames[i] to sNameOrig
            Get _DeclsForName of Self aAllDecls  sNameOrig to aOwn
            Get _DeclsForName of Self aInstDecls sNameOrig to aDf
            Get _DistinctTypeCount of Self aOwn to iOwnTypes
            // The DataFlex side's own spread matters when the workspace declares the name NOWHERE: 51
            // ComItem declarations across the CodeJock/Crystal wrappers, Variant vs String vs Integer,
            // ARE the 4544 all by themselves - and then no rename or align of ours can reach it.
            Get _DistinctTypeCount of Self aDf  to iDfTypes
            Move (SizeOfArray(aDf))  to iDfDecls
            Move (SizeOfArray(aOwn)) to iOwnDecls
            // DUPLICATE-EXTERNAL flavor: every declaration on BOTH sides is an External_Function -
            // the "already defined as an external_function" 4390 (the WinInet pair, LegacyApp
            // 2026-07-23). The name is not owned by "something else": both sides bind the SAME
            // Windows API, so the fix is delete-or-guard the duplicate, not a rename.
            // ONE of yours being an External_Function is enough - see _IsDuplicateExternalPair for
            // why requiring ALL of them sent a wrapper class's method to the rename lane.
            Get _IsDuplicateExternalPair of Self aOwn aDf to bExtDup
            If (sParam <> "") ;
                Set pbMismatchExternalDup to bExtDup
            Writeln channel iCh ("=== " + sNameOrig + " ===")
            // MIXED Overloaded usage is its OWN error - 4390 "this method was previously defined as
            // non-overloaded" - and it breaks the build whether or not anyone calls the name in an
            // expression. Neither 4544 fix addresses it, so say what it actually is.
            Get _OverloadedState of Self aOwn to iOvlState
            If (iOvlState = CI_OverloadMixed) Begin
                Writeln channel iCh "  (MIXED Overloaded usage - some declarations carry the Overloaded token and some do not."
                Writeln channel iCh "   That is error 4390, not 4544: DataFlex refuses to let an overloaded and a"
                Writeln channel iCh "   non-overloaded method share a name. Add the token to all of them, or to none.)"
            End
            If (bExtDup = True) ;
                Writeln channel iCh "  (duplicate External_Function - both sides declare the same Windows API)"
            If (bExtDup = False and (iOwnTypes >= 2 or iDfDecls > 0)) ;
                Writeln channel iCh "  (return-type mismatch - one name, more than one type)"
            // "your declarations agree with each other" needs declarations of yours to agree - with none
            // in the scanned source there is nothing to say that about (2026-08-19).
            If (bExtDup = False and iOwnTypes < 2 and iDfDecls = 0 and iOwnDecls > 0) ;
                Writeln channel iCh "  (name already in use - the workspace's declarations agree with each other; something else owns the name)"
            If (bExtDup = False and iOwnTypes < 2 and iDfDecls = 0 and iOwnDecls = 0) ;
                Writeln channel iCh "  (name already in use - nothing in the scanned source declares it; something outside it owns the name)"
            Writeln channel iCh ""
            If (iDfDecls = 0 and bScanFailed = True) Begin
                Writeln channel iCh ("  Could NOT read the target DataFlex's packages (" + psInstallScanPath(Self) + "),")
                Writeln channel iCh  "  so whether DataFlex declares this name is unknown - not confirmed absent."
                Writeln channel iCh ""
            End
            If (iDfDecls > 0) Begin
                Writeln channel iCh ("  DataFlex declares this name itself, in " + String(iDfDecls) + " place(s). You cannot change these:")
                For k from 0 to (iDfDecls - 1)
                    Writeln channel iCh ("      " + aDf[k].sKind + " returns " + aDf[k].sReturnType + "   " + aDf[k].sFile + " line " + String(aDf[k].iLine))
                Loop
                Writeln channel iCh ""
            End
            If (iOwnDecls > 0) Begin
                // "with 1 different return type(s)" was nonsense on a name whose declarations agree - and
                // that is the 4390 case, where the problem is the NAME, not the type (Nils 2026-07-22).
                If (iOwnTypes >= 2) ;
                    Writeln channel iCh ("  The workspace declares it in " + String(iOwnDecls) + " place(s), disagreeing over " + String(iOwnTypes) + " return types:")
                If (iOwnTypes < 2) ;
                    Writeln channel iCh ("  The workspace declares it in " + String(iOwnDecls) + " place(s), all agreeing on the return type:")
                For k from 0 to (iOwnDecls - 1)
                    Writeln channel iCh ("      " + aOwn[k].sKind + " returns " + aOwn[k].sReturnType + "   " + aOwn[k].sFile + " line " + String(aOwn[k].iLine))
                Loop
                Writeln channel iCh ""
            End
            If (aiCallCounts[i] > 0) Begin
                Writeln channel iCh ("  Status: ERRORING - about " + String(aiCallCounts[i]) + " expression call site(s) use this name.")
                Increment iErroring
            End
            Else Begin
                Writeln channel iCh "  Status: LATENT - no expression call site found (harmless until the name is used in one)."
                Increment iLatent
            End
            Writeln channel iCh ""
            // WHICH OF THE TWO FIXES APPLIES, decided here where both sides are known, rather than left
            // for the caller to guess. DataFlex owning the name means its type cannot move, so ours must
            // be renamed. Nothing outside owning it means a rename would carry the SAME disagreement to
            // the new name - there the fix is to align the odd one out (Nils 2026-07-22: pbSilent).
            Move "" to sMajority
            Set piMismatchDfDecls to iDfDecls
            If (iDfDecls > 0) ;
                Set psMismatchDfType to (aDf[0].sReturnType)
            If (iDfDecls = 0 and iOwnTypes >= 2) Begin
                Get _MajorityReturnType of Self aOwn to sMajority
                Set psMismatchMajorityType to sMajority
                If (sMajority <> "") Begin
                    Get pasReturnMismatchOdd to asMcOdd
                    For k from 0 to (iOwnDecls - 1)
                        If (Lowercase(Trim(aOwn[k].sReturnType)) <> Lowercase(sMajority)) Begin
                            Move ("Change this " + sNameOrig + " declaration from " + aOwn[k].sReturnType + " to " + sMajority) to sMcWhat
                            Move ("The other declaration(s) say " + sMajority + ", and nothing outside this workspace uses the name - so aligning this one clears the ambiguity without touching a single call site.") to sMcWhy
                            Move (aOwn[k].sFile + "|" + String(aOwn[k].iLine) + "|" + sMcWhat + "|" + sMcWhy) to asMcOdd[SizeOfArray(asMcOdd)]
                        End
                    Loop
                    Set pasReturnMismatchOdd to asMcOdd
                End
            End
            Writeln channel iCh "  What to do:"
            If (bExtDup = True) Begin
                // The "#"/"!" are Character()-built (35/33): a literal directive token in a .pkg
                // string is consumed by FMAC during OUR own compile (the #COMMAND lesson). The
                // version-check guard is deliberately the SAME one the guard converters write - it is
                // order-independent, where an "IFDEF get_" probe only works when DataFlex's
                // declaration happens to compile first (Nils 2026-07-23).
                Writeln channel iCh  "      DataFlex now ships this External_Function declaration itself. The workspace's declaration is a"
                Writeln channel iCh  "      duplicate of the same Windows API - delete it, or wrap it in the same"
                Writeln channel iCh  "      version guard the globals converter writes, so it only compiles on"
                Writeln channel iCh  "      releases where DataFlex does not declare it:"
                If (iGuardVer > 0) ;
                    Writeln channel iCh ("          " + Character(35) + "IF (" + Character(33) + "@ <= " + String(iGuardVer) + ")")
                If (iGuardVer = 0) ;
                    Writeln channel iCh ("          " + Character(35) + "IF (" + Character(33) + "@ <= <the workspace's version, e.g. 191>)")
                Writeln channel iCh  "              External_Function ... (the workspace's declaration)"
                Writeln channel iCh ("          " + Character(35) + "ENDIF")
                Writeln channel iCh  "      GuardDuplicateExternalFunctions applies exactly this, and the guided flow"
                Writeln channel iCh  "      offers to run it when this report closes."
                Writeln channel iCh  "      Mind the entry points before deleting: if the workspace's declaration binds the ANSI variant (...A)"
                Writeln channel iCh  "      and DataFlex's the wide one (...W), they are the same API for numeric"
                Writeln channel iCh  "      arguments - only calls passing text buffers need a closer look."
            End
            Else If (iDfDecls > 0 and iOwnDecls > 0) Begin
                // The common shape, and the one where the obvious fix is the wrong one.
                Writeln channel iCh ("      DataFlex holds this name as " + aDf[0].sReturnType + ", and that cannot change. Aligning the")
                Writeln channel iCh ("      " + String(iOwnDecls) + " declaration(s) above with EACH OTHER will NOT clear the error - they have to")
                Writeln channel iCh  "      stop using the name at all. Rename them, and the calls that target THOSE objects;"
                Writeln channel iCh  "      every other call site of the name then compiles untouched."
            End
            Else If (iOwnTypes >= 2) Begin
                Writeln channel iCh ("      These " + String(iOwnDecls) + " declaration(s) disagree with each other, and nothing outside this")
                Writeln channel iCh  "      workspace uses the name - so a RENAME would carry the same disagreement to the new"
                Writeln channel iCh  "      name and change nothing. Align them instead."
                If (sMajority <> "") Begin
                    Writeln channel iCh ("      Most of them say " + sMajority + ". Changing the odd one(s) above to match clears this")
                    Writeln channel iCh  "      without touching a single call site."
                End
                If (sMajority = "") ;
                    Writeln channel iCh  "      They split evenly, so there is no odd one out - decide which type is correct."
            End
            Else If (iOwnDecls > 0 and bScanFailed = True) Begin
                // NOT a finding - a folder we could not open. Saying "DataFlex does not declare it" here
                // would be inventing evidence out of a failed read (Nils 2026-07-22: DF 26 uninstalled
                // mid-session while the workspace still targeted it).
                Writeln channel iCh  "      CANNOT TELL. The target DataFlex's Pkg folder could not be read, so whether"
                Writeln channel iCh  "      DataFlex declares this name is unknown - and that is what decides between"
                Writeln channel iCh  "      renaming the workspace's declarations and aligning them."
                Writeln channel iCh ("      Looked in: " + psInstallScanPath(Self))
                Writeln channel iCh  "      Point the workspace at an installed DataFlex version and run this again."
            End
            Else If (iOwnDecls > 0) Begin
                Writeln channel iCh  "      The compiler rejects this name, but DataFlex does not declare it in its own Pkg"
                Writeln channel iCh  "      folder either - so it comes from the runtime, from a class method, or from an"
                Writeln channel iCh  "      External_Function. A version guard cannot help with those. RENAME the"
                Writeln channel iCh  "      declaration(s) above."
            End
            /* NOTHING OF YOURS DECLARES THE NAME (iOwnDecls = 0). Three states, and until 2026-08-19 all
               three fell into one catch-all that said "no declaration of this name was found in the
               scanned source ... Widen the scope, or check your Use graph."

               On Nils's WinServ that line was printed directly UNDER 51 declarations of ComItem, each with
               its file and line, in DataFlex 26's own cCJCommandBars / cCJGridControl / cCrystal* wrappers
               (Variant, String and Integer among them). Nothing was outside anything, there was no scope
               to widen, and the Use graph had nothing to do with it - while the dialog that opened seconds
               later, off the SAME data, said correctly that DataFlex owns the name and the call rewrite is
               the only fix. The ladder above keys on OUR declaration count alone, so the commonest shape
               of all - a COM-wrapper name you merely CALL - had no branch of its own.

               A name you never declare is not yours to rename or align, so all three branches end at the
               same place: the call sites. That is what the "Rewrite the calls" offer does, and it changes
               no declaration. */
            Else If (iDfDecls > 0) Begin
                If (iDfTypes >= 2) Begin
                    Writeln channel iCh ("      Nothing in the scanned source declares this name - DataFlex does, in the " + String(iDfDecls) + " place(s)")
                    Writeln channel iCh ("      listed above, and they do NOT agree on the return type (" + String(iDfTypes) + " different types between them).")
                    Writeln channel iCh  "      That side cannot change, so there is nothing here to rename or align."
                End
                Else Begin
                    Writeln channel iCh ("      Nothing in the scanned source declares this name - DataFlex does, in the " + String(iDfDecls) + " place(s)")
                    Writeln channel iCh ("      listed above, all returning " + aDf[0].sReturnType + ". That side cannot change, so there is nothing here")
                    Writeln channel iCh  "      to rename or align; whatever disagrees with it sits outside the scanned source."
                End
                Writeln channel iCh  "      The fix is to stop calling it from INSIDE AN EXPRESSION: each such call becomes a"
                Writeln channel iCh  "      Get statement on the line before, which is offered when this report closes"
                Writeln channel iCh  "      (ChangeAmbiguousCallToGet). No declaration is touched, and no return type changes."
            End
            Else If (bScanFailed = True) Begin
                Writeln channel iCh  "      CANNOT TELL. Nothing in the scanned source declares this name, and the target"
                Writeln channel iCh  "      DataFlex's Pkg folder could not be read either - so whether DataFlex declares it,"
                Writeln channel iCh  "      and with which types, is unknown."
                Writeln channel iCh ("      Looked in: " + psInstallScanPath(Self))
                Writeln channel iCh  "      Point the workspace at an installed DataFlex version and run this again. The"
                Writeln channel iCh  "      call-site rewrite offered when this report closes works whoever owns the name."
            End
            Else Begin
                Writeln channel iCh  "      No declaration was found - not in the scanned source, and not in the target"
                Writeln channel iCh  "      DataFlex's Pkg folder either. So the name comes from the runtime, from a class"
                Writeln channel iCh  "      method or an External_Function, or from a source folder that was not scanned."
                Writeln channel iCh  "      Nothing here can be renamed. If one of the workspace's libraries declares it, add that"
                Writeln channel iCh  "      folder to the scope and run this again so this report can name the declarations;"
                Writeln channel iCh  "      otherwise rewrite the call sites as Get statements (offered when this report closes)."
            End
            Writeln channel iCh ""
            // HAND THE DECLARATION SITES ON, don't just print them. The report answers "which declarations
            // disagree"; these rows make the answer walkable - double-click opens the file at the line. The
            // three lines here are the entire fix for a workspace-wide 4544 (Nils 2026-07-22).
            // How many of ours are Global - the hand-rename dialog describes the declarations, and
            // "outside any class" and "Global" are different properties that can appear in any mix.
            Move 0 to iGlobalDecls
            For k from 0 to (iOwnDecls - 1)
                If (aOwn[k].bGlobal = True) ;
                    Increment iGlobalDecls
            Loop
            Set piMismatchGlobalDecls to iGlobalDecls
            Get pasReturnMismatchDecls    to asMcDecls
            Get pasReturnMismatchOwnTypes to asMcTypes
            For k from 0 to (iOwnDecls - 1)
                If (bExtDup = True) Begin
                    Move ("Delete or version-guard this duplicate External_Function " + sNameOrig) to sMcWhat
                    If (iGuardVer > 0) ;
                        Move ("DataFlex ships the same Windows API declaration itself and that side cannot change - wrap this one in " + Character(35) + "IF (" + Character(33) + "@ <= " + String(iGuardVer) + ") ... " + Character(35) + "ENDIF (GuardDuplicateExternalFunctions applies it), or delete it.") to sMcWhy
                    If (iGuardVer = 0) ;
                        Move ("DataFlex ships the same Windows API declaration itself and that side cannot change - version-guard this one (GuardDuplicateExternalFunctions applies it), or delete it.") to sMcWhy
                End
                Else Begin
                    Move ("Rename this " + sNameOrig + " declaration (it returns " + aOwn[k].sReturnType + ")") to sMcWhat
                    If (iDfDecls > 0) ;
                        Move ("DataFlex ships " + sNameOrig + " as " + aDf[0].sReturnType + " in " + String(iDfDecls) + " place(s) and that cannot change, so aligning the workspace's declarations with each other will not clear the error.") to sMcWhy
                    If (iDfDecls = 0) ;
                        Move ("Declared with " + String(iOwnTypes) + " different return types in this workspace - align them all to one type, or rename the odd one out.") to sMcWhy
                End
                Move (aOwn[k].sFile + "|" + String(aOwn[k].iLine) + "|" + sMcWhat + "|" + sMcWhy) to asMcDecls[SizeOfArray(asMcDecls)]
                // The bare type beside it, for the even-split align offer (2026-09-03): the user picks the
                // type when no majority can, and needs to see what each declaration returns to choose.
                Move (aOwn[k].sFile + "|" + String(aOwn[k].iLine) + "|" + aOwn[k].sReturnType) to asMcTypes[SizeOfArray(asMcTypes)]
            Loop
            Set pasReturnMismatchDecls    to asMcDecls
            Set pasReturnMismatchOwnTypes to asMcTypes
            Increment iCollisions
        Loop
        If (iCollisions = 0) Begin
            Writeln channel iCh "(no name in this scope is declared with conflicting return types)"
            Writeln channel iCh ""
        End

        // ---- Legal overload sets: listed, never counted as collisions -----------------------------
        // These carry several return types under one name and are FINE - every declaration has the
        // Overloaded token, so DataFlex resolves the call by parameter count and no 4544 can arise
        // (measured on DF26, 2026-08-23). They are shown rather than dropped silently, because a
        // report that says nothing about what it skipped reads as "there was nothing there" - and
        // because DataFlex's own documentation calls overloading obsolete.
        Get _OverloadedNames of Self aAllDecls to asOvlNames
        Move (SizeOfArray(asOvlNames) - 1) to iOvlLast
        If (iOvlLast >= 0) Begin
            Writeln channel iCh ""
            Writeln channel iCh "--- Overloaded names (NOT errors, not counted above) ---"
            Writeln channel iCh "Each of these declares one name with more than one return type, and EVERY declaration"
            Writeln channel iCh "carries the Overloaded token - so DataFlex picks the right one by parameter count and"
            Writeln channel iCh "the name cannot raise error 4544. Nothing needs fixing. Listed only because DataFlex"
            Writeln channel iCh "documents overloading as obsolete: if you want to retire it, give the variants distinct"
            Writeln channel iCh "names or take a variable argument list with Num_Arguments. Neither 4544 fix applies here,"
            Writeln channel iCh "and both refuse to touch these names."
            Writeln channel iCh ""
            For m from 0 to iOvlLast
                Get _DeclsForName of Self aAllDecls asOvlNames[m] to aOvl
                Writeln channel iCh ("=== " + asOvlNames[m] + " (overloaded, " + String(SizeOfArray(aOvl)) + " declarations) ===")
                For k from 0 to (SizeOfArray(aOvl) - 1)
                    Writeln channel iCh ("    returns " + aOvl[k].sReturnType + "   " + aOvl[k].sFile + " (ln " + String(aOvl[k].iLine) + ")")
                Loop
                Writeln channel iCh ""
            Loop
        End

        If (bCancel = True) Begin
            Writeln channel iCh "*** Cancelled by the user - this report is INCOMPLETE. ***"
            Writeln channel iCh ""
        End
        // The tail names the overload sets too: a total that counted only collisions would leave the
        // reader wondering what the section above it was.
        Writeln channel iCh ("Total: " + String(iCollisions) + " name(s) examined [" + String(iErroring) + " erroring, " + String(iLatent) + " latent] across " + String(SizeOfArray(aAllDecls)) + " declaration(s) scanned, plus " + String(iOvlLast + 1) + " legal overloaded name(s) listed and not counted.")
        Send rfCloseOutput iCh

        If (iCollisions > 0) Begin
            Get pasOtherLogFiles to asOtherLogFiles
            Move sLogFile to asOtherLogFiles[SizeOfArray(asOtherLogFiles)]
            Set pasOtherLogFiles to asOtherLogFiles
        End
        Function_Return iCollisions
    End_Function

    /* GosubGotoReport - the Phase-1 worksheet for retiring legacy Gosub subroutines (and the
       Gotos interleaved with them; Nils 2026-07-09 - see the Gosub/Goto groundwork notes). The
       compiler flags Gosub (4533 "Obsolete command: Gosub") and the computed ON..GOSUB dispatch
       (4533 "Obsolete command: On"), but emits NOTHING for Goto - Goto is still legal DataFlex -
       so the report is where the developer gets the complete control-flow picture: which
       subroutines are mechanically extractable into Procedures, which need hand-untangling and
       WHY, plus every Goto with a concrete rewrite hint per class. The per-subroutine verdict is
       CONTROL-FLOW only: legacy flat modules share their variables across every subroutine, so
       an extraction still needs a variable-scope review (a Phase-2 concern). Analysis cores are
       pure + unit-tested: _GosubLineLabel / _GosubCallTargets / _GosubReturnKind / _GotoTarget /
       _GosubAuditFile (cRefactorFuncLib). Intra-subroutine goto/label pairs remain legal inside
       an extracted Procedure, so they never block a CANDIDATE verdict - only boundary-CROSSING
       gotos do.
    */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Reports only. Makes no changes to the source code.
        Lists every legacy Gosub subroutine (labels, calls, Gosub_Return lines and ON..GOSUB statements), and for each subroutine says whether it can be converted to a procedure (CANDIDATE) or must be changed by hand (MANUAL), and why.
        Each Goto gets a hint for how to rewrite it: a jump forward becomes an If/Else block, a jump backward becomes a While or Repeat loop, and a jump out of a block must be untangled first.
        The results are written to a log file; open it with Tools > Logs > Other Log Files.
        { MethodType = eReportFunctionAll }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: Gosub | Suggest }
        { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: On | Suggest }
        { SummaryText = Report: Gosub subroutines + Goto rewrite hints }
        """ }
    Function GosubGotoReport String[] ByRef asFiles String sParameter Returns Integer
        String sHomePath sLogFile sFilePath
        String[] asCode asReport asEmpty
        Integer iCh iFile iFileLast iTotal iN
        Boolean bPanel bCancel

        Get psHomeTarget of ghoApplication to sHomePath
        Move (vFolderFormat(sHomePath) + CS_BackupFolder + CS_DirSeparator + CS_GosubGotoReportLogFile) to sLogFile
        Get rfOpenOutput sLogFile to iCh
        If (iCh < 0) Begin
            Function_Return 0
        End

        Writeln channel iCh "Gosub / Goto usage report  (report only - no changes made)."
        Writeln channel iCh "Per subroutine (a label called via Gosub): CANDIDATE = its control flow is self-contained"
        Writeln channel iCh "and a Procedure extraction is mechanical; MANUAL = the listed reasons must be untangled"
        Writeln channel iCh "first. NOTE: the verdict is control-flow only - a legacy flat module shares its variables"
        Writeln channel iCh "across every subroutine, so review the extracted code's variable use in any case."
        Writeln channel iCh "The 'ConvertGosubToProcedure' refactoring function converts the CANDIDATE subset for you"
        Writeln channel iCh "(subroutine -> global Procedure, Gosub -> Send, ON..GOSUB -> Case) and promotes the shared"
        Writeln channel iCh "file-scope variables to Global_Variable, each tagged for review; MANUAL subs are left alone."
        Writeln channel iCh "Every Goto is classified with a rewrite hint. Goto is still legal DataFlex (the compiler"
        Writeln channel iCh "emits no warning for it) - the hints are for finishing a module by hand:"
        Writeln channel iCh "  forward skip       -> rewrite as an If/Else block around the skipped lines"
        Writeln channel iCh "  backward jump      -> a loop - rewrite as While / Repeat-Until"
        Writeln channel iCh "  boundary-crossing  -> untangle first; it BLOCKS converting that subroutine"
        Writeln channel iCh ""

        Move 0 to iTotal
        Move (SizeOfArray(asFiles)) to iFileLast
        Decrement iFileLast
        // This report loops files itself (eReportFunctionAll), so drive the status panel per
        // file - otherwise it looks frozen when this is the only function run.
        Get _ReportPanelStart iFileLast to bPanel
        For iFile from 0 to iFileLast
            Move asFiles[iFile] to sFilePath
            Send _ReportPanelFile bPanel "Gosub/Goto usage - File No:" "Mapping Gosub subroutines + Gotos:" sFilePath iFile iFileLast
            Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray(sFilePath)) to asCode
            Move asEmpty to asReport
            Get _GosubAuditFile (&asCode) (&asReport) to iN
            If (iN > 0) Begin
                Send _ReportWriteFindings iCh sFilePath asReport
                Add iN to iTotal
            End
            // Mid-run cancel (the Cancel button): a *All function gets ONE call for all files,
            // so the engine's per-file Cancel_Check cannot fire while we scan. Poll the safe
            // accessor (NEVER Cancel_Check - it consumes the signal) and bail; the engine's
            // outer Cancel_Check then runs the confirm pipeline once.
            Get pbUserCancelRequested of ghoEngine to bCancel
            If (bCancel = True) ;
                Move iFileLast to iFile
        Loop

        Send _ReportFinish iCh sLogFile iTotal bCancel bPanel " finding(s)."
        Function_Return iTotal
    End_Function

    /* GuardRedundantGlobalFunctions - wraps a file's OWN global Function/Procedure declarations that the
       TARGET DataFlex now ships NATIVELY in a compiler-version guard, so the home-brewed copy compiles only
       on the releases that lack the built-in. That clears duplicate-global error 4390 at the few
       DECLARATIONS instead of the (often hundreds of) callers. The emitted shape, the inclusive "<=" rule,
       the GLOBAL-keyword limit and the behaviour caution now live in the { Description } below.

       VERSION = sParameter when set, else the TARGET workspace's own DataFlex version (pTargetWS.sDfVersion,
       read from its .sws - JSON "df" or legacy [Properties] Version). Guessing is REFUSED: a blank or
       unparsable version changes nothing rather than writing a wrong number into someone's source.

       WHICH NAMES: the per-version catalogue of the target install's own Global function names
       (InstallGlobalFuncNames), so it follows the release the user actually targets instead of a hardcoded
       list. The GLOBAL keyword FOLLOWS the name (Function Utf8ToOem Global String s Returns String), which
       is why the match is positional - a same-named method inside a class never clashes with a global
       built-in, and a non-Global top-level function is not a backport at all. A global Set procedure goes by
       its two-word name, "Set Profile_Dword" (_GlobalDeclaredName).

       The emitted "#" and "!" are Character()-built (35 / 33). FMAC processes its directive/counter tokens
       BEFORE string-literal semantics exist, so a literal "#IF (!@ ..." held here would be evaluated during
       DFRefactor's OWN compile - writing our version into the output - exactly the |!C / #COMMAND lesson.

       (Was UnicodeRetireRedundantConversions, which COMMENTED OUT 10 hardcoded CharTranslate names.
       Commenting out makes the source compile only on the NEW release; a version guard keeps ONE tree
       building on both - what library code mid-migration actually needs. Nils 2026-07-20.)
    */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Puts the global functions in this file that the target DataFlex now provides itself inside an #IF (!@ <= version) check, so that they are only compiled in older versions. This fixes the duplicate-global error 4390 without changing any calls.
        The function is placed, unchanged and at its own indentation, between the version check and its #ENDIF. Nothing is deleted or rewritten; the change can be undone, and a function that already has the check is skipped when it runs again.
        The "<=" includes the workspace's own version on purpose: that version must still compile its own copy, because the DataFlex function does not exist there (a "< 191" check would break a 19.1 application). If the version cannot be read, nothing is changed, rather than writing a wrong number into the source code.
        Only top-level declarations with the GLOBAL keyword are changed, and only names that the target DataFlex itself includes. A method with the same name inside a class is not changed.
        Caution: in the newer version, every call then uses DataFlex's own function, which can differ in code page, error handling or special cases. This is why the function is a suggestion to review, not an automatic fix.
        { MethodType = eOtherFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4390 | OemToUtf8 | Suggest }
        { ModRecipe = 4390 | Utf8ToOem | Suggest }
        { ModRecipe = 4390 | AnsiToUtf8 | Suggest }
        { ModRecipe = 4390 | Utf8ToAnsi | Suggest }
        { ParamHelp = DataFlex version this code is kept for, e.g. 19.1 (191 or 26 also accepted). Leave blank to use the selected workspace's own version. }
        { SummaryText = Version-guarded global functions that DataFlex now provides natively }
        """ }
    Function GuardRedundantGlobalFunctions String[] ByRef asCode String sParameter Returns Integer
        String[] asNative
        String sVerText
        Integer iVer iGuarded
        tWorkspacePaths WS

        // Version boundary: the parameter wins, else the selected TARGET workspace's own DataFlex version
        // (from its .sws - JSON "df" or legacy [Properties] Version). Blank/unparsable = REFUSE, never
        // guess: writing a wrong version into someone's source is worse than changing nothing.
        Move (Trim(sParameter)) to sVerText
        If (sVerText = "") Begin
            Get pTargetWS of ghoApplication to WS
            Move WS.sDfVersion to sVerText
        End
        Get DfVersionToMacroNumber sVerText to iVer
        If (iVer = 0) Begin
            // The refusal is right - a wrong version written into someone else's source is worse
            // than no change - but it has to SAY so, or the caller invents a cause. sVerText is
            // whatever the target workspace .sws offered, so quoting it points straight at it.
            Set psGuardSkipReason to ("the target workspace does not state a DataFlex version this tool can read - got " + (If(sVerText = "", "nothing at all", (Character(34) + sVerText + Character(34)))) + ". The guard directive needs a version number to write.")
            Function_Return 0
        End
        // The Global function names the TARGET install itself ships (per-version catalogue), so this
        // follows the release the user actually targets instead of a hardcoded list. RunNativeGlobalFuncNames
        // (not InstallGlobalFuncNames) because this runs PER FILE: the underlying call re-probes and
        // re-parses the cache JSON every time, which on a big workspace stalled the run between files.
        Get RunNativeGlobalFuncNames of Self to asNative
        Get _GuardGlobalsInLines (&asCode) asNative iVer sVerText to iGuarded
        Function_Return iGuarded
    End_Function

    /* LegacyDbApiReport - inventories the legacy Database-API attribute COMMANDS (File_Size,
       Size_of_Field, Get_Current_Lockcount, Get_date_format, Get_number_format, Get_filelist,
       Set_filelist, Get_Date_Attribute, Ifchange) and, for each site, names the modern
       Get_Attribute / Set_Attribute replacement. REPORT ONLY - no source changes. Most deliberately
       get no auto-converter: File_Size has a READ form and a SET-max form with reversed args; Ifchange
       is structural (an If-wrapped Get_Attribute DF_FILE_CHANGED); Size_of_Field is ambiguous
       (DF_FIELD_LENGTH vs DF_FIELD_PRECISION); Get_Date_Attribute reads Y2K/epoch no-op state INTO a
       variable. Get_date_format is the exception since 2026-09-15 - ChangeGetDateFormatToAttribute
       converts it, value-exact - and stays listed here so the worksheet is the whole inventory.
       So this worksheet lists the sites for a hand migration. Logs to the Backup folder;
       surfaced via the 'Other Log Files' toolbar button. Mirrors TypeToStructReport. Matched at
       COMMAND position (first word, on a strings/comment-masked split) so a mention never flags. */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Reports only. Makes no changes to the source code.
        Lists the legacy database attribute commands (File_Size, Size_of_Field, Get_Current_Lockcount, Get_date_format, Get_number_format, Get_filelist, Set_filelist, Get_Date_Attribute and Ifchange), and for each line names the modern Get_Attribute or Set_Attribute replacement.
        These lines must be migrated by hand, because they have several forms, change the structure of the code, or are ambiguous, so nothing is changed here. Get_date_format has its own converter, ChangeGetDateFormatToAttribute.
        The results are written to a log file; open it with Tools > Logs > Other Log Files.
        { MethodType = eReportFunctionAll }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Report: legacy Database-API commands -> modern Get_Attribute/Set_Attribute sites }
        """ }
    Function LegacyDbApiReport String[] ByRef asFiles String sParameter Returns Integer
        String sHomePath sLogFile sFilePath
        String[] asCode asReport asEmpty
        Integer iCh iFile iFileLast iTotal iN
        Boolean bPanel bCancel

        Get psHomeTarget of ghoApplication to sHomePath
        Move (vFolderFormat(sHomePath) + CS_BackupFolder + CS_DirSeparator + CS_LegacyDbApiReportLogFile) to sLogFile
        Get rfOpenOutput sLogFile to iCh
        If (iCh < 0) Begin
            Function_Return 0
        End

        Writeln channel iCh "Legacy Database-API command / constant worksheet  (report only - no changes made)."
        Writeln channel iCh "Each maps to a modern Get_Attribute / Set_Attribute call, but NOT by a simple token swap -"
        Writeln channel iCh "every one needs a hand migration (see the per-command note). This ALSO lists the obsolete DB-file"
        Writeln channel iCh "constants / predefined variables (Fieldindex, Filenumber, DF_FILE_PRIMARY_INDEX_TRIGGER,"
        Writeln channel iCh "DF_FILE_TRANSLATE_OEM_TO_ANSI) matched anywhere on the line. This lists the sites."
        Writeln channel iCh ""

        Move 0 to iTotal
        Move (SizeOfArray(asFiles)) to iFileLast
        Decrement iFileLast
        // This report loops files itself (eReportFunctionAll), so drive the status panel per file -
        // otherwise it looks frozen when this is the only function run. The scan is one call per file
        // (the pure _LegacyDbApiScanFile core), so only the TOTAL bar advances, like GosubGotoReport.
        Get _ReportPanelStart iFileLast to bPanel
        For iFile from 0 to iFileLast
            Move asFiles[iFile] to sFilePath
            Send _ReportPanelFile bPanel "Legacy DB-API - File No:" "Scanning Database-API commands:" sFilePath iFile iFileLast
            Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray(sFilePath)) to asCode
            Move asEmpty to asReport
            Get _LegacyDbApiScanFile of Self (&asCode) (&asReport) to iN
            If (iN > 0) Begin
                Send _ReportWriteFindings iCh sFilePath asReport
                Add iN to iTotal
            End
            // Mid-run cancel: a *All function gets ONE call for all files, so the engine's per-file
            // Cancel_Check cannot fire while we scan. Poll the safe accessor (NEVER Cancel_Check - it
            // consumes the signal) and bail; the engine's outer Cancel_Check then confirms once.
            Get pbUserCancelRequested of ghoEngine to bCancel
            If (bCancel = True) ;
                Move iFileLast to iFile
        Loop

        Send _ReportFinish iCh sLogFile iTotal bCancel bPanel " legacy Database-API command / constant site(s)."
        Function_Return iTotal
    End_Function

    /* TechStackReadinessReport (Nils 2026-11-20) - where a workspace uses what DataFlex's TechStack, the DataFlex 27
       previews, changes or does not run yet: per topic, with its sites file by file. REPORT ONLY. The topics, their
       text and the DAW page each comes from are cRefactorFuncLib._TechStackTopics / _TechStackReportLines; the scan
       is the PURE _TechStackScanFile. Nils chose a report over analyzer rows: the data layer alone touches nearly
       every line of a Windows application, which would bury the grid. The summary comes first, so the sites are
       held until every file is read. A file counts as Windows UI by its extension (.vw .dg .sl .rv), or as a .src
       that names Start_UI. */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Reports only. Makes no changes to the source code.
        Counts, for each topic and each file, where the workspace uses something that DataFlex's TechStack (the DataFlex 27 previews) changes:
        - the data layer: Open, Relate, Add_Server_File, Make_File, File_Number, Main_File, Validate_Save, Field_Validate_msg, Entry_Item <table>.<column> and the table commands;
        - cHttpTransfer;
        - what TechStack does not support yet: the Windows user interface, COM/ActiveX, SOAP web services, and External_Function outside Windows;
        - a String moved into a Boolean, which TechStack converts differently.
        Each topic names the Data Access page it is based on. TechStack is a preview, so read the report as a list of where the work is, not as a final verdict.
        The results are written to a log file; open it with Tools > Logs > Other Log Files.
        { MethodType = eReportFunctionAll }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Report: what TechStack (DataFlex 27 previews) changes, counted per topic and file }
        """ }
    Function TechStackReadinessReport String[] ByRef asFiles String sParameter Returns Integer
        String sHomePath sLogFile sFilePath sExt
        String[] asCode asReport asEmpty asSections asHead
        Integer[] aiSites aiFiles aiBefore
        Integer iCh iFile iFileLast iTotal iN iTopic i iRead
        Boolean bPanel bCancel bWindowsUI

        Get psHomeTarget of ghoApplication to sHomePath
        Move (vFolderFormat(sHomePath) + CS_BackupFolder + CS_DirSeparator + CS_TechStackReadinessLogFile) to sLogFile
        For iTopic from 0 to (CI_TSTopicCount - 1)
            Move 0 to aiSites[iTopic]
            Move 0 to aiFiles[iTopic]
        Loop
        Move 0 to iTotal
        Move 0 to iRead
        Move False to bCancel
        Move (SizeOfArray(asFiles) - 1) to iFileLast
        // This report loops files itself (eReportFunctionAll), so it drives the status panel per file.
        Get _ReportPanelStart iFileLast to bPanel
        For iFile from 0 to iFileLast
            Move asFiles[iFile] to sFilePath
            Send _ReportPanelFile bPanel "TechStack - File No:" "Counting what TechStack changes:" sFilePath iFile iFileLast
            Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray(sFilePath)) to asCode
            Increment iRead
            Move (Lowercase(ParseFileExtension(sFilePath))) to sExt
            Move (sExt = "vw" or sExt = "dg" or sExt = "sl" or sExt = "rv") to bWindowsUI
            If (bWindowsUI = False and sExt = "src") ;
                Get _FileContainsText (&asCode) "start_ui" to bWindowsUI
            Move aiSites to aiBefore
            Move asEmpty to asReport
            Get _TechStackScanFile (&asCode) bWindowsUI (&aiSites) (&asReport) to iN
            If (iN > 0) Begin
                For iTopic from 0 to (CI_TSTopicCount - 1)
                    If (aiSites[iTopic] > aiBefore[iTopic]) ;
                        Move (aiFiles[iTopic] + 1) to aiFiles[iTopic]
                Loop
                Move ("=== " + sFilePath + " ===") to asSections[SizeOfArray(asSections)]
                For i from 0 to (SizeOfArray(asReport) - 1)
                    Move asReport[i] to asSections[SizeOfArray(asSections)]
                Loop
                Move "" to asSections[SizeOfArray(asSections)]
                Add iN to iTotal
            End
            // Mid-run cancel: the safe accessor, NEVER Cancel_Check (it consumes the signal).
            Get pbUserCancelRequested of ghoEngine to bCancel
            If (bCancel = True) ;
                Move iFileLast to iFile
        Loop

        Get rfOpenOutput sLogFile to iCh
        If (iCh < 0) Begin
            Function_Return 0
        End
        Get _TechStackReportLines (&aiSites) (&aiFiles) iRead to asHead
        For i from 0 to (SizeOfArray(asHead) - 1)
            Writeln channel iCh asHead[i]
        Loop
        For i from 0 to (SizeOfArray(asSections) - 1)
            Writeln channel iCh asSections[i]
        Loop
        Send _ReportFinish iCh sLogFile iTotal bCancel bPanel " site(s) where the workspace uses something TechStack changes."
        Function_Return iTotal
    End_Function

    /* LegacyDfPathReport - inventories the obsolete Set_dfpath command (char-mode only; it REPLACED the
       whole DFPATH environment variable). REPORT ONLY - no source changes. Modern DataFlex assembles the
       runtime search path in the cWorkspace object: psSystemDfPath (the system Lib/Usr dirs plus any you
       add) feeds psDfPath during DoAssignPaths. So there is no safe 1:1 rewrite - the fix is a per-site
       judgement: most legacy calls are redundant now (the .ws config sets the paths -> delete); to ADD a
       search dir, append to psSystemDfPath; a literal wholesale replace maps to Set psDfPath but that
       wipes the system dirs. The three options are spelled out in the report header; each site gets a
       one-liner. The scan core is the pure cRefactorFuncLib._LegacyDfPathScanFile (unit-tested); this
       wrapper does the file I/O. Mirrors LegacySqlReport. */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Reports only. Makes no changes to the source code.
        Lists the obsolete Set_dfpath command, which replaced the whole DFPATH and was used in character mode, and for each line explains the modern cWorkspace alternative:
        remove the line (the paths come from the .ws configuration), add a folder to psSystemDfPath, or, rarely, Set psDfPath to replace the whole path, which also removes the system folders.
        Each line needs its own decision, not a simple replacement, so nothing is changed.
        The results are written to a log file; open it with Tools > Logs > Other Log Files.
        { MethodType = eReportFunctionAll }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Report: obsolete Set_dfpath -> cWorkspace pathing (psSystemDfPath / psDfPath) sites }
        """ }
    Function LegacyDfPathReport String[] ByRef asFiles String sParameter Returns Integer
        String sHomePath sLogFile sFilePath
        String[] asCode asReport asEmpty
        Integer iCh iFile iFileLast iTotal iN
        Boolean bPanel bCancel

        Get psHomeTarget of ghoApplication to sHomePath
        Move (vFolderFormat(sHomePath) + CS_BackupFolder + CS_DirSeparator + CS_LegacyDfPathReportLogFile) to sLogFile
        Get rfOpenOutput sLogFile to iCh
        If (iCh < 0) Begin
            Function_Return 0
        End

        Writeln channel iCh "Obsolete Set_dfpath worksheet  (report only - no changes made)."
        Writeln channel iCh "Set_dfpath REPLACED the whole DFPATH environment variable (char-mode DataFlex only). Modern"
        Writeln channel iCh "DataFlex assembles the runtime search path in the cWorkspace object: psSystemDfPath (the system"
        Writeln channel iCh "Lib/Usr dirs plus any you add) feeds psDfPath during DoAssignPaths. There is no safe 1:1 rewrite;"
        Writeln channel iCh "pick per site:"
        Writeln channel iCh "  1. DELETE - usually redundant now: the .ws / .sws workspace config already defines the paths."
        Writeln channel iCh "  2. ADD a dir - append to psSystemDfPath, e.g."
        Writeln channel iCh "       Set psSystemDfPath of (phoWorkspace(ghoApplication)) to (psSystemDfPath(phoWorkspace(ghoApplication)) + ';' + <dir>)"
        Writeln channel iCh "  3. TRUE wholesale replace (rare) - Set psDfPath of (phoWorkspace(ghoApplication)) to <value>,"
        Writeln channel iCh "       but that WIPES the Lib/Usr system dirs the runtime needs - almost never what you want."
        Writeln channel iCh ""

        Move 0 to iTotal
        Move (SizeOfArray(asFiles)) to iFileLast
        Decrement iFileLast
        // This report loops files itself (eReportFunctionAll), so drive the status panel per file. The
        // scan is one call per file (the pure _LegacyDfPathScanFile core), so only the TOTAL bar advances,
        // like LegacySqlReport.
        Get _ReportPanelStart iFileLast to bPanel
        For iFile from 0 to iFileLast
            Move asFiles[iFile] to sFilePath
            Send _ReportPanelFile bPanel "Set_dfpath - File No:" "Scanning Set_dfpath:" sFilePath iFile iFileLast
            Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray(sFilePath)) to asCode
            Move asEmpty to asReport
            Get _LegacyDfPathScanFile of Self (&asCode) (&asReport) to iN
            If (iN > 0) Begin
                Send _ReportWriteFindings iCh sFilePath asReport
                Add iN to iTotal
            End
            // Mid-run cancel: poll the safe accessor (NEVER Cancel_Check - it consumes the signal)
            // and bail; the engine's outer Cancel_Check then confirms once (see CLAUDE.md).
            Get pbUserCancelRequested of ghoEngine to bCancel
            If (bCancel = True) ;
                Move iFileLast to iFile
        Loop

        Send _ReportFinish iCh sLogFile iTotal bCancel bPanel " obsolete Set_dfpath site(s)."
        Function_Return iTotal
    End_Function

    /* CharacterModeInventoryReport - what a DataFlex 3.x / console-mode program carries that DataFlex
       26 has no equivalent for (Nils 2026-09-20, on taking on character-mode workspaces). REPORT
       ONLY - no source changes. The guided Check runs it silently for such a program and shows the
       totals in its notice; the notice's chip opens this log. The scan core is the pure
       cRefactorFuncLib._CharModeInventoryScanFile (unit-tested); this wrapper does the file I/O and
       publishes the totals on pCharModeInventory. Mirrors LegacyDfPathReport. */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Reports only. Makes no changes to the source code.
        Lists what a DataFlex character-mode program contains that has no equivalent in DataFlex 26: screen images (/name ... /*), screen and keyboard commands (Page, Accept, Entry, Gotoxy ...), user interface objects (Entry_Form, Entry_List, Menu, Query ...) and Keyproc handlers, for each file, with totals.
        Those parts must be rewritten by hand; the rest of the code can be modernized with DFRefactor.
        The results are written to a log file; open it with Tools > Logs > Other Log Files.
        { MethodType = eReportFunctionAll }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Report: character-mode inventory (images, screen commands, UI objects, Keyprocs) }
        """ }
    Function CharacterModeInventoryReport String[] ByRef asFiles String sParameter Returns Integer
        String sHomePath sLogFile sFilePath
        String[] asCode asReport asEmpty
        tCharModeInventory Inv
        Integer iCh iFile iFileLast iTotal iN
        Boolean bPanel bCancel

        Set pCharModeInventory to Inv          // zeroed until the scan below has run
        Get psHomeTarget of ghoApplication to sHomePath
        Move (vFolderFormat(sHomePath) + CS_BackupFolder + CS_DirSeparator + CS_CharModeInventoryLogFile) to sLogFile
        Get rfOpenOutput sLogFile to iCh
        If (iCh < 0) ;
            Function_Return 0

        Writeln channel iCh "Character-mode inventory  (report only - no changes made)."
        Writeln channel iCh ""
        Writeln channel iCh "This program was written for DataFlex character mode (it starts with Use UI)."
        Writeln channel iCh "DataFlex 26 has no character mode, so the program cannot be compiled by DataFlex 26 until"
        Writeln channel iCh "it has been rewritten as a Windows program (one that starts with Use DFAllEnt.pkg)."
        Writeln channel iCh ""
        Writeln channel iCh "Most of the code can be modernised automatically by this tool. The screen handling cannot:"
        Writeln channel iCh "it has to be rewritten by hand. This report lists every place that needs that hand work."
        Writeln channel iCh "  IMAGES           the screen layouts, written as /name ... /*"
        Writeln channel iCh "                   -> become dbViews and dbForms (layouts used for printing can stay, for Output)"
        Writeln channel iCh "  SCREEN COMMANDS  Page, Accept, Entry, Entry_Display, Gotoxy, Show, Cls and the like"
        Writeln channel iCh "                   -> replaced by data-entry objects, or simply removed"
        Writeln channel iCh "  UI OBJECTS       Entry_Form, Entry_List, Menu, Query, Selection_List and the like"
        Writeln channel iCh "                   -> become dbView, dbForm and dbGrid objects, and CodeJock menus"
        Writeln channel iCh "  KEYPROC          keyboard handlers"
        Writeln channel iCh "                   -> become OnKey / On_Key procedures on the object"
        Writeln channel iCh "The counts below show how much of this program has to be rewritten by hand. Everything else -"
        Writeln channel iCh "Chain, Status, If-Not, Insert, Gosub, indicators, old comparison words - this tool changes for you."
        Writeln channel iCh ""

        Move 0 to iTotal
        Move (SizeOfArray(asFiles)) to iFileLast
        Decrement iFileLast
        Get _ReportPanelStart iFileLast to bPanel
        For iFile from 0 to iFileLast
            Move asFiles[iFile] to sFilePath
            Send _ReportPanelFile bPanel "Character-mode inventory - File No:" "Counting the screen layer:" sFilePath iFile iFileLast
            Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray(sFilePath)) to asCode
            Move asEmpty to asReport
            Get _CharModeInventoryScanFile of Self (&asCode) (&asReport) (&Inv) to iN
            If (iN > 0) Begin
                Send _ReportWriteFindings iCh sFilePath asReport
                Add iN to iTotal
            End
            // Mid-run cancel: poll the safe accessor (NEVER Cancel_Check - it consumes the signal).
            Get pbUserCancelRequested of ghoEngine to bCancel
            If (bCancel = True) ;
                Move iFileLast to iFile
        Loop

        Set pCharModeInventory to Inv
        Send _ReportFinish iCh sLogFile iTotal bCancel bPanel (" site(s) in " + String(Inv.iFiles) + " file(s): " + String(Inv.iImages) + " image(s), " + String(Inv.iScreenCmds) + " screen / keyboard command(s), " + String(Inv.iUiObjects) + " UI object(s), " + String(Inv.iKeyprocs) + " Keyproc handler(s).")
        Function_Return iTotal
    End_Function

    /* LegacyFieldOptionsReport - inventories the character-mode field ENTRY OPTIONS (autofind, capslock,
       displayonly, findreq, forceput, noenter, noput, skipfound, retainall, zero_suppress, autofind_ge) and
       names the modern DataDictionary Field_Options target per site. REPORT ONLY - no source changes: the
       modern equivalent lives in the DataDictionary CLASS (you set Field_Options / DD_* on the DD field),
       a CROSS-FILE change away from the old inline declaration, so there is no safe in-place rewrite. The
       scan is a boundary-aware token match (_FindKeywordPos, same as the Deep Scan grid) - it can in
       principle hit a same-named identifier; harmless for a read-only report (noted in the header). The
       scan core is the pure cRefactorFuncLib._LegacyFieldOptionsScanFile (unit-tested); this wrapper does
       the file I/O. Only the ENABLED entry-option Deep Scan rules are covered (the generic/noisy ones stay
       Explain). Mirrors LegacyDfPathReport. */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Reports only. Makes no changes to the source code.
        Lists the character-mode field options (autofind, capslock, displayonly, findreq, forceput, noenter, noput, skipfound, retainall, zero_suppress and autofind_ge), and for each line names the modern Field_Options setting in the data dictionary.
        The modern setting belongs in the data dictionary class, which is a change in another file, so nothing is changed.
        The results are written to a log file; open it with Tools > Logs > Other Log Files.
        { MethodType = eReportFunctionAll }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Report: char-mode field entry options -> DataDictionary Field_Options sites }
        """ }
    Function LegacyFieldOptionsReport String[] ByRef asFiles String sParameter Returns Integer
        String sHomePath sLogFile sFilePath
        String[] asCode asReport asEmpty
        Integer iCh iFile iFileLast iTotal iN
        Boolean bPanel bCancel

        Get psHomeTarget of ghoApplication to sHomePath
        Move (vFolderFormat(sHomePath) + CS_BackupFolder + CS_DirSeparator + CS_LegacyFieldOptionsReportLogFile) to sLogFile
        Get rfOpenOutput sLogFile to iCh
        If (iCh < 0) Begin
            Function_Return 0
        End

        Writeln channel iCh "Character-mode field entry-options worksheet  (report only - no changes made)."
        Writeln channel iCh "The old inline field options (autofind, noenter, displayonly, ...) are set on the modern side in"
        Writeln channel iCh "the DataDictionary CLASS via Field_Options / the DD_* constants (e.g. Set_Field_Options DD_NOENTER)."
        Writeln channel iCh "That is a CROSS-FILE change (the DD, not the declaration), so nothing is rewritten - this just lists"
        Writeln channel iCh "each site with its target. Matching is a boundary-aware token scan, so a same-named identifier could"
        Writeln channel iCh "occasionally appear here; verify each against its context."
        Writeln channel iCh ""

        Move 0 to iTotal
        Move (SizeOfArray(asFiles)) to iFileLast
        Decrement iFileLast
        // This report loops files itself (eReportFunctionAll), so drive the status panel per file. The
        // scan is one call per file (the pure _LegacyFieldOptionsScanFile core), so only the TOTAL bar
        // advances, like LegacyDfPathReport.
        Get _ReportPanelStart iFileLast to bPanel
        For iFile from 0 to iFileLast
            Move asFiles[iFile] to sFilePath
            Send _ReportPanelFile bPanel "Field options - File No:" "Scanning field entry options:" sFilePath iFile iFileLast
            Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray(sFilePath)) to asCode
            Move asEmpty to asReport
            Get _LegacyFieldOptionsScanFile of Self (&asCode) (&asReport) to iN
            If (iN > 0) Begin
                Send _ReportWriteFindings iCh sFilePath asReport
                Add iN to iTotal
            End
            // Mid-run cancel: poll the safe accessor (NEVER Cancel_Check - it consumes the signal)
            // and bail; the engine's outer Cancel_Check then confirms once (see CLAUDE.md).
            Get pbUserCancelRequested of ghoEngine to bCancel
            If (bCancel = True) ;
                Move iFileLast to iFile
        Loop

        Send _ReportFinish iCh sLogFile iTotal bCancel bPanel " character-mode field entry-option site(s)."
        Function_Return iTotal
    End_Function

    /* LegacyRegistryReport - inventories the legacy Windows-Registry / profile-string / INI COMMANDS
       (Reg_Open_Key / Reg_Close_Key / Reg_Create_Key / Reg_Enum_* / Reg_*_Access_Rights, the
       Get/Set_Profile_String family, Get/Set_Registry_Root, Read_dfini) and names the modern class
       for each. REPORT ONLY - no source changes. The docs point every one at the cRegistry class
       (Read_dfini -> cIniFile) but give no 1:1 method map, because cRegistry is an OBJECT that holds
       the key path in properties - not the legacy Reg_Open_Key -> hKey -> Reg_Close_Key handle model,
       and the Reg_Enum_* enumerations used callbacks that have no modern equivalent (loop rewrite).
       So this worksheet lists the sites + the migration shape for a hand port. Get_Program_Key is
       left Explain (no modern equivalent). The scan core is the pure cRefactorFuncLib.
       _LegacyRegistryScanFile (unit-tested); this wrapper does the file I/O. Mirrors LegacyDbApiReport. */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Reports only. Makes no changes to the source code.
        Lists the legacy commands for the Windows registry, profile strings and INI files (Reg_Open_Key, Reg_Enum_Value, Get/Set_Profile_String, Get/Set_Registry_Root, Read_dfini ...), and for each line names the modern class to use: cRegistry, or cIniFile for Read_dfini.
        cRegistry works with objects, not with the legacy handles and callbacks, so these lines must be changed by hand.
        The results are written to a log file; open it with Tools > Logs > Other Log Files.
        { MethodType = eReportFunctionAll }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Report: legacy Registry / profile-string / INI commands -> cRegistry / cIniFile sites }
        """ }
    Function LegacyRegistryReport String[] ByRef asFiles String sParameter Returns Integer
