        Get _ReportPanelStart iFileLast to bPanel
        For iFile from 0 to iFileLast
            Move asFiles[iFile] to sFilePath
            Send _ReportPanelFile bPanel "Legacy SQL - File No:" "Scanning Embedded SQL commands:" sFilePath iFile iFileLast
            Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray(sFilePath)) to asCode
            Move asEmpty to asReport
            Get _LegacySqlScanFile of Self (&asCode) (&asReport) to iN
            If (iN > 0) Begin
                Send _ReportWriteFindings iCh sFilePath asReport
                Add iN to iTotal
            End
            // Mid-run cancel: poll the safe accessor (NEVER Cancel_Check - it consumes the signal)
            // and bail; the engine's outer Cancel_Check then confirms once (see CLAUDE.md).
            Get pbUserCancelRequested of ghoEngine to bCancel
            If (bCancel = True) ;
                Move iFileLast to iFile
        Loop

        Send _ReportFinish iCh sLogFile iTotal bCancel bPanel " legacy Embedded SQL command site(s)."
        Function_Return iTotal
    End_Function

    /* MakeFileAutonomous - gives a view / dialog / report / sortlist a "Use <package>" for every
       class it instantiates, so the file compiles on its own instead of relying on whatever the
       .src happened to include first.

       The class -> package map is BUILT FROM CONTENT, never guessed from the name: of the 1,208
       class definitions in DataFlex 25's Pkg folder only 24% live in a file named after the class,
       so "Use cFoo.pkg" for class cFoo is wrong three times in four. The index scans the target
       install's Pkg plus the workspace's own source and libraries, each package read once.

       .src AND .pkg ARE IN SCOPE SINCE 2026-08-28 (Nils): a package instantiates classes as much
       as a view does, and a program file's autonomy matters for the same reason. That decision
       retired the 2026-07-31 ".src is never touched" rule - a Use added to a .src once changed
       which file first pulled a package in, a runtime error 98 after a clean compile - whose
       lesson is mitigated rather than forgotten: new lines go after the LAST existing Use rather
       than at the top, so everything already included keeps its order, at column zero with no
       #IFDEF wrapper - the level every other Use in these files sits at - and the Check loop
       compile-verifies every run.

       Ambiguity is not a theoretical case: a workspace using both VDFQuery and vwin32fh has
       vWin32fh's classes twice over, and choosing wrongly compiles the wrong implementation - so a
       class found in more than one package is declined and reported rather than guessed at.

       The user-facing half of this note (where the lines land, and what gets declined) now lives in
       the { Description }, which is what the manual is generated from.
       THE COMPILE ERRORS IT FIXES (2026-11-13). A class the file uses with no Use bringing its package in is
       error 4345 "Type check error U_<class>, MUST BE DEFINED" and 4328 "Undefined symbol in argument U_<class>"
       on the line that names it. One workspace's dbList subclass package ("Class cMyDbList is a DBList") had no
       Use for Dfsellst.pkg - DataFlex 26's DfAllEnt no longer brings it - and nothing offered this function for
       it: the two ModRecipe tags below do. The analyzer dry-runs it per file (VerifyAutoFixWholeFile), so a row
       is offered only where it would add a Use. */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Adds a 'Use <package>' for every class that a view, dialog, report, sort list, package or program creates objects of, so that the file does not depend on other files to compile.
        Which package defines a class is read from the DataFlex installation and the workspace's own packages, never guessed from the class name.
        The new Use lines are added after the last existing Use line, at the start of the line and without #IFDEF, so the order in which files are included does not change.
        A class defined in the file itself needs no Use line. A class that is not found, or that is defined in more than one package (choosing the wrong one would compile the wrong code), is skipped, and the run summary names every class that was skipped.
        { MethodType = eOtherFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { EnumList = "ANY" }
        { InitialValue = vw;dg;rv;sl;pkg;src }
        { ParamHelp = The file extensions to act on, separated by ';' - leading dots optional;
        \nvw;dg;rv;sl;pkg;src - the default: views, dialogs, reports, lookup-lists, packages, programs,
        \nvw;dg - views and dialogs only.
        \nA blank parameter falls back to the default. New Use lines land after the file's LAST
        \nexisting Use, so the include order of everything already there is unchanged. }
        { ModRecipe = 4345 | error u_ | AutoFix }
        { ModRecipe = 4328 | argument u_ | AutoFix }
        { SummaryText = Added: Use statements for instantiated classes }
        """ }
    Function MakeFileAutonomous String[] ByRef asCode String sParameter Returns Integer
        String[] asExtra asAmbiguous asUnknown asSeen
        String sThisFile sInstallPkg sKey
        Handle hoIdx
        Integer iCount i iMax

        // Scope by extension - the normalisation, the vw;dg;rv;sl;pkg;src default and the
        // 2026-08-28 retirement of the .src hard-block all live in _MfaExtensionInScope,
        // where the unit tests reach them.
        Get psCurrentSourceFileName of ghoEngine to sThisFile
        If (_MfaExtensionInScope(Self, sThisFile, sParameter) = False) ;
            Function_Return 0

        // The run's class -> package index: the install's Pkg plus the SELECTED source folders, built
        // once per run (_RunClassPackageIndex in cRefactorFuncLib - shared with the grid converters).
        Get _RunClassPackageIndex to hoIdx
        Get AddMissingUses of hoIdx (&asCode) (&asAmbiguous) (&asUnknown) to iCount

        /* Account for what was declined - a silent skip and a clean file look identical
           otherwise.

           ADDRESSED TO ghoStatusLog, not sent bare (Nils 2026-08-03). Log_Status is
           implemented on cRefactorStatusLog, and inherited by the engine from
           BusinessProcess - the FUNCLIB is neither, so a bare Send delegated up
           oRefactorFuncLib -> oClientArea -> oMain -> DESKTOP, found no handler, and the
           desktop raised an error. These two lines were the only Log_Status calls in the
           whole library and they produced 38 of the 40 errors in the first headless run
           over Order Entry: one per declined class, on a path that only fires when there
           IS something to report, which is why it survived every clean run.

           Guarded on ghoStatusLog because the funclib is also driven directly by the
           DFUnit runner, which builds no engine and therefore no status log. */
        If ((SizeOfArray(asAmbiguous) > 0 or SizeOfArray(asUnknown) > 0) and ghoStatusLog <> 0) Begin
            /* ONE NOTE PER CLASS, not per USE. AddMissingUses walks ClassesUsedInLines, which
               yields an entry per OCCURRENCE, so a class referenced four times in a .dd arrived
               here four times and was logged four times (Nils 2026-08-03 - "four times for each
               .dd file"). The reader wants the set of declined classes, not a concordance. */
            Move (ResizeArray(asSeen, 0)) to asSeen
            Move (SizeOfArray(asAmbiguous) - 1) to iMax
            For i from 0 to iMax
                If (SearchArray(asAmbiguous[i], asSeen) = -1) Begin
                    Move asAmbiguous[i] to asSeen[SizeOfArray(asSeen)]
                    Send AddRunNote "MakeFileAutonomous" (asAmbiguous[i] * "is defined in more than one package - left alone.")
                End
            Loop
            Move (ResizeArray(asSeen, 0)) to asSeen
            Move (SizeOfArray(asUnknown) - 1) to iMax
            For i from 0 to iMax
                If (SearchArray(asUnknown[i], asSeen) = -1) Begin
                    Move asUnknown[i] to asSeen[SizeOfArray(asSeen)]
                    Send AddRunNote "MakeFileAutonomous" ("no package found for" * asUnknown[i] * "- left alone.")
                End
            Loop
        End
        Function_Return iCount
    End_Function

    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Removes the space between the type and [] in an array variable declaration: "String [] asAddress" becomes "String[] asAddress".
        Example: String [] asAddress -> String[] asAddress
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Changed: NormalizeArrayNotation }
        """ }
    Function NormalizeArrayNotation String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        Boolean bChanged
        Get _NormalizeArrayNotation (&sLine) to bChanged
        Function_Return bChanged
    End_Function

    /* Bare shell globals -> Global_Variable (the globals companion to RefactorWorkspaceTocApplication,
       deliberately split out - Nils 2026-07-10). The wizard-era shells declare command-level
       variables with a bare 'String x' / 'Integer y' at FILE scope. On a modern target, every SHARED
       package that was hand-modernized to the g-naming convention (the login package reads
       gsP_auto_login@) then references a name the un-modernized program never declares - and the
       compiler's failure mode is baffling: it splits the unknown @-suffixed name AT the '@' and
       reports the stem as 4328 undefined PLUS error 52 '(operator) @', so one missing declaration
       reads like an expression-syntax bug (the identical expression compiles clean in a program that
       declares the g-name). The naming rule is mechanical and reproduces the reference shell's names
       exactly - see _GlobalShellVarName. NO {ModRecipe} on purpose: those errors pin to the SHARED
       package's read lines, not to the program file that needs the declaration fix (the same
       fix-site/error-site mismatch as the 4536-at-package backlog item) - run this via the Manual
       flow across the programs. A name another file of the run reads by its old name keeps it (2026-10-31:
       a whole-workspace run renamed shell globals that views, reports and shared packages still read). */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Changes global variables that are declared with a plain command in a program file to Global_Variable declarations with the modern g-prefix names: 'String P_auto_login@ arg001' becomes 'Global_Variable String gsP_auto_login@ gsArg001', and every use in the file is renamed too (text in strings and comments is never changed).
        A name that another file in the run uses without declaring it (a view, a report or a shared package) keeps its name, with a run note, because a new name would break that file.
        The prefixes are String gs, Integer gi, Number gn, Date gd and Boolean gb. An existing one-letter type prefix is replaced (sCmdString becomes gsCmdString), and old size values (String arg1 1) are kept.
        Only declarations at file level are changed. Local variables in procedures and functions, struct members, anything inside an object or class, existing Global_Variable lines, and a line that continues the line above it with ';' (such as a parameter of an External_Function on several lines) are not changed.
        { MethodType = eOtherFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Refactored: bare shell globals -> Global_Variable (g-named) }
        """ }
    Function RefactorBareGlobalsToGlobalVariable String[] ByRef asCode String sParameter Returns Integer
        Integer iRetval
        Get _TransformBareGlobals (&asCode) to iRetval
        Function_Return iRetval
    End_Function

    /* dbEdit -> cDbTextEdit findings (docs.dataflex.dev + the DF26 packages, Nils 2026-07-03). The
       user-facing half of this comment (the lineage caveat, the shared and differing property surfaces,
       and what a default run does to a user subclass) moved into the { Description } on 2026-08-23;
       what is left is the evidence behind it.

       The shared surface covers everything LegacyApp's ~190 edit objects actually set, which is what
       makes the swap safe there at all; the 'Class dbEdit0 is a dbEdit' shape in that same workspace is
       what the object-level parameter form exists for. The .prn-verified 4537 text is "Obsolete class:
       DBEDIT" (match-text "class: DBEDIT"; it does NOT substring-match "class: EDIT"). */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Converts dbEdit memo controls to cDbTextEdit. The class is changed on both 'Object ... is a dbEdit' and 'Class <sub> is a dbEdit' declarations, so the base class of a subclass is changed too.
        Wrap_State becomes pbWrap in the converted object. Legacy properties without a modern equivalent (the scroll bar settings and Capslock_State) get a TODO comment for you to decide.
        The function's parameter is 'OldEditClass,NewEditClass'. When it is empty, it is 'dbEdit,cDbTextEdit' plus the subclasses that the last Check found: their objects keep their class, whose base class is changed in its own file. 'dbEdit0,cDbTextEdit' converts the objects of that one subclass.
        This is not only a change of class name, because the two classes come from different base classes (dbEdit from Edit and DfBaseEdit, cDbTextEdit from cTextEdit and DFBaseTextEdit). Only the properties they share are kept unchanged: Entry_Item, Server, Size and Location, the Label properties, Enabled_State, Read_Only_State, Value, Line_Count, peAnchors, Status_Help and the DEO verify_*_msg properties.
        What differs: Wrap_State becomes pbWrap (not pbWordWrap), a text limit becomes piMaxChars, and the scroll bar settings and Capslock_State have no modern equivalent.
        { MethodType = eOtherFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4537 | class: DBEDIT }
        { EnumList = "ANY" }
        { InitialValue = dbEdit,cDbTextEdit }
        { ParamHelp = Two comma separated class names;
        \nMyDbEdit - dbEdit or a workspace subclass of it,
        \nMycDbTextEdit - cDbTextEdit or a workspace subclass of it }
        { SummaryText = Refactored: dbEdit -> cDbTextEdit }
        """ }
    Function RefactorDbEditTocDbTextEdit String[] ByRef asCode String sParameter Returns Integer
        Integer iRetval

        // Parameter parsing, the learned-subclass reach and the run notes live in _ConvertEditFamily.
        Get _ConvertEditFamily (&asCode) sParameter "dbEdit" "cDbTextEdit" "dbedit" "RefactorDbEditTocDbTextEdit" to iRetval
        Function_Return iRetval
    End_Function

    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Converts the obsolete multi-line Edit control to cTextEdit. The class is changed on both 'Object ... is a Edit' and 'Class <sub> is a Edit' declarations, so the base class of a subclass is changed too.
        Wrap_State becomes pbWrap in the converted object. Legacy properties without a modern equivalent (the scroll bar settings and Capslock_State) get a TODO comment.
        Only the whole word Edit is matched, so a dbEdit object is never changed by this function; use RefactorDbEditTocDbTextEdit for those.
        The function's parameter is 'OldEditClass,NewEditClass'. When it is empty, it is 'Edit,cTextEdit' plus the subclasses that the last Check found: their objects keep their class, whose base class is changed in its own file. 'MyEdit,cTextEdit' converts the objects of that one subclass.
        { MethodType = eOtherFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4537 | class: EDIT }
        { EnumList = "ANY" }
        { InitialValue = Edit,cTextEdit }
        { ParamHelp = Two comma separated class names;
        \nMyEdit - Edit or a workspace subclass of it,
        \nMycTextEdit - cTextEdit or a workspace subclass of it }
        { SummaryText = Refactored: Edit -> cTextEdit }
        """ }
    Function RefactorEditTocTextEdit String[] ByRef asCode String sParameter Returns Integer
        Integer iRetval

        // Parameter parsing, the learned-subclass reach and the run notes live in _ConvertEditFamily.
        Get _ConvertEditFamily (&asCode) sParameter "Edit" "cTextEdit" "edit" "RefactorEditTocTextEdit" to iRetval
        Function_Return iRetval
    End_Function

    /* WinReport -> cWinReport2 (WinPrint 1 -> WinPrint 2). WinReport is { Obsolete=True } in the obsolete
       package Dfwinrpt.pkg (docs.dataflex.dev "Converting a WinPrint Report" + the DF26 Pkg, verified
       2026-07-08); cWinReport2 lives in its own cWinReport2.pkg (which itself Uses WinPrint2.pkg). A near-pure
       class swap - WinReport and cWinReport2 share the BasicReport surface, so no property translation. The
       obsolete 'Use Dfwinrpt.pkg' (warning 4536) is retired and 'Use cWinReport2.pkg' guaranteed by
       _EnsureUsePackage - the same helper the Edit/dbEdit converters use. Whole-token match: an object NAME
       containing 'WinReport' is never touched; the shared report surface (Add_SubTotal, DFNew_Page,
       Output_Device_Mode, ...) passes through untouched. Subclass objects convert via the
       'OldReportClass,NewReportClass' parameter. NOTE: WinPrint 2
       additionally requires header commands (DFHeaderFrame, DFHeaderLineCheck, DFHeaderMargin, DFHeaderPos,
       DFHeaderWrap) to sit inside DFBeginHeader/DFEndHeader blocks - a separate runtime concern this pass does
       not address.
       RECIPES: the warning route is 4537 "Obsolete class: WinReport" (match "class: WINREPORT" - distinct
       from EDIT/DBEDIT). The ERROR routes (added 2026-07-10, the LegacyApp3 error-wall find): a workspace whose
       report views relied on the OLD Dfallrpt.pkg to pull WinPrint 1 in gets HARD ERRORS on a modern target,
       because modern Dfallrpt no longer includes it - 4345/4328 U_WINREPORT (class undefined), 4328
       WINPRINTID (the WinPrint-1 global; WinPrint 2's Winprint_Commands.pkg re-provides it as a compat
       Global_Variable that cWinReport2 fills), and a 4298 "undefined command" per report-command line
       (the DFWrite, DFFont, DFHeader, DFBeginHeader/DFEndHeader and margin families - all re-provided by
       Winprint_Commands.pkg, which cWinReport2.pkg Uses, so the class+Use swap clears every one of them;
       DFSetMetrics/DFSetMargins are procedures on the WinPrint2 engine and resolve too, verified against
       the DF25 Pkg). Match-texts are the locale-independent command/symbol tokens. Review (Suggest) until
       the real-workspace conversion is verified - promote to Auto-fix after. (No star-slash sequences in
       this comment: a DFWrite-star-slash shorthand TERMINATED the block early and compiled the tail.) */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Converts reports that use the obsolete WinPrint 1 class WinReport to the WinPrint 2 class cWinReport2. The class is changed on both 'Object ... is a WinReport' and 'Class <sub> is a WinReport' declarations; a subclass gets the new base class, and its objects keep the subclass name.
        The obsolete 'Use Dfwinrpt.pkg' is replaced with 'Use cWinReport2.pkg', which also provides the WinPrint report commands (DFWriteLn, DFFont, headers, margins) and the WinPrintId global variable. This also fixes the errors that a WinPrint 1 view gets in a modern DataFlex, where Dfallrpt.pkg no longer includes WinPrint 1.
        The function's parameter is 'OldReportClass,NewReportClass'; when it is empty, it is 'WinReport,cWinReport2'.
        { MethodType = eOtherFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4537 | class: WINREPORT }
        { ModRecipe = 4345 | U_WINREPORT | AutoFix }
        { ModRecipe = 4328 | U_WINREPORT | AutoFix }
        { ModRecipe = 4328 | WINPRINTID | AutoFix }
        { ModRecipe = 4298 | DFWRITE | AutoFix }
        { ModRecipe = 4298 | DFFONT | AutoFix }
        { ModRecipe = 4298 | DFHEADER | AutoFix }
        { ModRecipe = 4298 | DFBEGINHEADER | AutoFix }
        { ModRecipe = 4298 | DFENDHEADER | AutoFix }
        { ModRecipe = 4298 | DFLEFTMARGIN | AutoFix }
        { ModRecipe = 4298 | DFRIGHTMARGIN | AutoFix }
        { EnumList = "ANY" }
        { InitialValue = WinReport,cWinReport2 }
        { ParamHelp = Two comma separated class names;
        \nMyWinReport - WinReport or a workspace subclass of it,
        \nMycWinReport2 - cWinReport2 or a workspace subclass of it }
        { SummaryText = Refactored: WinReport -> cWinReport2 }
        """ }
    Function RefactorWinReportTocWinReport2 String[] ByRef asCode String sParameter Returns Integer
        Integer iRetval
        String sOldClass sNewClass
        String[] asParts

        Move "WinReport" to sOldClass
        Move "cWinReport2" to sNewClass
        // It returns OBJECTS converted, not lines - the summary line says so.
        Send SetSummaryUnit "RefactorWinReportTocWinReport2" "object(s)"
        If (Trim(sParameter) contains ",") Begin
            Move (StrSplitToArray(sParameter, ",")) to asParts
            If (SizeOfArray(asParts) >= 2) Begin
                If (Trim(asParts[0]) <> "") ;
                    Move (Trim(asParts[0])) to sOldClass
                If (Trim(asParts[1]) <> "") ;
                    Move (Trim(asParts[1])) to sNewClass
            End
        End
        Get _TransformWinReportToWinReport2 (&asCode) sOldClass sNewClass to iRetval
        Function_Return iRetval
    End_Function

    /* Legacy pre-Studio program shell: the Workspace object + the shell chrome around it.
       Workspc.pkg was REMOVED from modern DataFlex, so this shell no longer merely warns - it fails
       hard: 4313 (cannot include WORKSPC.PKG), 4345/4328 (U_WORKSPACE undefined), and every
       downstream ghoWorkspace read dies with it. The modern replacement is the cApplication object
       (reached via DfAllEnt.pkg, which these wizard-era shells already Use): it instantiates the
       workspace internally and re-provides ghoWorkspace, and psAutoOpenWorkspace names the runtime
       .ws config to open. Around the converted object, _ScaffoldModernShell (see it for the full
       step list) brings in the modern chrome: the CodeJock command-bar Use lines, an oHtmlHelp
       object (Std_Help.pkg is commented out) + peHelpType, commented psCompany/psProduct
       placeholders, an oToolTipController, an oCommandBarSystem inside the main panel, the
       AppClientArea -> ClientArea swap with the Client_Area object renamed to oClientArea (every
       whole-token in-file reference follows; Register_Object Client_Area is commented out), and a
       live Use StdAbout.pkg moved into the client area with an Activate_About skeleton added (the
       DoAbout example line stays commented for the user to fill in). The
       scaffolding runs only in a REAL program file (a live Use survives) and every step is
       presence-guarded, so re-runs and half-modernized files never duplicate chrome. STILL out of
       scope (separate future functions, Nils 2026-07-10): bare command-level globals, the login
       block, .mn menus. A 'Class X is a Workspace' subclass is NOT converted (subclassing
       cApplication is a design decision, not a swap). The recipes below are ERROR-shaped (the first
       in the library): this construct produces no warning to hook - the compile errors ARE the
       signal, and the match-texts key on the locale-independent symbol/file tokens (the surrounding
       message text is localized on non-English compilers). */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Converts the legacy Workspace object (from Workspc.pkg, which modern DataFlex no longer includes) to a cApplication object, and adds the modern program structure around it.
        'Object x is a Workspace' becomes 'Object x is a cApplication' (renamed to oApplication when nothing refers to it), and 'Set WorkspaceName' becomes 'Set psAutoOpenWorkspace to "<parameter>"'. Workspace properties without an equivalent are commented out with a TODO comment.
        The obsolete Use Workspc.pkg, Use Std_Help.pkg, #REPLACE and Register_Object lines are commented out. The CodeJock menu bar packages, and the oHtmlHelp, peHelpType, oToolTipController and oCommandBarSystem objects, are added.
        The AppClientArea client area becomes a ClientArea named oClientArea, and Use StdAbout.pkg is moved inside it, with an Activate_About procedure added.
        { MethodType = eOtherFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4313 | WORKSPC | Suggest }
        { ModRecipe = 4345 | U_WORKSPACE | Suggest }
        { ModRecipe = 4328 | U_WORKSPACE | Suggest }
        { EnumList = "ANY" }
        { InitialValue = Config.ws }
        { ParamHelp = The workspace config file for psAutoOpenWorkspace;
        \nConfig.ws - the DataFlex default,
        \nMyApp.ws - a named workspace config file }
        { SummaryText = Refactored: Workspace -> cApplication }
        """ }
    Function RefactorWorkspaceTocApplication String[] ByRef asCode String sParameter Returns Integer
        Integer iRetval
        Get _TransformWorkspaceToApplication (&asCode) (Trim(sParameter)) to iRetval
        Function_Return iRetval
    End_Function

    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Removes the 'Local' keyword from variable declarations in procedures and functions.
        Example: Local Integer iRetval iOk -> Integer iRetval iOk
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: Local }
        { SummaryText = Removed: 'Local' key word }
        """ }
    Function RemoveLocalKeyWord String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        String sText
        tTokenizer TokenizerData

        Get pTokenizer to TokenizerData
        If (TokenizerData.bIsVariableDeclaration = False) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End

        Get _RemoveLocalKeyWord sLine to sText
        If (Length(sText) = Length(sLine)) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End
        Move sText to sLine
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    /* RemovePropertyPrivate - WAS a line-by-line eStandardFunction that blindly stripped 'Private' off every
       'Property ... Private'. That CORRUPTS the private-backing-store idiom: a 'Property <type> <name> Private'
       paired with a same-named custom 'Procedure Set <name>' / 'Function <name>' MUST keep Private - making the
       property public collides the compiler's auto accessor with the custom one, the property fails to register,
       and every (qualified) access of it goes undefined (4328). LegacyApp hit this on APS_PANEL_MX.PMINIMUMSIZE,
       APS_CONTAINER_MX.P_AUTO_COLUMN, CDDRecordSet.Current_Record. Telling a backing store from an ordinary
       Private property needs WHOLE-FILE context (the accessor can sit anywhere relative to the property), which a
       line-by-line pass cannot see - so this is now an eOtherFunction with a 2-pass scan: collect the custom
       accessor names first, then strip Private only from Property lines whose name is NOT one of them. Un-scoped
       on purpose - protecting a same-named property in an unrelated class is harmless (it just keeps a Private it
       could have shed); stripping one it should not have is what breaks the build. */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Removes the 'Private' keyword from Property declarations.
        It is kept on a property that stores the value for a custom 'Procedure Set' or 'Function' with the same name, because there Private is needed to prevent a conflict with the custom method. The whole file is checked for such methods first.
        Example: Property String pSel1 Private "" -> Property String pSel1 "" (the Private keyword goes).
        { MethodType = eOtherFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4535 | Private or Public keyword }
        { SummaryText = Removed: 'Private' property key word }
        """ }
    Function RemovePropertyPrivate String[] ByRef asCode String sParameter Returns Integer
        String[] asProtected asCodeOnly
        String sLine sName sLC
        Integer iCount iSize iChanges
        Boolean bChanged bProtected
        tTokenizer TD

        Move (SizeOfArray(asCode) - 1) to iSize
        If (iSize < 0) ;
            Function_Return 0

        // PASS 1: collect the protected backing-store / mixin accessor names. Shared with the analyzer's
        // WouldRemovePropertyChange fast path (via _CollectPropertyProtectedNames) so the two never drift -
        // see that helper for the STATELESS-parsing rationale (the Tokenizer mis-tracked cross-line scope, so
        // a Property after a Procedure/Function was silently never seen - LegacyApp's broken backing stores).
        Get _CollectPropertyProtectedNames (&asCode) to asProtected

        // PASS 2: strip 'Private' from each Property declaration whose name is NOT a protected accessor. The
        // strip (RemovePropertyPublicPrivate -> ReplaceLineToken) needs a Tokenizer for the line, so each
        // Property line is tokenised FRESH after a reset - a single declaration line tokenises correctly in
        // isolation, sidestepping the cross-line scope corruption that defeated the whole-file passes above.
        // A line is judged on its CODE (2026-10-29): a multi-line string's text, a #HEADER region and macro code
        // are blank in _CodeOnlyFileLines, so a 'Property ... Private' there is never changed.
        Get _CodeOnlyFileLines (&asCode) to asCodeOnly
        Move 0 to iChanges
        For iCount from 0 to iSize
            // Per-file progress nudge; no-op when no panel is active (mirrors DefineSelectMacro).
            If (Mod(iCount, 20) = 0 and (Active_State(ghoStatusPanel) = True)) Begin
                Send Update_StatusPanel of ghoStatusPanel ("Source line:" * String(iCount + 1) * "of:" * String(iSize + 1))
                Set piPosition of ghoProgressBar to (iCount + 1)
            End
            Move (Lowercase(Trim(asCodeOnly[iCount]))) to sLC
            If (Left(sLC, 2) <> "//" and Pos("property ", sLC) = 1) Begin
                Get _NthWord asCode[iCount] 3 to sName
                Get _NameInList sName asProtected to bProtected
                If (sName <> "" and bProtected = False) Begin
                    Send ClearFuncLibProperties
                    Move asCode[iCount] to sLine
                    Get Tokenizer sLine to TD
                    Get RemovePropertyPublicPrivate (&sLine) "private" to bChanged
                    If (bChanged = True) Begin
                        Move sLine to asCode[iCount]
                        Increment iChanges
                    End
                End
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return iChanges
    End_Function

    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Removes the 'Public' keyword from Property declarations.
        Example: Property String pSel1 Public "" -> Property String pSel1 "" (the Public keyword goes).
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ModRecipe = 4535 | Private or Public keyword }
        { SummaryText = Removed: 'Public' property key word }
        """ }
    Function RemovePropertyPublic String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        Boolean bChanged
        Get RemovePropertyPublicPrivate (&sLine) "public" to bChanged
        Function_Return bChanged
    End_Function

    /* RemoveResidentImageOption - a legacy report image (a "page") opens with its name line, such as
       '/Body', and character-mode DataFlex took options on that line: 'Resident' kept the image in
       memory, so a report did not read it from disk for every line it printed. The DataFlex 26
       help lists the option as obsolete, for character mode and BasicReport only, and a DataFlex
       26 report whose image still carries it crashes, or prints output that is no use (Nils
       2026-10-03). The word never changed what an image prints, so removing it is safe: '/Body
       Resident' becomes '/Body'.

       Every other function leaves the image name line alone - the tokenizer marks it, like the
       image's text, as no code to change - so this one reads the raw line: IsImageStart, then the
       word after the name (_ImageNameOptionPos, which Deep Scan's DS 9233 reads too, so the scan
       and the fix agree on the line). A #HEADER region stays as written. A word that blanks do
       not separate is left alone - _ImageNameOptionPos says why. */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Removes the obsolete 'Resident' option from the name line of a legacy report image. In DataFlex 26, a report can crash because of it, or print wrong output. The image itself is not changed.
        Example: /Body Resident -> /Body
        { MethodType = eStandardFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { SummaryText = Removed: 'Resident' image option }
        """ }
    Function RemoveResidentImageOption String ByRef sLine String sParameter Returns Boolean
        String sText
        Boolean bInHeader

        Get pbInHeader to bInHeader
        If (bInHeader = True) ;
            Function_Return False
        Get _RemoveImageNameOption sLine "resident" to sText
        If (Length(sText) = Length(sLine)) ;
            Function_Return False
        Move sText to sLine
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    /* RenameCollidingGlobalFunction - the RENAME half of the 4390 story, for names a guard cannot
       help: the newer DataFlex owns the name from its RUNTIME or a CLASS METHOD (Split is
       regex-flavoured in the runtime; SQLExecute is sql.pkg's statement procedure), so the
       workspace's own global must stop using the name. The declaration rename is one line; the CALL
       SITES are the pain - Nils hand-renamed SPLIT across 164 instances (2026-07-23) before this
       converter existed.

       WHAT RENAMES: the Global declaration (Function <Old> ... Global / Register_Function <Old>),
       expression calls (<Old> followed by "(", spaces allowed), and OF-LESS Get statements
       (Get <Old> args to var). WHAT NEVER RENAMES: Get/Send <Old> OF hObject - the of-form is
       OBJECT-targeted, which is a class method and usually exactly the native API the name clashes
       with (Send SQLExecute of hStmt must survive the rename untouched). That discriminator is what
       makes the rename safe to automate. Masked matching (literals/comments never touched);
       whole-word boundaries make it idempotent (SplitX never re-matches Split).

       Run it over the WHOLE selected scope - call sites live everywhere, not just the declaring
       file. A half-migrated workspace UNIFIES: sites already on the new name are simply not
       matched. Suggest-tier by design: renames on customer code are opt-in, never auto-fix. */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Renames a global function that has the same name as a function in the newer DataFlex (error 4390, where a version check cannot help).
        The declaration, calls in expressions, and Get statements without 'of' are renamed. Get and Send ... OF <object> are never changed, because those call the class method that has the same name.
        Set the parameter to OldName|NewName, or only OldName to add an X to the name. Run it on all selected files, because the calls can be in any file.
        { MethodType = eOtherFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ParamHelp = OldName|NewName, or OldName alone to rename to OldNameX (SPLIT -> SPLITX). The name of the global function to rename away from a DataFlex-owned name. }
        { SummaryText = Renamed a colliding global function and its call sites }
        """ }
    Function RenameCollidingGlobalFunction String[] ByRef asCode String sParameter Returns Integer
        String sOld sNew
        Integer iPos iRen
        Boolean bGate

        Move (Trim(sParameter)) to sOld
        If (sOld = "") Begin
            // Configuration state, not a fault - logged and skipped, per the policy set on
            // AlignCollidingDeclarationType (Nils 2026-08-03). This one returned 0 in SILENCE, so a
            // Select All that swept it in produced no change and no reason anywhere.
            If (ghoStatusLog <> 0) ;
                Send AddRunNote "RenameCollidingGlobalFunction" "SKIPPED - needs a parameter of the form OldName|NewName, or OldName alone to rename to OldNameX, for example SPLIT|SPLITX."
            Function_Return 0
        End
        Move (Pos("|", sOld)) to iPos
        If (iPos > 1) Begin
            Move (Trim(Right(sOld, (Length(sOld) - iPos)))) to sNew
            Move (Trim(Left(sOld, (iPos - 1))))             to sOld
        End
        Else ;
            Move (sOld + "X") to sNew
        If (sOld = "" or sNew = "") ;
            Function_Return 0
        // Cheap raw-text gate: no occurrence of the old name = nothing this file can contribute.
        Get _FileContainsText (&asCode) (Lowercase(sOld)) to bGate
        If (bGate = False) ;
            Function_Return 0
        Get _RenameGlobalNameInLines (&asCode) sOld sNew to iRen
        Set piRenamedGlobalCount to (piRenamedGlobalCount(Self) + iRen)
        Function_Return iRen
    End_Function

    /* RenameSameNamedMethod - the THIRD 4544 fix (Nils 2026-09-04, a workspace's same-named function). Two unrelated
       methods share a name: Unit.sl's fCount returns a description ("Fixed amount"), RETBACK's and
       PRETBACK's return a count. The align offer retyped the text one to Integer - which compiles,
       and returns 0. Neither existing route fits that case: ChangeCollidingMethodName walks CLASS
       families and these are object-level declarations; RenameCollidingGlobalFunction refuses a
       keyword-less Function on purpose. This is that renamer with the method declaration allowed
       (pbRenameMethodDecl, a one-shot), meant to run over the ODD declaration's own file - the
       guided board scopes it so - so the other declarations keep their name. The declaration,
       expression calls and of-less Gets rename; of-forms never do. Never a {ModRecipe}: which
       declaration is the odd one is a harvest-time fact (FunctionReturnsTextIn), not a line shape. */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Renames a method that has the same name as an unrelated method with a different return type (error 4544), so that it gets a name of its own. Use this when giving them the same type would change what one of them returns (a function that returns text, changed to Integer, returns 0).
        The function's parameter is OldName|NewName, or only OldName to add Text to the name.
        The Function declaration (global or not), calls in expressions, and Get statements without 'of' are renamed in each file it runs on. Get and Send ... OF <object> are never changed.
        Run it only on the file with the declaration that differs, as the guided Check does, so that the other declarations keep their names.
        { MethodType = eOtherFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ParamHelp = OldName|NewName, or OldName alone to rename to OldNameText (fCount -> fCountText). Run it over the file that declares the odd one out. }
        { SummaryText = Renamed a same-named method and its calls in its own file }
        """ }
    Function RenameSameNamedMethod String[] ByRef asCode String sParameter Returns Integer
        String sOld sNew
        Integer iPos iRen
        Boolean bGate

        Move (Trim(sParameter)) to sOld
        If (sOld = "") Begin
            If (ghoStatusLog <> 0) ;
                Send AddRunNote "RenameSameNamedMethod" "SKIPPED - needs a parameter of the form OldName|NewName, or OldName alone to rename to OldNameText, for example fCount|fCountText."
            Function_Return 0
        End
        Move (Pos("|", sOld)) to iPos
        If (iPos > 1) Begin
            Move (Trim(Right(sOld, (Length(sOld) - iPos)))) to sNew
            Move (Trim(Left(sOld, (iPos - 1))))             to sOld
        End
        Else ;
            Move (sOld + "Text") to sNew
        If (sOld = "" or sNew = "") ;
            Function_Return 0
        Get _FileContainsText (&asCode) (Lowercase(sOld)) to bGate
        If (bGate = False) ;
            Function_Return 0
        Set pbRenameMethodDecl to True
        Get _RenameGlobalNameInLines (&asCode) sOld sNew to iRen
        Set pbRenameMethodDecl to False
        Set piRenamedMethodCount to (piRenamedMethodCount(Self) + iRen)
        Function_Return iRen
    End_Function

    /* RenameCollidingClass - the CLASS flavour of the duplicate-name error 4345 ("Type check error
       |CI<n>, SHOULD NOT BE DEFINED" on a "Class <Name> is a ..." line): the newer DataFlex has a
       class of that name - cRegEx moved into the DF 25/26 runtime, so no Pkg file declares it and
       no catalogue can see it; the compiler is the oracle. The workspace's own class must stop
       using the name, and its references live everywhere: Object declarations ("is a"), subclasses,
       RefClass() creates and "for <Class>" augmentations. Renames exactly those four shapes and
       nothing else that spells the word (see _RenameClassNameInLines). Masked, whole-word,
       idempotent. Suggest-tier by design: renames on customer code are opt-in. (Nils 2026-09-03:
       "it is vital that a class name clash gets a recipe and one with high priority".) */
    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
        Renames a class that has the same name as a class that the newer DataFlex defines itself (error 4345, "should not be defined", on its Class line).
        The Class declaration is renamed, and so is every "is a <Class>" (objects and subclasses), RefClass(<Class>) and "for <Class>" in the file. Nothing else that contains the word is changed.
        Run it on all selected files, because references can be in any file. Set the parameter to OldName|NewName, or only OldName to add an X to the name.
        { MethodType = eOtherFunction }
        { FnGroup = Modernization }
        { ParamHelp = OldName|NewName, or OldName alone to rename to OldNameX (cRegEx -> cRegExX). The class to rename away from a DataFlex-owned name. }
        { SummaryText = Renamed a colliding class and its references }
        """ }
    Function RenameCollidingClass String[] ByRef asCode String sParameter Returns Integer
        String sOld sNew
        Integer iPos iRen
        Boolean bGate

        Move (Trim(sParameter)) to sOld
        If (sOld = "") Begin
            Send AddRunNote "RenameCollidingClass" "SKIPPED - needs a parameter of the form OldName|NewName, or OldName alone to rename to OldNameX."
            Function_Return 0
        End
        Move (Pos("|", sOld)) to iPos
        If (iPos > 0) Begin
            Move (Trim(Right(sOld, (Length(sOld) - iPos)))) to sNew
            Move (Trim(Left(sOld, (iPos - 1))))             to sOld
        End
        Else ;
            Move (sOld + "X") to sNew
        If (sOld = "" or sNew = "") ;
            Function_Return 0
        Get _FileContainsText (&asCode) (Lowercase(sOld)) to bGate
        If (bGate = False) ;
            Function_Return 0
        Get _RenameClassNameInLines (&asCode) sOld sNew to iRen
        Set piRenamedClassCount to (piRenamedClassCount(Self) + iRen)
        Function_Return iRen
    End_Function

    { Published = True }
    { Description = """
      Changes the names of data dictionary objects to one consistent style.
      The naming style of data dictionary objects has changed over time. The current style is:
      Object o<tablename>_DD is a c<TableName>Datadictionary
      There is also a setting to keep the older style:
      Object o<tablename>_DD is a <tablename>_Datadictionary
      Before VDF 12, the names looked like this:
      Object <tableName>_DD is a <tablename>_Datadictionary
      Without the extra 'o', object names clashed with other names in many places. Code copied from old views into new views and dialogs then stops working, and when a workspace mixes styles, the compiler does not report it.
      Examples:
      <TableName>_DD - o<TableName>DD (eDDStudioStyle)
      <TableName>_DD - o<TableName>_dd (neDDNewStyle)

      { MethodType = eOtherFunctionAll }
      { FnGroup = Modernization }
      { EnumList = "eDDStudioStyle, eDDNewStyle, eDDOldStyle" }
      { InitialValue = eDDNewStyle }
      { ParamHelp = Valid values;
      \neDDStudioStyle - o<TableName>DD
      \neDDNewStyle - o<TableName>_dd
      \neDDOldStyle - <TableName>_DD }
      { SummaryText = Renamed DataDictionary objects }
      """ }
    Function RestyleDDOs String[] ByRef asFiles String sParameter Returns Integer
        String sHomePath
        Handle ho
        Boolean bIsStyle
        Integer iRetval iSize iCount eDDOStyle
        tRefactorSettings RefactorSettings
        tWorkspacePaths TargetWS OrgWS

        If (SizeOfArray(asFiles) <= 0) Begin
            Function_Return False
        End

        Get Create (RefClass(cDDOReStyler)) to ho
        If (ho = 0) Begin
            Error "Could not create cDDORestyler object (Function: RestyleDDOs)"
            Function_Return 0
        End

        // sParameter arrives as the enum NAME from the UI combo or as the numeric VALUE
        // from tests - resolve each member explicitly via _ParameterIsEnum (a bare Move
        // of the name into the Integer enum yields eDDUnknown; and NOTE the {EnumList}
        // display order differs from the Enum_List value order here, so positional
        // mapping would be wrong). Unmatched/blank keeps eDDUnknown, as before. NO
        // num_arguments guard - it is unreliable after nested sends/dynamic dispatch,
        // and an omitted parameter arrives as "" (no member matches).
        Get _ParameterIsEnum sParameter "eDDStudioStyle" eDDStudioStyle to bIsStyle
        If (bIsStyle = True) ;
            Move eDDStudioStyle to eDDOStyle
        Get _ParameterIsEnum sParameter "eDDNewStyle" eDDNewStyle to bIsStyle
        If (bIsStyle = True) ;
            Move eDDNewStyle to eDDOStyle
        Get _ParameterIsEnum sParameter "eDDOldStyle" eDDOldStyle to bIsStyle
        If (bIsStyle = True) ;
            Move eDDOldStyle to eDDOStyle

        // Path for the workspace we are refactoring: target if selected,
        // otherwise DFRefactor's own (defensive - RestyleDDOs is normally
        // invoked with a target workspace selected).
        Get pTargetWS of ghoApplication to TargetWS
        If (TargetWS.sHome <> "") Begin
            Move TargetWS.sHome to sHomePath
        End
        Else Begin
            Get pOrgWS of ghoApplication to OrgWS
            Move OrgWS.sHome to sHomePath
        End
        Set psHomeTarget  of ho to sHomePath
        Set peDDOStyle  of ho to eDDOStyle
        Set pasAllFiles of ho to asFiles
        Set pbStrict    of ho to False

        // StartDDORestyler briefly redirects the active filelist to the target
        // workspace for the one-time table enumeration only, then restores it
        // before the per-file processing - so DFRefactor's own error logging
        // stays intact even if the restyle raises errors.
        Get StartDDORestyler of ho to iRetval
        Send Destroy of ho

        Function_Return iRetval
    End_Function

    /* UnicodeBufferPairToString collapses the DF 19.1 two-line buffer-conversion idiom

           Move (OemToUtf8Buffer((AddressOf(sVar)),(Length(sVar)))) to aBuf
           Move aBuf to sVar

       into the single DF 20+ line that does the same work

           Move (OemToUtf8(sVar)) to sVar

       for all four CharTranslate pairs. Nils asked for it 2026-07-29, having been doing
       it by hand.

       It is the shape UnicodePointerToString deliberately declines - "a two-argument
       call is the pointer-level API used deliberately" - and that judgement is right
       for a two-argument call ON ITS OWN. The SECOND line is what changes the answer:
       moving the buffer straight back into the same variable proves the pointer was
       only ever a way to get a string back again.

       Kept as its OWN function rather than folded into UnicodePointerToString because
       that one promises to WRAP an operand, while this DELETES a line. A transform that
       removes source deserves its own opt-in, its own summary and its own risk.

       It also removes a memory leak: OemToUtf8Buffer & co return a heap buffer the
       caller must free ("use free() to deallocate", CharTranslate.pkg) and this idiom
       never does, while the String siblings free internally.

       Guards (see the class): both arguments name the SAME variable, the next code line
       hands the buffer back to it, the buffer is used nowhere else, the variable is a
       declared String, and neither line carries a trailing comment. The buffer's own
       declaration is left behind - removing declarations is RemoveUnusedLocals's job.
    */

    /* ----- Unicode paradigm family -----
       Refactors that bring pre-Unicode (DataFlex < 2022) source up to the
       Unicode compiler's stricter rules. Logic lives in dedicated classes; see
       cUnicodePointerToString.pkg.

       UnicodePointerToString fixes compile error 4539. The user-facing half of
       this comment - the error text, the before/after pairs and the rules that
       decide when a line qualifies - moved into the { Description } on
       2026-08-23, where the generated help can reach it.

       Why it is deliberately conservative: a missed line is harmless, a wrong
       wrap is not, so nothing is rewritten unless BOTH operand types are known
       from the local declarations or the method signature.

       2026-07-23 (LegacyApp mining): the same 4539 also fires on the DF 19.1
       runtime buffer-conversion idiom, where the pointer hides inside the
       source expression. For the four CharTranslate buffer functions the fix is
       the String-returning sibling - the identical conversion with the pointer
       gone. The two-argument form (AddressOf + length) is the pointer-level API
       used deliberately, so it is left for a hand migration.
    */
    
    /* THE BROKEN-OUT FAMILIES GO IN LAST, and that is not cosmetic. They reference helpers
       defined ABOVE in this file, and a RefFunc / function-style reference resolves at COMPILE
       time - DefineSelectMacro does RefFunc(_HasBareSelectKeyword), which lives up there, and
       with the #Include at the top of the object it was Error 54. Nothing in this file
       references these functions at compile time, so the other direction is free.

       AND THEY MUST STAY ABOVE THE NOTE BELOW. The JSON import locates End_Object, checks
       that the line at [End_Object - 2] is that note, truncates the array at [End_Object - 3]
       and re-appends the note and End_Object itself. Anything BELOW the note is outside the
       region it keeps, so four #Include lines put there would be dropped on the next import -
       silently taking 42 published functions out of the build. (Nils caught this, 2026-09-01.)

       The blank line between the last #Include and the note is the truncation point. Keep it. */
    #Include CodeJockRefactorFunctions.inc
    #Include CleanupRefactorFunctions.inc
    #Include CommentOutRefactorFunctions.inc
    #Include CommandToFunctionRefactorFunctions.inc
    #Include IndicatorRefactorFunctions.inc
    #Include Unicode64RefactorFunctions.inc

// NOTE: Do *not* add any code after this "End_Object"!
//       It will be removed by the JSON Export/Import logic.
End_Object
